window.QF=[{"id":"accounts","name":"Solicitors Accounts","topics":[{"id":"accounts/framework-client-money","name":"Solicitors Accounts 1: The Accounts Rules and client money","qs":[{"q":"Money received on account of costs before any bill is delivered is classed as what?","o":["Client money","Firm money","Office money held on trust","Mixed money"],"e":"**r.2.1(d)**: money for fees and unpaid disbursements held **before delivery of a bill** is **client money**."},{"q":"Who in a firm must report serious breaches of the Accounts Rules to the SRA?","o":["The COFA","The COLP","The MLRO","The senior partner"],"e":"The **COFA** is responsible for Accounts Rules compliance and reporting **serious breaches** promptly."},{"q":"What is the maximum residual balance per client matter that may go to charity without SRA authorisation?","o":["£500","£50","£1,000","£250"],"e":"Balances of **£500 or less** may be paid to charity under **r.5.1(c)** after reasonable steps to trace the owner; above that needs **SRA authorisation**."},{"q":"Which rule allows Legal Aid Agency payments for your costs to bypass client account?","o":["Rule 2.3","Rule 2.2","Rule 4.1","Rule 5.1"],"e":"**r.2.3(b)**: an LAA payment for **your costs** need not be paid into client account."}]},{"id":"accounts/client-account-withdrawals","name":"Solicitors Accounts 2: Client account and withdrawals","qs":[{"q":"Where must a client account be held?","o":["A bank or building society branch in England and Wales","Any UK-regulated bank","Any FCA-authorised payment provider","A bank anywhere in the UK or EU"],"e":"**r.3.1**: at a branch or head office of a **bank or building society in England and Wales**."},{"q":"Which word must appear in the name of a client account?","o":["Client","Trust","Designated","Escrow"],"e":"**r.3.2**: the name must include the firm's name and the word **'client'**."},{"q":"Which rule prohibits using a client account to provide banking facilities?","o":["Rule 3.3","Rule 5.3","Rule 4.2","Rule 2.4"],"e":"**r.3.3**: no banking facilities; movements must relate to **regulated services** delivered by you."},{"q":"Under rule 5.3, when may client money be withdrawn for a client?","o":["Only if sufficient funds are held for that client","If the general client account is in credit","If replaced within 14 days","If the client has orally agreed"],"e":"**r.5.3**: only if **sufficient funds are held for that specific client**; otherwise other clients' money is used."}]},{"id":"accounts/separation-mixed-payments","name":"Solicitors Accounts 3: Keeping client and firm money separate","qs":[{"q":"What must a firm do with a mixed payment under rule 4.2?","o":["Allocate it promptly to the correct accounts","Pay it all into business account","Transfer firm money within 14 days exactly","Hold it all in client account until matter ends"],"e":"**r.4.2**: allocate **promptly** to client and business accounts. Usually pay it into client account and promptly transfer the firm's share."},{"q":"A client reimburses a search fee the firm already paid from business account. Where does the money go?","o":["Business account","Client account","Separate designated client account","Suspense account"],"e":"Reimbursement of a disbursement **already paid** by the firm is **firm money**, so it goes to the **business account**."},{"q":"Which rule requires client money to be kept separate from firm money?","o":["Rule 4.1","Rule 2.1","Rule 6.1","Rule 8.1"],"e":"**r.4.1**: keep client money separate from the authorised body's money."},{"q":"What must be given to the client before client money is transferred to pay the firm's costs?","o":["A bill or written notification of costs","An oral estimate","A client care letter only","A cash statement"],"e":"**r.4.3**: a **bill of costs or other written notification** must be given **before** the transfer, for the specific sum."}]},{"id":"accounts/double-entry-basics","name":"Solicitors Accounts 4: Double-entry bookkeeping: the basics","qs":[{"q":"What entries record receipt of client money?","o":["DR client cash; CR client ledger client column","CR client cash; DR client ledger client column","DR business cash; CR client ledger business column","DR client ledger client column; CR business cash"],"e":"Money in: **DR cash account**, **CR ledger** (client columns for client money)."},{"q":"What does a debit balance on a client ledger client column indicate?","o":["The ledger is overdrawn: a breach","Money is held for the client","The client owes the firm costs","Interest is due to the client"],"e":"A **DR balance on the client column** means more has been paid out than held: an **overdrawn** ledger and a breach."},{"q":"How is an inter-client transfer recorded?","o":["DR transferor ledger; CR transferee ledger, no cash entry","DR client cash; CR transferee ledger","CR client cash; DR transferor ledger","DR business cash; CR transferee ledger"],"e":"Money stays in client account, so only the two **ledgers** are posted: DR transferor, CR transferee. **No cash entry**."},{"q":"What entries record a payment from business account on the client's behalf?","o":["DR ledger business column; CR business cash","DR ledger client column; CR client cash","DR business cash; CR ledger business column","DR ledger business column; CR client cash"],"e":"Payment out of business account: **DR client ledger business column**, **CR business cash**."}]},{"id":"accounts/bills-vat-disbursements","name":"Solicitors Accounts 5: Bills, VAT and disbursements","qs":[{"q":"Which accounts are credited when a bill of profit costs plus VAT is delivered?","o":["Profit costs and HMRC (VAT)","Client cash and business cash","Client ledger client column only","Business cash and HMRC"],"e":"On delivery: DR client ledger business column (gross); **CR profit costs** and **CR HMRC (VAT)**. No cash moves."},{"q":"Which VAT method applies to a disbursement invoice addressed to the firm?","o":["Principal method","Agency method","Exempt method","Zero-rated method"],"e":"Invoice addressed to the **firm**: **principal method**. The firm reclaims input VAT and charges output VAT on its bill."},{"q":"Does VAT apply to Land Registry fees or SDLT?","o":["No: not a VAT supply","Yes: at the standard rate","Yes: at a reduced rate","Only under the principal method"],"e":"**SDLT, Land Registry and court/probate fees** are not VAT supplies; they are treated under the **agency method** with no VAT."},{"q":"What entries record an abatement of a delivered bill?","o":["DR profit costs and HMRC; CR ledger business column","CR profit costs and HMRC; DR ledger business column","DR bad debts; CR ledger business column","DR ledger client column; CR client cash"],"e":"An **abatement** reverses the bill entries: **DR profit costs**, **DR HMRC**, **CR client ledger business column**, with a VAT credit note."}]},{"id":"accounts/interest-breaches","name":"Solicitors Accounts 6: Interest and correcting breaches","qs":[{"q":"What does rule 7.1 require on client money held?","o":["Account for a fair sum of interest","Pay interest at base rate","Pay all interest earned","Pay interest only above £20"],"e":"**r.7.1**: account for a **fair sum of interest**. No minimum rate or sum is set."},{"q":"How can a firm contract out of paying interest on client money?","o":["Written agreement with informed consent","Oral agreement with the client","Notice on the firm's website","It cannot contract out"],"e":"**r.7.2**: by **written agreement** with sufficient information for **informed consent**."},{"q":"When must breaches of the Accounts Rules be corrected?","o":["Promptly upon discovery","Within 14 days","At the next reconciliation","When the client repays"],"e":"**r.6.1**: correct breaches **promptly upon discovery**; improperly withdrawn money must be **immediately** replaced."},{"q":"How is a dishonoured client cheque recorded?","o":["DR client ledger client column; CR client cash","DR client cash; CR client ledger client column","DR business cash; CR client cash","DR bad debts; CR client cash"],"e":"Reverse the receipt: **DR client ledger client column**, **CR client cash**. Any resulting overdraft must be replaced from business account."}]},{"id":"accounts/records-reconciliation","name":"Solicitors Accounts 7: Records, reconciliation and retention","qs":[{"q":"How often must client account reconciliations be carried out?","o":["At least every five weeks","At least monthly","At least every six weeks","At least quarterly"],"e":"**r.8.3**: at least **every five weeks**, a three-way reconciliation."},{"q":"Who must sign off a client account reconciliation?","o":["The COFA or a manager","The cashier","The COLP only","The firm's accountant"],"e":"**r.8.3**: signed off by the **COFA or a manager**, not just the person who prepared it."},{"q":"How long must accounting records be retained?","o":["At least six years","At least three years","At least twelve years","At least six months"],"e":"**r.13**: store securely for **at least six years**."},{"q":"Which three figures are compared in a client account reconciliation?","o":["Bank statement, cash book and client ledger total","Bank statement, business cash and profit costs","Cash book, HMRC account and ledgers","Client ledger, business ledger and bills record"],"e":"A **three-way reconciliation**: adjusted **bank statement**, **cash book** and **client ledger total**."}]},{"id":"accounts/joint-own-tpma","name":"Solicitors Accounts 8: Joint accounts, clients' own accounts and TPMAs","qs":[{"q":"Which Accounts Rules apply to a joint account with another law firm?","o":["Statements and bills record only","All of Part 2","Statements and reconciliation only","None at all"],"e":"**r.9**: only **r.8.2** (statements) and **r.8.4** (bills record) apply."},{"q":"Which rule covers a solicitor operating a client's own account as attorney?","o":["Rule 10","Rule 9","Rule 11","Rule 12"],"e":"**r.10**: operating a client's own account as signatory. Statements, reconciliation and bills record apply."},{"q":"What is a key condition for using a third party managed account?","o":["The firm does not receive or hold client money","The firm holds the money in client account","The client pays no TPMA fees","The account is reconciled weekly"],"e":"**r.11**: the TPMA must not result in the firm **receiving or holding** client money, and the client must be informed of terms and fees."}]},{"id":"accounts/accountants-reports","name":"Solicitors Accounts 9: Accountants' reports","qs":[{"q":"Within what time after the accounting period must an accountant's report be obtained?","o":["Six months","Three months","Twelve months","Five weeks"],"e":"**r.12.1**: obtain the report **within six months** of the end of the accounting period."},{"q":"Which accountant's reports must be delivered to the SRA?","o":["Qualified reports showing money at risk","All accountant's reports","All qualified reports","Only reports for firms over £250,000"],"e":"Only a report **qualified** to show **money at risk** is delivered; others are kept by the firm."},{"q":"What are the balance limits for the low-balance accountant's report exemption?","o":["Average £10,000 and maximum £250,000","Average £25,000 and maximum £250,000","Average £10,000 or maximum £250,000","Average £5,000 and maximum £100,000"],"e":"**r.12.2(b)**: **both** an average not over **£10,000** and a maximum not over **£250,000**."},{"q":"What qualification must the reporting accountant hold?","o":["Chartered accountancy member and registered auditor","Any qualified bookkeeper","An SRA-approved accountant","A member of the Law Society"],"e":"**r.12.5**: member of a **chartered accountancy body** and a **registered auditor** (or working for one)."}]},{"id":"accounts/conveyancing-accounts","name":"Solicitors Accounts 10: Accounts in conveyancing (property context)","qs":[{"q":"In whose favour is a deposit held by a stakeholder?","o":["Both buyer and seller","The seller only","The buyer only","The estate agent"],"e":"A **stakeholder** holds the deposit for **both parties** until completion, on a separate stakeholder ledger."},{"q":"When acting for buyer and lender, whose money is the mortgage advance before completion?","o":["The lender's","The buyer's","The seller's","The firm's"],"e":"The advance is **client money held for the lender** until completion."},{"q":"Within what time of completion are the SDLT return and payment due?","o":["14 days","30 days","28 days","3 months"],"e":"SDLT is due within **14 days** of completion; it is an agency disbursement with no VAT."},{"q":"How is a released stakeholder deposit moved to the seller's ledger on completion?","o":["Inter-client transfer","Transfer to business account","Payment out of client account","Bank transfer to seller"],"e":"An **inter-client transfer**: DR stakeholder ledger, CR seller ledger, with no cash entry."}]},{"id":"accounts/probate-accounts","name":"Solicitors Accounts 11: Accounts in probate and estates (wills context)","qs":[{"q":"Who is the client in an estate administration?","o":["The personal representatives","The beneficiaries","The deceased's spouse","HMRC"],"e":"The **PRs** (executors or administrators) are the clients; money goes to the **estate ledger**."},{"q":"When is IHT on non-instalment property due?","o":["6 months after end of month of death","6 months after death","12 months after death","On issue of the grant"],"e":"IHT is due **6 months after the end of the month of death** and usually must be paid before the grant."},{"q":"Under which IHT funding route does the firm make no entries in its books?","o":["Direct payment scheme","Bank loan to PRs","Payment from client account","Payment from business account"],"e":"Under the **direct payment scheme** the deceased's bank pays HMRC directly, so nothing passes through the firm's accounts."},{"q":"Money held by a partner acting as executor and paid into client account falls under which rule 2.1 category?","o":["2.1(c)","2.1(a)","2.1(b)","2.1(d)"],"e":"**r.2.1(c)**: money held as **trustee** or holder of a **specified office**, such as executor."}]}]},{"id":"blp","name":"Business Law and Practice","topics":[{"id":"blp/business-structures","name":"Business Law and Practice 1: Business structures and legal personality","qs":[{"q":"Two friends start selling cakes together, sharing the profits, with no written agreement and nothing registered. What are they?","o":["A general partnership","Sole traders","An LLP","Nothing until they sign an agreement"],"e":"A partnership arises **automatically** when people carry on a business in common with a view of profit (**PA 1890 s.1**). No paperwork is needed."},{"q":"A creditor of a general partnership is owed £30,000. Who can it sue for the whole amount?","o":["Any one partner","Only the partnership name","Each partner for an equal share only","Only the senior partner"],"e":"Partners have **unlimited liability** for the firm's debts, so the creditor can claim the **whole debt** from any one of them, who then seeks contributions."},{"q":"With no partnership agreement, what happens when one of three partners dies?","o":["The partnership is dissolved","It continues with the other two","The deceased's heir becomes a partner","It converts into an LLP"],"e":"By default a partner's **death or bankruptcy dissolves** the partnership (**PA 1890 s.33**), unless the agreement says otherwise."},{"q":"How many designated members must an LLP have?","o":["At least 2","At least 1","At least 3","None: all members are equal"],"e":"An LLP needs at least **2 members** and **2 designated members**, who handle filings such as accounts and the confirmation statement. If fewer are named, every member is treated as designated."},{"q":"How is an LLP's profit taxed?","o":["Each member pays income tax on their share","The LLP pays corporation tax","Members pay tax only on drawings","The LLP pays income tax at the basic rate"],"e":"An LLP is **tax transparent**: like partners, members are taxed on their **share of profits**, whether or not drawn."},{"q":"What is the minimum number of directors for a private limited company?","o":["1, who must be a natural person","2, one of them a natural person","1, which may be another company","2 directors and a secretary"],"e":"A private company needs at least **1 director**, and at least one director must be a **natural person** (CA 2006 s.155). A secretary is optional."},{"q":"A new plc starts trading before getting its trading certificate. A supplier is not paid within 21 days of demand. Who must indemnify it?","o":["The directors personally","No one: the contract is void","The shareholders","Companies House"],"e":"The transaction stays **valid**, but the **directors** are jointly and severally liable to indemnify if the company fails to meet the obligation within **21 days** (CA 2006 s.767)."},{"q":"Asha bought 1,000 £1 shares at an issue price of £1.50 each and has paid £1,000. The company goes into insolvent liquidation owing £200,000. How much can Asha be made to pay?","o":["£500","£0","£1,500","£200,000"],"e":"Limited liability caps a shareholder's loss at what is **unpaid on their shares, including premium**: £1,500 due minus £1,000 paid = **£500**."},{"q":"A director signs a contract for a company that has not yet been formed. Who is liable on it?","o":["The director personally","The company once formed, automatically","No one: it is void","The other promoters equally"],"e":"A pre-incorporation contract takes effect as a contract with the **person acting for the company**, who is personally liable (**CA 2006 s.51**)."},{"q":"A seller agrees to sell his house, then transfers it to a company he controls to avoid completing. What will the court do?","o":["Order specific performance against him and the company","Nothing: the company is a separate person","Award damages against the company only","Dissolve the company"],"e":"Using a company to **evade an existing obligation** is the rare case where the court will look through it, so specific performance is ordered against both (Jones v Lipman)."},{"q":"Under the evasion principle, when may a court pierce the corporate veil?","o":["As a last resort, where a company is used to evade an existing legal obligation","Whenever justice requires it","Whenever one person controls the company","Whenever a group of companies acts as one economic unit"],"e":"Piercing is **very rare**: only where someone deliberately **evades an existing obligation** through a company, and only if no other remedy works (Prest)."},{"q":"Which business structures can grant a floating charge?","o":["Companies and LLPs","Only plcs","All five structures","Sole traders and partnerships only"],"e":"In practice only **companies and LLPs** can grant a **floating charge** over changing assets such as stock; individuals and partnerships cannot."},{"q":"How long does a private company have to file its accounts after its year end?","o":["9 months","6 months","12 months","3 months"],"e":"Private companies and LLPs must file accounts within **9 months** of the year end; a plc within **6 months**."},{"q":"What is the minimum allotted share capital for a public limited company?","o":["£50,000","£25,000","£100,000","£12,500"],"e":"A plc needs allotted share capital of at least **£50,000**, at least a quarter paid up."},{"q":"A sole trader incorporates his business and holds nearly all the shares. The company becomes insolvent. Are its debts his personal debts?","o":["No: the company is a separate legal person","Yes: he is the company in reality","Yes: a one-man company is his agent","Yes: the company was a sham from the start"],"e":"A company is a **separate legal person**: it owns its assets and owes its own debts, even if one person controls it (Salomon v Salomon)."},{"q":"A husband holds properties through companies he controls, but did not use them to evade any existing obligation. Can the divorce court pierce the corporate veil to treat the properties as his?","o":["No: the veil is pierced only to stop evasion of an existing obligation","Yes: he controls the companies, so their assets are his","Yes: whenever justice requires it","Yes: family courts can always pierce the veil"],"e":"The **veil** is pierced only where a company is used to **evade an existing legal obligation** (Prest v Petrodel; there, the properties were held on trust instead)."}]},{"id":"blp/formation","name":"Business Law and Practice 2: Forming a company, partnership or LLP (incl. ECCTA 2023)","qs":[{"q":"Which section makes the person acting for an unformed company personally liable on the contract?","o":["CA 2006 s.51","CA 2006 s.39","CA 2006 s.20","CA 2006 s.761"],"e":"**s.51**: the promoter is personally liable; the company cannot later ratify, so a **novation** is needed."},{"q":"Which articles apply if a company files none on incorporation?","o":["The Model Articles","Table A","No articles at all","Articles chosen by the registrar"],"e":"**s.20 CA 2006**: the **Model Articles** apply automatically by default."},{"q":"From what date is identity verification compulsory for new directors?","o":["18 November 2025","4 March 2024","26 January 2026","6 April 2026"],"e":"Under **ECCTA 2023**, IDV became compulsory for new directors and PSCs from **18 November 2025**."},{"q":"Which register must a company still keep itself after 18 November 2025?","o":["Register of members","Register of directors","Register of PSCs","Register of secretaries"],"e":"The Companies House register replaced the company's own director, secretary and PSC registers, but the **register of members** must still be kept."},{"q":"Jo signs a shop lease \"for and on behalf of Jo's Deli Ltd\" on Monday; the company is incorporated on Friday. How can liability on the lease be moved to the company?","o":["A novation: a new contract between the company and the landlord","The company ratifies the lease by board resolution","Automatically, on incorporation","By filing the lease at Companies House"],"e":"Jo is **personally liable** under s.51. The company **cannot ratify** a contract made before it existed, so a **novation** with the landlord's agreement is needed."},{"q":"Who is a person with significant control (PSC) on the shareholding test?","o":["Someone holding more than 25% of the shares or votes","Someone holding at least 10% of the shares","Someone holding more than 50% of the shares","Any director who also holds shares"],"e":"A **PSC** holds **more than 25%** of shares or votes, can appoint or remove a **majority of the board**, or has **significant influence or control**."},{"q":"What is the conclusive evidence that a company has been validly formed?","o":["The certificate of incorporation","The memorandum of association","The statement of compliance","The first board minutes"],"e":"Companies House issues a **certificate of incorporation** (s.15): **conclusive evidence** of valid formation; the company exists **from the date on it**."},{"q":"The articles say Mr Eley will be the company's solicitor for life. Can he enforce this under s.33 when the company stops using him?","o":["No: it is a right held as solicitor, not as a member","Yes: the articles are a contract with every member","Yes, if he also holds shares","Only by special resolution of the members"],"e":"Under **s.33** a member can enforce only rights held **as a member**, not rights held in **another capacity** such as solicitor or director."},{"q":"Oak Ltd has shareholders holding 40%, 35% and 25%. The first two vote to amend the articles; the 25% holder votes against. Does the resolution pass?","o":["Yes: 75% of the votes is enough for a special resolution","No: the 25% holder can block it","No: changing the articles needs unanimity","Yes, but only as an ordinary resolution"],"e":"Articles are changed by **special resolution** (at least **75%**, s.21). 75% passes it; a holder needs **more than 25%** to block."},{"q":"A company changes its name by special resolution. When does the change take effect?","o":["On the date of the new certificate of incorporation on change of name","On the date the resolution is passed","15 days after filing form NM01","At the next confirmation statement"],"e":"File the resolution and **NM01**; the change takes effect from the date of the **new certificate** (s.81). The company remains the **same legal person**."},{"q":"Within what period must a special resolution amending the articles be filed at Companies House?","o":["15 days","14 days","21 days","1 month"],"e":"File the **special resolution** within **15 days** (s.30) and the **amended articles** within **15 days** of the amendment taking effect (s.26)."},{"q":"Which of these may be used as a company's registered office address since 4 March 2024?","o":["A street address where documents will come to a company representative's attention","A PO box","The registered email address","Any address the directors choose, whether or not anyone is there"],"e":"Under **ECCTA** the registered office must be an **appropriate address** where delivery can be acknowledged. A **PO box is not appropriate**."},{"q":"Ravi and Sam trade as \"Fresh Fix Plumbing\". What must they do about the name?","o":["Show both partners' names and a UK service address on invoices and at their premises","Register the name at Companies House","Add \"LLP\" to the name","Nothing: partnerships face no name rules"],"e":"A firm trading under a name that is **not just the partners' surnames** must disclose the **partners' names** and a **UK address for service** (CA 2006 ss.1202, 1204)."},{"q":"What must a private company file to re-register as a plc, apart from the special resolution and amended articles?","o":["A balance sheet from the last 7 months with an unqualified auditor's report and statement","A trading certificate application on SH50","A declaration of solvency","A statement of lawful purpose only"],"e":"Re-registration (ss.90-96) needs a **balance sheet** within **7 months**, an **unqualified auditor's report** and an auditor's statement on net assets. A re-registered plc needs **no trading certificate**."}]},{"id":"blp/directors","name":"Business Law and Practice 3: Directors: appointment, removal and duties","qs":[{"q":"How much special notice is needed of a resolution to remove a director under s.168?","o":["28 days","14 days","21 days","7 days"],"e":"**s.168/s.312**: special notice to the company at least **28 days** before the meeting."},{"q":"Which clause gives a director extra votes on a resolution to remove them?","o":["A Bushell v Faith clause","A drag-along clause","A pre-emption clause","An indulgence clause"],"e":"A **Bushell v Faith** weighted-voting clause is valid and can block removal."},{"q":"Which section requires directors to avoid conflicts of interest?","o":["s.175","s.172","s.177","s.174"],"e":"**s.175** covers conflicts, e.g. exploiting company opportunities. s.177 is declaring interests in proposed transactions."},{"q":"What resolution approves a director's service contract guaranteed for over 2 years?","o":["Ordinary resolution","Special resolution","Board resolution","Unanimous resolution"],"e":"**s.188**: prior approval by **ordinary resolution** of members."},{"q":"A majority shareholder is not on the board, but the directors are accustomed to act on his instructions. What is he?","o":["A shadow director","A de jure director","A non-executive director","An alternate director"],"e":"A **shadow director** (s.251) is someone in accordance with whose directions the directors are **accustomed to act**. Professional advisers are not caught just because their advice is followed."},{"q":"Can shareholders remove a director under s.168 by written resolution?","o":["No: it must be an ordinary resolution at a general meeting","Yes, by a written ordinary resolution","Yes, by a written special resolution","Only if the director consents"],"e":"A written resolution **cannot** be used for s.168 removal (s.288(2)), because the director has the **right to be heard** at the meeting."},{"q":"Ffion, managing director, signs a £70,000 contract although the articles require board approval for contracts over £50,000. Can the supplier, acting in good faith, enforce it?","o":["Yes, but Ffion breaches s.171","No: the contract exceeds her powers","Only if the shareholders ratify it","Yes, and Ffion commits no breach"],"e":"Under **s.40** (and apparent authority) the directors' power is treated as **free of constitutional limits** for a good faith third party. Internally, Ffion breaches her duty to **act within powers (s.171)**."},{"q":"Directors honestly allot shares to a favoured bidder to dilute the majority shareholders and defeat an unwanted takeover. Which duty have they breached?","o":["s.171: using a power for an improper purpose","s.174: lack of reasonable care","s.176: accepting a benefit","None, as they acted honestly"],"e":"The power to allot is for raising capital, not manipulating control. Breach of the **proper purpose** rule in **s.171** even if the directors acted honestly."},{"q":"An experienced chartered accountant is finance director. By what standard is her care and skill judged under s.174?","o":["The higher standard of her own knowledge and experience","Only that of a reasonable finance director","Only what she honestly believed","The lower of the objective and subjective standards"],"e":"**s.174** sets an **objective minimum**, raised by the director's **actual** knowledge, skill and experience. Her expertise **raises** the standard."},{"q":"A managing director takes for himself a contract that the client would never have given to the company. Is he liable to the company?","o":["Yes: he must account for his profits","No: the company could not have obtained the contract","Only if the company suffered loss","Only if he was still a director when paid"],"e":"**s.175(2)**: it does **not matter whether the company could take the opportunity**. The usual remedy is an **account of profits**."},{"q":"In a private company formed under CA 2006, who can authorise a director's conflict of interest under s.175?","o":["The other directors, unless the constitution prohibits it","Only the shareholders by special resolution","Only the court","No one: conflicts cannot be authorised"],"e":"**s.175(5)**: the **board** can authorise unless the constitution prohibits it. The conflicted director does **not count** in the quorum or the vote."},{"q":"A director fails to declare an interest in a transaction the company has already entered into. What is the consequence under s.182?","o":["A criminal offence, but the transaction stays valid","The transaction is void","The transaction is voidable only","Nothing, as no declaration is needed"],"e":"**s.182** (existing transactions): failure to declare is a **criminal offence** (a fine), but it does **not** affect the transaction's validity. s.177 breach is civil."},{"q":"Members ratify a director's breach of duty under s.239. Whose votes are disregarded?","o":["The director's and any connected member's","Only the director's","No one's","All directors' votes"],"e":"Ratification is by **ordinary resolution**, disregarding the votes of the **director** and members **connected** with them (e.g. spouse, a company they control)."},{"q":"How long must a court disqualify an unfit director of an insolvent company for under CDDA s.6?","o":["At least 2 and up to 15 years","At least 1 and up to 10 years","At least 5 and up to 20 years","Up to 15 years, with no minimum"],"e":"**CDDA s.6**: disqualification is **mandatory** for **2 to 15 years**. Acting while disqualified is a crime and brings personal liability (s.15)."}]},{"id":"blp/decision-making","name":"Business Law and Practice 4: Decision-making: board, general meetings and resolutions","qs":[{"q":"What is the notice period for a general meeting of a private company?","o":["14 clear days","21 clear days","28 clear days","7 clear days"],"e":"**s.307**: 14 clear days (21 for a public company AGM)."},{"q":"What percentage of voting shares is needed for short notice in a private company?","o":["90%","95%","75%","51%"],"e":"Short notice needs a **majority in number** holding at least **90%** (95% for a public company)."},{"q":"Above what value is a substantial property transaction always caught by s.190?","o":["£100,000","£50,000","£10,000","£5,000"],"e":"**s.190**: over **£100,000**, or over 10% of net assets and over £5,000."},{"q":"When does a proposed written resolution lapse if not passed?","o":["After 28 days","After 14 days","After 21 days","After 42 days"],"e":"A written resolution **lapses after 28 days** unless the articles set another period."},{"q":"What is the quorum for a board meeting under the private company Model Articles?","o":["2, unless the directors fix a higher number","1","3","A majority of the directors"],"e":"**Art.11**: quorum is **2** unless the directors fix a different number (never less than 2), except a company with a **sole director**."},{"q":"A two-director company wants to buy an asset from one of its directors. Under art.14 he cannot count in the quorum. What is the usual solution?","o":["An ordinary resolution of the shareholders disapplying art.14","The other director decides alone","The chair uses a casting vote","A special resolution changing the quorum to 1"],"e":"With only one director able to count, the board is **inquorate**. The shareholders can **disapply art.14 by ordinary resolution** (art.14(3)(a)), or the remaining director can appoint another director."},{"q":"Notice of a private company general meeting is emailed on Monday 1 June. What is the earliest date the meeting can be held?","o":["Thursday 18 June","Monday 15 June","Wednesday 17 June","Tuesday 16 June"],"e":"Deemed received **48 hours** later (Wednesday 3 June). **14 clear days** exclude that day and the meeting day: 4 to 17 June, so the earliest is **18 June**."},{"q":"Shareholders holding at least what percentage of paid-up voting capital can require the directors to call a general meeting?","o":["5%","10%","25%","15%"],"e":"**s.303**: holders of **5%**. Directors must call it within **21 days**, for a date within **28 days** of the notice."},{"q":"A private company has 100 shares. 60 eligible votes agree to a written special resolution; the rest do not reply. Does it pass?","o":["No: it needs 75% of the total eligible votes","Yes: everyone who replied agreed","Yes: 60% is a majority","Only if no one objects within 28 days"],"e":"On a **written resolution**, majorities are of **all eligible votes**, not those replying. A special one needs **75**; non-replies count against."},{"q":"Pine Ltd has net assets of £400,000. It agrees to buy a £45,000 van from its director's wife. Is shareholder approval needed?","o":["Yes: it is a substantial property transaction","No: it is under £100,000","No: the wife is not a director","No: vans are not substantial assets"],"e":"The wife is a **connected person**, and £45,000 exceeds **10% of net assets** (£40,000) and **£5,000**, so an **ordinary resolution** is needed under s.190."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT a person connected with a director for the substantial property transaction rules?","o":["The director's brother","The director's spouse","The director's parent","A company in which the director holds 25% of the voting power"],"e":"Connected persons include a **spouse**, **children**, **parents** and a company where the director holds at least **20%**. **Siblings are not** connected."}]},{"id":"blp/shareholders-minority","name":"Business Law and Practice 5: Shareholders' rights and minority protection","qs":[{"q":"Which section allows a member to petition for unfairly prejudicial conduct?","o":["CA 2006 s.994","CA 2006 s.260","IA 1986 s.122(1)(g)","CA 2006 s.168"],"e":"**s.994**: the usual remedy under s.996 is a buy-out at fair value."},{"q":"What shareholding is needed to block a special resolution?","o":["More than 25%","More than 10%","More than 50%","At least 5%"],"e":"A special resolution needs 75%, so **over 25%** can block it."},{"q":"Who receives the remedy in a successful derivative claim?","o":["The company","The claimant shareholder","All shareholders pro rata","The company's creditors"],"e":"A derivative claim is brought **on behalf of the company**, so any remedy goes to the company."},{"q":"Three people run a company as a quasi-partnership based on mutual trust. Two remove the third as director and exclude him from management. On what basis may he petition to wind it up?","o":["Just and equitable winding up","Inability to pay its debts","Wrongful trading by the directors","Members' voluntary winding up"],"e":"In a **quasi-partnership**, exclusion from management after a breakdown of trust can justify **just and equitable winding up** (IA 1986 s.122(1)(g); Ebrahimi v Westbourne Galleries)."},{"q":"A buyer pays for shares and receives a signed stock transfer form. When does she become a member?","o":["When entered in the register of members","When she pays the price","When she receives the transfer form","When stamp duty is paid"],"e":"A person becomes a member when they agree to and are **entered in the register of members** (s.112). Until then she cannot vote."},{"q":"Shares are sold for £10,100. How much stamp duty is payable, and by whom?","o":["£55, by the buyer","£50.50, by the buyer","£55, by the seller","Nothing: only transfers over £20,000 are stamped"],"e":"Stamp duty is **0.5%** (£50.50), **rounded up to the nearest £5** = **£55**, paid by the **buyer** on transfers over **£1,000**."},{"q":"At the full permission hearing, when MUST the court refuse a derivative claim for a past breach of duty?","o":["If the breach has been validly ratified","If the claimant became a member after the breach","If the claimant holds under 5% of the shares","If the claim is against a former director"],"e":"**s.263(2)**: permission must be refused if a s.172 director would not continue it, or the act was **authorised or ratified**. Joining after the wrong does **not** bar a claim (s.260(4))."},{"q":"Directors' breach of duty causes the company a loss, and a shareholder's shares fall in value. Can she sue the directors personally for that fall?","o":["No: it merely reflects the company's loss","Yes, as she has suffered a personal loss","Yes, under s.33","Only if she holds over 25%"],"e":"Under the **reflective loss** rule a shareholder cannot recover a fall in share value that **reflects the company's loss** where the company has its own claim. The company (or a derivative claim) sues."},{"q":"A quasi-partner wins a s.994 petition and the court orders a buy-out of his minority stake. How are his shares usually valued?","o":["Pro rata, with no minority discount","At nominal value","Pro rata, less a minority discount","At the price he originally paid"],"e":"In a **quasi-partnership**, shares are usually valued **pro rata** to the company's value **without a minority discount**."},{"q":"A minority shareholder is not excluded and no agreement or understanding was broken, but trust has broken down. Does she have a s.994 petition?","o":["No: there is no right to exit at will","Yes: breakdown of trust is always unfair","Yes, if she holds 25% or more","Only if the company is solvent"],"e":"Unfairness usually needs **breach of the agreed terms** or conduct equity regards as contrary to good faith. There is **no right to leave** just because trust has broken down."},{"q":"A shareholder dies. Under the Model Articles, what can his personal representatives do before being registered?","o":["Receive dividends, but not attend or vote at general meetings","Attend and vote at general meetings","Nothing until registered","Vote only on written resolutions"],"e":"A **transmittee** has the holder's rights (e.g. dividends) but **cannot attend or vote** until registered (art.27(3)). They may elect to be registered or transfer the shares."}]},{"id":"blp/partnerships","name":"Business Law and Practice 6: Partnerships and LLPs: authority, agreements and liability","qs":[{"q":"Under the Partnership Act 1890 default rules, how are profits shared?","o":["Equally","In proportion to capital","By time worked","By majority decision"],"e":"Default rule: capital and profits shared **equally**, whatever each partner contributed."},{"q":"Is an incoming partner liable for firm debts incurred before they joined?","o":["No, under s.17(1)","Yes, jointly with others","Yes, jointly and severally","Only if they had notice"],"e":"**s.17(1) PA 1890**: an incoming partner is not liable for debts incurred before joining."},{"q":"What interest does a partner receive on advances beyond capital by default?","o":["5%","No interest","3%","Bank base rate"],"e":"Default rule: **no interest on capital**, but **5%** on advances beyond capital."},{"q":"Which section of the Partnership Act 1890 makes every partner an agent of the firm?","o":["s.5","s.1","s.14","s.36"],"e":"**s.5**: acts in the usual way of business bind the firm unless the third party knew of the lack of authority."},{"q":"Two sisters inherit a house and share the rent. Does that make them partners?","o":["No: co-ownership alone does not create a partnership","Yes: they share income from a common asset","Yes, if the rent exceeds their costs","Only if they register for VAT"],"e":"**PA 1890 s.2**: **co-owning property** does not by itself create a partnership, nor does sharing gross returns. A share of profits is only prima facie evidence."},{"q":"With no partnership agreement, what consent is needed to admit a new partner?","o":["All the partners","A majority of the partners","Partners holding 75% of the capital","The senior partner alone"],"e":"Admitting a **new partner** needs **unanimous** consent (s.24(7)); so does changing the nature of the business. Ordinary matters go by majority."},{"q":"With no partnership agreement, can a majority of partners expel a partner who is not pulling his weight?","o":["No: there is no expulsion power unless expressly agreed","Yes, by simple majority","Yes, by a 75% majority","Only by court order under s.35"],"e":"**s.25**: a majority **cannot expel** a partner unless an **express power** has been agreed. That is why agreements include expulsion clauses."},{"q":"A builders' partnership agreement bars orders over £5,000 without consent. Dan orders £12,000 of timber from a supplier unaware of the limit. Is the firm bound?","o":["Yes: it is usual business and the supplier had no notice","No: Dan exceeded his actual authority","Only if the other partners ratify it","Only up to £5,000"],"e":"Under **s.5** an act in the **usual way** of the firm's business binds it unless the outsider **knew** of the lack of authority (s.8). Dan must **indemnify** his partners."},{"q":"Ella retires on 31 March. In May the firm buys on credit from a long-standing supplier who was never told she left; a Gazette notice was published. Is Ella liable?","o":["Yes: existing contacts need actual notice","No: the Gazette notice protects her","No: she was not a partner in May","Only if she agreed to the purchase"],"e":"**s.36**: old customers can treat her as a partner until they get **actual notice**. A **London Gazette** notice protects only against people who had not dealt with the firm before."},{"q":"How can a retiring partner be released from liability for firm debts incurred while she was a partner?","o":["By a novation agreed with the creditor","By an indemnity from the continuing partners","By a notice in the London Gazette","Automatically, on retirement"],"e":"She stays liable for debts from her time (s.17(2)) unless there is a **novation** with the creditor (s.17(3)). An indemnity from partners does not bind the creditor."},{"q":"On dissolution, in what order are a partnership's assets applied under s.44?","o":["Outside creditors, partners' advances, partners' capital, surplus","Partners' capital, outside creditors, advances, surplus","Outside creditors, partners' capital, advances, surplus","Advances, outside creditors, capital, surplus"],"e":"**s.44**: (1) **outside creditors**, (2) partners' **advances**, (3) partners' **capital**, (4) **surplus** in the profit-sharing ratio."},{"q":"An LLP has no members' agreement. Can members be paid a salary for working in the business?","o":["No: there is no remuneration by default","Yes, in proportion to hours worked","Yes, if a majority agrees","Only the designated members"],"e":"**LLP Regulations 2001 reg.7(4)**: **no remuneration** by default; profits and capital are shared **equally**."}]},{"id":"blp/equity-finance","name":"Business Law and Practice 7: Equity finance: shares, allotment, buyback and redemption","qs":[{"q":"Which section gives existing shareholders pre-emption rights on new shares issued for cash?","o":["s.561","s.551","s.550","s.569"],"e":"**s.561**: new ordinary shares for cash must first be offered pro rata, with at least 14 days to accept."},{"q":"What is the maximum duration of a s.551 authority to allot?","o":["5 years","3 years","1 year","10 years"],"e":"**s.551**: authority must state the maximum number of shares and last no more than **5 years**."},{"q":"What resolution approves a contract for a company to buy back its own shares?","o":["Ordinary resolution","Special resolution","Board resolution only","Unanimous resolution"],"e":"An **ordinary resolution** approves the buyback contract; the seller's votes do not count."},{"q":"Within what period must form SH01 be filed after an allotment?","o":["1 month","14 days","21 days","2 months"],"e":"File the **SH01 return of allotment within 1 month**."},{"q":"A private company with one class of ordinary shares and Model Articles wants to issue more ordinary shares. Do the directors need an ordinary resolution giving authority to allot?","o":["No: s.550 gives them authority unless the articles prohibit it","Yes: s.551 always applies","Yes: a special resolution is needed","Only if the shares are issued at a premium"],"e":"**s.550**: in a private company with **one class**, the directors can allot more of that class **unless the articles prohibit it**. Otherwise s.551 authority is needed."},{"q":"A company issues new ordinary shares to a seller in exchange for her business premises. Do statutory pre-emption rights apply?","o":["No: the shares are not issued for cash","Yes: all new ordinary shares are caught","Yes, unless disapplied by special resolution","Only if the company is a plc"],"e":"**s.561** applies only to equity securities issued **wholly for cash**. Shares issued for **non-cash consideration** are outside it (s.565)."},{"q":"A s.551 authority to allot is given by ordinary resolution. Must it be filed at Companies House?","o":["Yes, within 15 days","No: only special resolutions are filed","Yes, within 1 month with the SH01","Only if the company is public"],"e":"A **s.551 resolution** is an **ordinary** resolution but **must still be filed** within **15 days** (s.551(9), s.30)."},{"q":"A private company's articles allow de minimis cash buybacks. Its share capital is £200,000. What is the most it can buy back from cash in a financial year without identifying distributable profits?","o":["£10,000","£15,000","£20,000","£5,000"],"e":"The de minimis limit is the **lower of £15,000 or 5% of share capital**. 5% of £200,000 = **£10,000**."},{"q":"Within what period after the special resolution must a private company make a payment out of capital for a buyback?","o":["No earlier than 5 weeks and no later than 7 weeks","Within 1 week","No earlier than 28 days","Within 15 days"],"e":"**s.723**: payment between **5 and 7 weeks** after the special resolution, after the 5-week objection period for members and creditors."},{"q":"Within what period after the directors' statement must the special resolution approving a buyback out of capital be passed?","o":["On the same day or within one week","Within 15 days","Within 5 weeks","Within 28 days"],"e":"**s.716**: the special resolution must be passed on, or within **one week after**, the date of the directors' statement."},{"q":"A seller has owned shares in an unquoted trading company for 3 years (not inherited). The company buys them all back for the benefit of the trade. How is the payment taxed?","o":["As a distribution (dividend income) above the subscription price","As a capital gain with BADR","Wholly as a capital gain","It is tax free"],"e":"Capital treatment needs **5 years' ownership** (3 if inherited), among other conditions. Failing that, the excess over the subscribed amount is a **distribution** taxed as dividend income."}]},{"id":"blp/debt-security","name":"Business Law and Practice 8: Debt finance and security","qs":[{"q":"Within how many days must a charge be registered at Companies House?","o":["21 days","14 days","28 days","1 month"],"e":"**s.859A**: file MR01 within **21 days** of creation."},{"q":"A bank takes a charge labelled 'fixed' over a company's book debts, but the company may pay the proceeds into its ordinary account and use them freely. How is the charge classified?","o":["Floating: the company controls the asset","Fixed: the parties' label decides","Fixed: book debts can only be subject to fixed charges","Void: book debts cannot be charged"],"e":"The **degree of control** over the asset, not the label, decides; free use of proceeds makes the charge **floating** (Re Spectrum Plus)."},{"q":"What happens to an unregistered charge on the company's insolvency?","o":["Void against liquidator and creditors","Valid but ranks last","Valid against everyone","Converted to a floating charge"],"e":"An unregistered charge is **void** against a liquidator, administrator and creditors; the debt becomes immediately repayable but unsecured."},{"q":"Which clause stops a company granting other security without the lender's consent?","o":["Negative pledge","Event of default","Condition precedent","Financial covenant"],"e":"A **negative pledge** prohibits further security without consent."},{"q":"Cedar Ltd grants a bank a fixed charge on 1 March. The bank files the MR01 on 25 March. What is the position?","o":["The charge is void against a liquidator and creditors; the loan becomes immediately repayable","The charge is valid: filing was within a month","The loan itself is void","The charge ranks behind later charges but stays valid"],"e":"The **21 days** begin the day after creation (2 to 22 March). Filing late makes the **security void** (s.859H), but the **debt** stays valid and becomes **immediately repayable**."},{"q":"A charge was not registered within 21 days. How can it still be registered?","o":["Only with a court order extending time","By paying a late filing fee","By the company filing a statement of satisfaction","It cannot be registered at all"],"e":"Late registration needs a **court order** under **s.859F** (e.g. failure was accidental), usually without prejudice to rights acquired meanwhile."},{"q":"Elm Ltd gives Bank A a floating charge with a negative pledge, then gives Bank B a fixed charge over its warehouse. B did not know of the negative pledge. Who has priority over the warehouse?","o":["Bank B","Bank A","They rank equally","Whoever registered first"],"e":"A **later fixed charge** beats an earlier floating charge, unless the fixed chargee had **actual notice** of a **negative pledge**. Registration alone is not notice."},{"q":"Which of these events automatically crystallises a floating charge?","o":["The company goes into liquidation","The company breaches a financial covenant","The company makes a loss for the year","A director resigns"],"e":"Crystallisation happens by law on **winding up**, appointment of a **receiver**, or the company **ceasing business**, and otherwise as the charge provides."},{"q":"A director personally guarantees his company's bank loan. Can he vote on the board resolution approving the facility under the Model Articles?","o":["Yes: a guarantee of the company's obligations is a permitted cause","No: he is interested so cannot vote","Only if the shareholders pass a special resolution","Only if he is the chair"],"e":"He must declare his interest (s.177), but **art.14(4)** makes a director's guarantee of the company's obligations a **permitted cause**, so he can vote and count."},{"q":"What is the main difference between a term loan and a revolving credit facility?","o":["Under an RCF the company can repay and re-borrow during the term","A term loan is repayable on demand","An RCF must be repaid in one bullet payment","A term loan can be redrawn once repaid"],"e":"An **RCF** lets the borrower **borrow, repay and re-borrow** up to a maximum. A **term loan** once repaid cannot be redrawn. An **overdraft** is usually repayable on demand."}]},{"id":"blp/distributions-accounts","name":"Business Law and Practice 9: Distributions, dividends and accounting requirements","qs":[{"q":"Which section limits dividends to profits available for distribution?","o":["CA 2006 s.830","CA 2006 s.847","CA 2006 s.386","CA 2006 s.172"],"e":"**s.830**: accumulated realised profits less accumulated realised losses."},{"q":"Who decides to pay an interim dividend under the Model Articles?","o":["The directors alone","Shareholders by ordinary resolution","Shareholders by special resolution","The auditors"],"e":"**Interim** dividends are decided by the directors; **final** dividends are declared by ordinary resolution."},{"q":"How long after its year end must a private company file its accounts?","o":["9 months","6 months","12 months","3 months"],"e":"Private companies: **9 months**; public companies: 6 months."},{"q":"What is the turnover limit for a small company from 6 April 2025?","o":["£15m","£10.2m","£36m","£1m"],"e":"Small company: turnover up to **£15m**, balance sheet up to £7.5m, up to 50 employees (2 of 3)."},{"q":"Ash Ltd made realised profits of £40,000 this year but has £55,000 of realised losses brought forward. Its land was revalued up by £100,000. What dividend can it pay?","o":["None","£40,000","£85,000","£100,000"],"e":"Distributable profits are **accumulated realised** profits less losses: £40,000 − £55,000 = **−£15,000**. The **revaluation gain is unrealised** and does not count."},{"q":"The directors recommend a final dividend of 10p per share. Under the Model Articles, can the shareholders declare 15p?","o":["No: they cannot exceed the amount recommended","Yes, by ordinary resolution","Yes, by special resolution","Only if distributable profits allow"],"e":"**Art.30(2)**: shareholders can declare a final dividend **up to** the amount the directors recommend, not more."},{"q":"Shareholder-directors take dividends knowing the company has no profits, but not knowing that this is unlawful. Must they repay?","o":["Yes: knowing the facts is enough; ignorance of the law is no defence","No: they did not know the law","Only if the company is insolvent","No, if they acted on an accountant's advice"],"e":"Under **s.847** a member repays if they **knew or had reasonable grounds to believe** the facts making it unlawful. **Ignorance of the law** is no defence."},{"q":"What additional test must a public company satisfy before paying a dividend?","o":["Net assets must not fall below called-up share capital plus undistributable reserves","It must have positive cash flow for 12 months","It must hold an AGM first","Its auditors must approve the dividend"],"e":"**s.831 net assets test**: after the distribution, a plc's net assets must be at least **called-up share capital plus undistributable reserves**."},{"q":"A small company is exempt from audit. Members holding what percentage can require an audit?","o":["10% of the nominal value of issued shares","5%","25%","Any single member"],"e":"**s.476**: holders of at least **10%** can require an audit, by notice no later than **one month before** the year end."}]},{"id":"blp/corporate-insolvency","name":"Business Law and Practice 10: Corporate insolvency procedures","qs":[{"q":"Above what debt can a creditor serve a statutory demand to deem a company insolvent?","o":["£750","£5,000","£500","£1,000"],"e":"**IA 1986 s.123**: a debt of more than **£750** unpaid 3 weeks after a statutory demand."},{"q":"What is the first purpose of administration?","o":["Rescue the company as a going concern","Better result for creditors as a whole","Realise property for secured creditors","Sell the business by pre-pack"],"e":"**Sch B1 para 3**: purposes are a strict hierarchy, starting with **rescue**."},{"q":"What creditor approval is needed for a CVA?","o":["75% by value of those voting","50% by value of all creditors","90% by number of creditors","Unanimous creditor approval"],"e":"**75% by value** of creditors voting, and not opposed by over 50% of unconnected creditors."},{"q":"Within what period must a declaration of solvency precede the resolution for an MVL?","o":["5 weeks","12 months","14 days","3 months"],"e":"Directors declare solvency (debts paid within 12 months) in the **5 weeks** before the special resolution."},{"q":"A supplier owed £2,000 serves a statutory demand on Kite Ltd on 1 May. Kite pays nothing and does not dispute it. From when is Kite deemed unable to pay its debts?","o":["22 May, after 3 weeks","15 May, after 14 days","1 June, after 1 month","Immediately on service"],"e":"**s.123(1)(a)**: a debt over **£750** unpaid, unsecured and uncompounded for **3 weeks** after a statutory demand."},{"q":"A company owns valuable property but has no cash to pay bills now due. Is it unable to pay its debts under s.123?","o":["Yes: it fails the cash flow test","No: it is balance sheet solvent","Only if a statutory demand is served","Only if its liabilities exceed its assets"],"e":"Either test is enough. A company can be **balance sheet solvent but cash flow insolvent**, and that satisfies **s.123(1)(e)**."},{"q":"How long does a Part A1 moratorium initially last?","o":["20 business days","28 days","8 weeks","1 year"],"e":"An initial **20 business days**, extendable by the directors for **20 more** without creditor consent, and up to **1 year** with creditors' consent."},{"q":"A CVA is approved by the required majority of creditors. Is a secured creditor who voted against bound to give up its security?","o":["No: a CVA cannot affect secured creditors' rights without consent","Yes: a CVA binds all creditors","Yes, if 75% in value approved","Only if the court confirms the CVA"],"e":"A CVA binds every **unsecured** creditor entitled to vote, but cannot affect **secured** or **preferential** creditors' rights **without their consent** (s.4(3)-(4))."},{"q":"The directors want to appoint an administrator out of court. How much notice of intention must they give to a qualifying floating charge holder?","o":["5 business days","2 business days","14 days","28 days"],"e":"**Para.22**: **5 business days' notice** to any **QFCH**, who may appoint its own choice of administrator instead. A QFCH appointing gives 2 business days' notice to a prior QFCH."},{"q":"Within what period must an administrator send creditors a statement of proposals?","o":["8 weeks","14 days","10 weeks","6 months"],"e":"**Para.49**: as soon as reasonably practicable and within **8 weeks**; creditors normally decide on them within **10 weeks**."},{"q":"A winding-up petition is presented on 1 March and the order is made on 15 April. The company paid a supplier £10,000 on 20 March. What is the status of the payment?","o":["Void unless the court validates it","Valid: it was before the winding-up order","Voidable only as a preference","Valid if made in good faith"],"e":"**s.127**: dispositions **after presentation of the petition** are **void** unless the court orders otherwise. Winding up is treated as starting at the **petition** date (s.129)."},{"q":"In a creditors' voluntary liquidation, the members and creditors nominate different liquidators. Whose choice prevails?","o":["The creditors'","The members'","The directors'","The court decides in every case"],"e":"In a **CVL** the members may nominate, but the **creditors' choice prevails**. In an **MVL** the members appoint."},{"q":"A lender with a fixed charge over a company's office building wants to be repaid from that building only. What is the usual step?","o":["Appoint a fixed charge (LPA) receiver","Appoint an administrator out of court","Propose a CVA","Seek a Part A1 moratorium"],"e":"A **fixed charge / LPA receiver** collects income from or sells **the charged asset** for the appointing lender. Out-of-court administrator appointment needs a **QFCH**."}]},{"id":"blp/insolvency-clawback-priority","name":"Business Law and Practice 11: Clawback, directors' liability and order of priority","qs":[{"q":"What is the time limit for a preference to an unconnected creditor?","o":["6 months","2 years","12 months","3 years"],"e":"**IA s.239**: 6 months (unconnected) or 2 years (connected)."},{"q":"An insolvent company repays its bank overdraft only because the bank threatened to withdraw its facilities and the directors wanted to keep trading. Is the payment a voidable preference?","o":["No: the company was not influenced by a desire to prefer","Yes: any payment improving a creditor's position is a preference","Yes: the bank was paid ahead of other creditors","Only if the bank was a connected person"],"e":"A preference needs the company to be **influenced by a desire to prefer**; yielding to **commercial pressure** is not that (Re MC Bacon)."},{"q":"What is the maximum prescribed part for floating charges created from 6 April 2020?","o":["£800,000","£600,000","£1,000,000","£250,000"],"e":"The prescribed part is 50% of the first £10,000 and 20% of the rest, capped at **£800,000**."},{"q":"Which IA 1986 section creates liability for wrongful trading?","o":["s.214","s.213","s.212","s.216"],"e":"**s.214**: wrongful trading. s.213 is fraudulent trading; s.212 misfeasance; s.216 phoenix names."},{"q":"18 months before liquidation, an insolvent company sells a £30,000 van to its director's brother for £5,000. Can the liquidator challenge it?","o":["Yes: a transaction at an undervalue within 2 years","No: the 6-month limit has passed","No: a brother is not connected","Only if the company intended to defraud creditors"],"e":"**s.238**: undervalue within **2 years**. The brother is an **associate** of a director, so **connected**, and insolvency at the time is **presumed**."},{"q":"Two months before liquidation, an insolvent company repays an overdraft personally guaranteed by its director Gail. Whom has it preferred?","o":["Gail, whose guarantee liability is removed","Only the bank","No one: repaying a bank is never a preference","The trade creditors"],"e":"A preference can benefit a **guarantor**. Gail is **connected**, so the **2-year** limit applies and **desire to prefer is presumed** (s.239(6))."},{"q":"An insolvent company owing its bank £100,000 grants it a floating charge and the bank lends a further £40,000 the same day. Liquidation follows 9 months later. What does the charge validly secure?","o":["Only the £40,000 new money (plus interest)","The full £140,000","Nothing: the charge is wholly invalid","Only the original £100,000"],"e":"**s.245**: a floating charge to an unconnected chargee within **12 months** is invalid except for **new consideration** given at or after creation."},{"q":"A floating charge is granted to a director 18 months before liquidation, when the company was solvent. Can it be challenged under s.245?","o":["Yes: for a connected person solvency at the time is irrelevant","No: the company was solvent","No: the 12-month period has passed","Only if the director acted dishonestly"],"e":"For a **connected** chargee the period is **2 years** and **solvency at the time is irrelevant**. It is still valid for new value given."},{"q":"Which claim needs proof of dishonesty and can be brought against anyone knowingly involved, not just directors?","o":["Fraudulent trading (s.213)","Wrongful trading (s.214)","Misfeasance (s.212)","Transaction at an undervalue (s.238)"],"e":"**Fraudulent trading** needs **intent to defraud** and catches **anyone** knowingly a party; it is also a crime. **Wrongful trading** is directors only, with no dishonesty."},{"q":"After expenses and preferential debts, £200,000 of floating charge assets remain (charge created in 2022). What is the prescribed part?","o":["£43,000","£40,000","£45,000","£100,000"],"e":"**50% of the first £10,000** (£5,000) + **20% of the remaining £190,000** (£38,000) = **£43,000** for unsecured creditors."},{"q":"An employee is owed £1,500 of unpaid wages for the last 2 months when the company goes into liquidation. How much is an ordinary preferential debt?","o":["£800","£1,500","£700","None: wages are unsecured"],"e":"Unpaid wages for the **4 months** before insolvency are preferential up to **£800** per employee. The other **£700** is unsecured."},{"q":"In a liquidation, which tax debt owed to HMRC is a secondary preferential debt?","o":["PAYE income tax deducted from employees","Corporation tax","Employer's NICs","Interest on late corporation tax"],"e":"HMRC is secondary preferential only for taxes **collected for others**: **VAT, PAYE, employees' NICs**. **Corporation tax** and **employer's NICs** are **unsecured**."}]},{"id":"blp/personal-insolvency","name":"Business Law and Practice 12: Personal insolvency: bankruptcy and IVAs","qs":[{"q":"What is the minimum debt for a creditor's bankruptcy petition?","o":["£5,000","£750","£10,000","£2,500"],"e":"A creditor's petition needs an unsecured debt of at least **£5,000**."},{"q":"When is a bankrupt automatically discharged?","o":["After 1 year","After 3 years","After 6 months","After 2 years"],"e":"Discharge is **automatic after 1 year**, but vested property stays with the trustee."},{"q":"How far back can a trustee challenge a transaction at an undervalue in bankruptcy?","o":["5 years","2 years","6 months","3 years"],"e":"**IA s.339**: within **5 years** before the petition; insolvency need not be shown if within 2 years."},{"q":"An IVA is approved by 80% in value of creditors voting. An unsecured creditor voted against. Is it bound?","o":["Yes: an IVA binds every creditor entitled to vote","No: only creditors who voted for it are bound","Only if it received notice of the vote","No: unanimity is needed"],"e":"An approved IVA binds **every creditor entitled to vote**, whether they voted for or against (s.260), but not **secured** or **preferential** creditors without consent."},{"q":"Within what period of service can a debtor apply to set aside a statutory demand?","o":["18 days","21 days","14 days","28 days"],"e":"The debtor has **3 weeks** to pay, but may apply to set the demand aside within **18 days** (r.10.4), e.g. if the debt is genuinely disputed."},{"q":"A self-employed carpenter is made bankrupt. What happens to the basic tools he needs for his work?","o":["They are excluded from his estate, unless worth more than a reasonable replacement","They vest in the trustee and are sold","They are sold and he gets 50% of the proceeds","They are held until discharge"],"e":"**s.283(2)**: **tools of the trade** necessary for the bankrupt personally are excluded, but the trustee can claim items worth more than a **reasonable replacement** (s.308)."},{"q":"A bankrupt inherits £40,000 six months after the bankruptcy order, before discharge. Can the trustee claim it?","o":["Yes, as after-acquired property, by written notice","No: only property owned at the start vests","Only if the court makes an income payments order","No: inheritances are always exempt"],"e":"**s.307**: the trustee can claim **after-acquired property** received before discharge, by notice within **42 days** of learning of it."},{"q":"Eighteen months after Sara's bankruptcy, the trustee seeks sale of the family home where her teenage children live. What will the court presume?","o":["The creditors' interests outweigh all others, unless circumstances are exceptional","The children's needs come first","The spouse's interests outweigh the creditors'","That sale should be postponed until the children leave school"],"e":"**s.335A**: after **one year** the court must assume the **creditors' interests** outweigh everything else unless circumstances are **exceptional**. School disruption is not exceptional."},{"q":"An undischarged bankrupt seeks a loan. Above what amount must he disclose his bankruptcy?","o":["£500","£1,000","£250","£5,000"],"e":"**s.360**: obtaining credit of more than **£500** without disclosing bankruptcy is a **criminal offence**."},{"q":"After discharge from bankruptcy, which debt is the former bankrupt still liable for?","o":["A criminal fine","An unsecured credit card debt","An unpaid supplier's invoice","An unsecured bank overdraft"],"e":"Discharge releases bankruptcy debts **except** fraud debts, **fines**, personal injury damages, family orders and student loans (s.281)."}]},{"id":"blp/income-tax","name":"Business Law and Practice 13: Income tax: employees, sole traders, partners, shareholders and lenders","qs":[{"q":"What is the personal allowance for 2026/27?","o":["£12,570","£12,500","£13,000","£11,850"],"e":"**£12,570**, reduced by £1 for every £2 of income over £100,000."},{"q":"In what order are types of income taxed?","o":["Non-savings, savings, dividends","Dividends, savings, non-savings","Savings, non-savings, dividends","Non-savings, dividends, savings"],"e":"**Non-savings first, then savings, then dividends** on top."},{"q":"Which relief carries back a loss from the first 4 tax years against 3 previous years' income?","o":["s.72 early years relief","s.64 relief","s.83 carry forward","s.89 terminal loss relief"],"e":"**s.72**: carry back against total income of the previous 3 years, earliest first."},{"q":"What is the dividend allowance taxed at 0%?","o":["£500","£1,000","£2,000","£5,000"],"e":"The first **£500** of dividends is taxed at 0%; it is a band, not a deduction."},{"q":"Dev has adjusted net income of £110,000 in 2026/27. What is his personal allowance?","o":["£7,570","£12,570","£0","£2,570"],"e":"The **£12,570** allowance falls by **£1 for every £2** over £100,000: £10,000 excess ÷ 2 = £5,000, leaving **£7,570**."},{"q":"How much savings income does a higher rate taxpayer receive at 0% under the personal savings allowance?","o":["£500","£1,000","£0","£5,000"],"e":"**PSA**: **£1,000** for basic rate, **£500** for higher rate, **£0** for additional rate taxpayers. It still uses up the rate bands."},{"q":"An employee receives a £40,000 non-contractual compensation payment for loss of employment. How much is taxable as earnings?","o":["£10,000","£40,000","£0","£30,000"],"e":"A genuine **non-contractual** termination payment is **tax-free up to £30,000**; the excess (**£10,000**) is taxed as earnings. Contractual PILONs are taxed in full."},{"q":"A sole trader takes clients to dinner to win business. Is the cost deductible from trading profits?","o":["No: business entertainment is specifically not deductible","Yes: it is wholly and exclusively for the trade","Yes, up to £500 a year","Only half of it"],"e":"**Business entertainment** of clients is specifically **not deductible** (ITTOIA s.45), even if incurred for the trade."},{"q":"A sole trader has trading profits of £30,000 in 2026/27. How much Class 4 NIC does she pay?","o":["£1,045.80","£1,800.00","£1,394.40","£600.00"],"e":"Class 4 is **6%** on profits between **£12,570** and **£50,270**: (£30,000 − £12,570) × 6% = **£1,045.80**."},{"q":"A trader makes a loss in the final 12 months before ceasing to trade. Which relief carries it back against profits of the same trade of the previous 3 years, latest first?","o":["s.89 terminal loss relief","s.72 early years relief","s.83 carry forward","s.86 incorporation relief"],"e":"**s.89**: loss of the final 12 months set against profits of the **same trade** in the final year and **3 previous** years, **latest first**."},{"q":"Ann and Bo's firm makes £100,000. Ann gets a £20,000 \"salary\" and the rest is split equally. On how much is Ann taxed?","o":["£60,000","£40,000","£50,000","£20,000"],"e":"Partners' \"salaries\" are **not deductible**; they are part of the profit share. Ann: £20,000 + half of £80,000 = **£60,000**."},{"q":"When are a sole trader's two payments on account of income tax for 2026/27 due?","o":["31 January 2027 and 31 July 2027","31 January 2028 and 31 July 2028","5 October 2027 and 31 January 2028","31 July 2026 and 31 January 2027"],"e":"Payments on account are due **31 January in the tax year** and **31 July after it**; the **balancing payment** is due 31 January 2028."}]},{"id":"blp/cgt","name":"Business Law and Practice 14: Capital gains tax and business reliefs","qs":[{"q":"What is the CGT annual exempt amount for 2026/27?","o":["£3,000","£6,000","£12,300","£1,500"],"e":"The annual exempt amount is **£3,000**."},{"q":"What is the lifetime limit for Business Asset Disposal Relief?","o":["£1m","£10m","£500,000","£2.5m"],"e":"**BADR** applies to qualifying gains up to a **£1m** lifetime limit."},{"q":"What minimum shareholding must the seller hold for BADR on shares?","o":["5%","10%","25%","50%"],"e":"The company must be the seller's **personal company**: at least 5% of shares and votes, for 2 years."},{"q":"Within what period must proceeds be reinvested for rollover relief?","o":["1 year before to 3 years after","3 years before to 1 year after","6 months either side","2 years before to 2 years after"],"e":"**TCGA s.152**: from 1 year before to 3 years after the sale."},{"q":"A shareholder dies owning shares that have doubled in value. What CGT is payable on death?","o":["None, and the heir takes them at probate value","CGT on the gain, paid by the estate","CGT on the gain, paid by the heir on inheriting","CGT at 18% under BADR"],"e":"**Death is not a disposal**: no CGT is due and the heir takes the asset at **market value at death** (the tax-free uplift). IHT may apply instead."},{"q":"A man gives shares worth £50,000 (cost £10,000) to his wife, with whom he lives. What are the CGT consequences?","o":["No gain/no loss: she takes his £10,000 base cost","He is taxed on a £40,000 gain","She is taxed on receipt","He is taxed at market value but gets gift relief automatically"],"e":"Transfers between **spouses** living together are **no gain / no loss** (s.58); the recipient takes over the **original base cost**."},{"q":"A director holds 4.9% of the shares and votes in her trading company and has worked there for 10 years. She sells the shares. Is BADR available?","o":["No: she does not hold at least 5%","Yes: she is an officer for over 2 years","Yes, as it is a trading company","Only on half the gain"],"e":"BADR on shares needs a **personal company**: at least **5%** of shares and votes (plus economic interest), as an officer or employee, for **2 years**. 4.9% fails."},{"q":"Kate sells a workshop for £300,000 at a gain of £80,000 and buys a new workshop for £260,000 within a year. How much of the gain is taxed now?","o":["£40,000","£80,000","£0","£20,000"],"e":"Under **rollover relief**, proceeds **not reinvested** (£40,000) are taxed now, up to the whole gain. The other **£40,000** is rolled over into the new asset."},{"q":"Raj gives his daughter shares in an unquoted trading company that cost £10,000 and are worth £210,000. They make a joint s.165 election. What is her base cost?","o":["£10,000","£210,000","£200,000","£0"],"e":"**Gift relief** holds over the £200,000 gain: the recipient's base cost is market value less the held-over gain = **£10,000**."},{"q":"A sole trader transfers her business to a company for £300,000 of shares and a £100,000 loan account. Her gain is £120,000. How much is deferred under s.162?","o":["£90,000","£120,000","£30,000","£100,000"],"e":"Deferred gain = gain × shares ÷ total consideration = £120,000 × 300/400 = **£90,000**. The other £30,000 is taxed now."}]},{"id":"blp/corporation-tax","name":"Business Law and Practice 15: Corporation tax","qs":[{"q":"What is the main rate of corporation tax on profits over £250,000?","o":["25%","19%","20%","28%"],"e":"Main rate **25%**; small profits rate 19% up to £50,000, with marginal relief in between."},{"q":"When is corporation tax due for a company that is not large?","o":["9 months and 1 day after period end","12 months after period end","31 January after period end","3 months after period end"],"e":"Due **9 months and 1 day** after the accounting period ends; the CT600 is due 12 months after."},{"q":"What shareholding does the substantial shareholding exemption require?","o":["10% for 12 months","5% for 2 years","25% for 12 months","50% for 6 months"],"e":"**SSE**: at least 10% in a trading company held for 12 months."},{"q":"Under s.37, how far back can a company carry a trading loss?","o":["12 months","3 years","2 years","No carry back"],"e":"**s.37**: current period first, then back **12 months**. 3 years is only for terminal losses."},{"q":"A company with no associated companies has taxable total profits of £100,000 for the year to 31 March 2027. What is its corporation tax?","o":["£22,750","£25,000","£19,000","£26,500"],"e":"25% × £100,000 = £25,000, less marginal relief of 3/200 × (£250,000 − £100,000) = £2,250, giving **£22,750**."},{"q":"A company prepares accounts for an 18-month period. How is it taxed?","o":["As one 12-month accounting period and one 6-month period","As one 18-month accounting period","As two 9-month accounting periods","As one 12-month period, ignoring the rest"],"e":"An **accounting period can never exceed 12 months**: the first **12 months** form one AP and the **remainder** another."},{"q":"How does a company's chargeable gain differ from an individual's?","o":["Indexation (frozen at December 2017) but no annual exempt amount","An annual exempt amount but no indexation","Neither indexation nor any exemption, taxed at CGT rates","Indexation to the date of sale and an annual exempt amount"],"e":"Companies get **indexation frozen at December 2017** and **no annual exempt amount**; gains are taxed at the **corporation tax rate**."},{"q":"Which payment by an owner-managed company is NOT deductible in computing its taxable profits?","o":["A dividend to the shareholder","A salary to the director","Interest on a loan from the director","Rent for premises owned by the director"],"e":"**Dividends** are an appropriation of post-tax profit and are **not deductible**. Salary, interest and rent are deductible."},{"q":"A close company lends its director-shareholder £50,000 in June 2026 and it is still unpaid 9 months after the end of the accounting period. What is the s.455 charge?","o":["£17,875","£16,875","£12,500","£10,000"],"e":"For loans from 6 April 2026 the **s.455** charge is **35.75%**: £50,000 × 35.75% = **£17,875**, refunded when the loan is repaid."}]},{"id":"blp/vat-iht-bpr","name":"Business Law and Practice 16: VAT and inheritance tax business property relief","qs":[{"q":"What is the VAT registration threshold since April 2024?","o":["£90,000","£85,000","£88,000","£100,000"],"e":"Compulsory registration if taxable turnover exceeds **£90,000**. £88,000 is the deregistration threshold."},{"q":"What rate of BPR applies to unquoted shares in a trading company (within the allowance)?","o":["100%","50%","25%","75%"],"e":"Unquoted trading company shares get **100% BPR**, within the £2.5m allowance from 6 April 2026."},{"q":"What is the combined allowance for 100% BPR and APR per person from 6 April 2026?","o":["£2.5m","£1m","£5m","£325,000"],"e":"**£2.5m** per person, with 50% relief above it; unused allowance passes to a spouse."},{"q":"What is a taxable supply charged at 0% VAT called?","o":["Zero-rated supply","Exempt supply","Reduced-rate supply","Outside-the-scope supply"],"e":"A **zero-rated** supplier can recover input tax; an **exempt** supplier cannot."},{"q":"Jo's taxable turnover for the 12 months to the end of August 2026 first exceeds £90,000. By when must she notify HMRC, and from when is she registered?","o":["Notify by 30 September; registered from 1 October 2026","Notify by 31 August; registered from 1 September 2026","Notify by 31 October; registered from 1 November 2026","Notify by 5 April 2027; registered from 6 April 2027"],"e":"**Historic test**: notify within **30 days** of the month end; registration from the **first day of the second month** after the threshold was exceeded."},{"q":"On 10 May a business signs a £95,000 contract to be performed in the next 30 days alone. From when must it be VAT registered?","o":["10 May","1 June","1 July","9 June"],"e":"**Future test**: if taxable supplies in the **next 30 days alone** will exceed **£90,000**, registration runs from the **start** of that 30-day period."},{"q":"A standard-rated item is sold for £1,200 including VAT. How much of the price is VAT?","o":["£200","£240","£120","£180"],"e":"At 20%, the VAT element of a **VAT-inclusive** price is **1/6**: £1,200 ÷ 6 = **£200**."},{"q":"A sole trader sells her business as a going concern to a VAT-registered buyer who will carry on the same kind of business. Is VAT charged on the sale?","o":["No: a transfer of a going concern is neither a supply of goods nor services","Yes, at 20% on all assets","Yes, but only on the goodwill","Yes, at the reduced rate of 5%"],"e":"A **TOGC** to a buyer who is or becomes registered and carries on the same kind of business is **outside VAT**."},{"q":"Ana dies in October 2026 owning unquoted trading company shares worth £3m, with no lifetime gifts or transferred allowance. How much of their value is left chargeable after BPR?","o":["£250,000","£0","£500,000","£1,500,000"],"e":"First **£2.5m** at **100%**; the remaining £500,000 at **50%**, leaving **£250,000** chargeable."},{"q":"A partnership agreement obliges surviving partners to buy, and the estate to sell, a deceased partner's share. What is the BPR consequence?","o":["No BPR: it is a binding contract for sale","100% BPR still applies","50% BPR applies instead","BPR applies only after 2 years"],"e":"**s.113**: no BPR on property subject to a **binding contract for sale**. Agreements use **options** instead to keep the relief."}]}]},{"id":"contract","name":"Contract Law","topics":[{"id":"contract/offer-acceptance","name":"Contract Law 1: Agreement: offer and acceptance","qs":[{"q":"A shop displays a handbag priced at £50 on a shelf. A customer takes it to the till, but the cashier refuses to sell it. Was a contract formed when the customer picked it up?","o":["No: the display is an invitation to treat","Yes: the display was an offer accepted by picking it up","Yes: a price tag is a binding unilateral offer","No: shop sales need a written contract"],"e":"**Goods on display are an invitation to treat**: the customer makes the offer at the till, which the shop may refuse (Boots)."},{"q":"S offers to sell a horse for £1,000. B replies 'I'll pay £900.' S refuses, so B says 'I accept £1,000.' Is there a contract?","o":["No: the counter-offer destroyed the original offer","Yes: B accepted before the offer was revoked","Yes: the original offer stays open for a reasonable time","No: acceptance of an offer must be in writing"],"e":"A **counter-offer rejects and destroys** the original offer; a mere request for information would not (Hyde v Wrench)."},{"q":"When does a posted letter of acceptance usually take effect?","o":["When properly posted","When received by the offeror","When opened by the offeror","When the offeror reads it"],"e":"**Adams v Lindsell**: the postal rule makes acceptance effective on **posting**, if use of the post is reasonable."},{"q":"When is a postal revocation of an offer effective?","o":["On receipt by the offeree","On posting by the offeror","On the offeror signing it","After a reasonable time"],"e":"**Byrne v Van Tienhoven**: revocation must be **communicated**; the postal rule does not apply to revocation."},{"q":"A writes to B: 'If I hear no more, I shall consider the horse mine at £30.' B never replies. Is there a contract?","o":["No: silence cannot amount to acceptance","Yes: A waived communication of acceptance","Yes: silence is acceptance if the offeror says so","Yes: B had a reasonable time to reject"],"e":"**Silence is not acceptance**, even where the offeror says it will be (Felthouse v Bindley)."}]},{"id":"contract/consideration","name":"Contract Law 2: Consideration and promissory estoppel","qs":[{"q":"A agrees to sell his car to B in return for a peppercorn. Is there valid consideration?","o":["Yes: consideration must be sufficient, not adequate","No: consideration must be of roughly equal value","No: a token is only past consideration","Only if the contract is made by deed"],"e":"Consideration must be **sufficient but need not be adequate**; the court does not weigh the bargain (Chappell v Nestle: chocolate wrappers)."},{"q":"A builder running late is promised an extra £10,000 to finish on time, with no threats made. The client avoids a penalty under its own head contract. Is the promise binding?","o":["Yes: the practical benefit is good consideration","No: performing an existing duty is never consideration","No: the builder gave nothing new in return","Only if the promise was made by deed"],"e":"A **practical benefit**, obtained without duress or fraud, is good consideration for a promise to **pay more** (Williams v Roffey)."},{"q":"A creditor agrees to accept £5,000 in full settlement of a £10,000 debt now due. The debtor argues the creditor gained the practical benefit of prompt payment. Can the creditor sue for the balance?","o":["Yes: practical benefit does not apply to part payment of a debt","No: practical benefit is good consideration","No: an agreement to accept less always binds","Only if the creditor objected within 6 months"],"e":"Part payment of a debt is not consideration (**Foakes v Beer**); **practical benefit does not extend** to promises to accept less (Re Selectmove)."},{"q":"A man promises his ex-wife £100 a week maintenance; she gives nothing in return but relies on it. When he stops paying, can she sue him relying on promissory estoppel?","o":["No: promissory estoppel is a shield, not a sword","Yes: her reliance makes the promise enforceable","Yes: estoppel creates a new cause of action","Yes: estoppel replaces consideration in all contracts"],"e":"**Promissory estoppel cannot create a cause of action**; it only stops a promisor going back on strict rights (Combe v Combe)."}]},{"id":"contract/intention-certainty-capacity","name":"Contract Law 3: Intention, certainty and capacity","qs":[{"q":"A husband, living happily with his wife, promises her a monthly allowance. Is the promise presumed to be legally binding?","o":["No: domestic agreements are presumed not binding","Yes: all promises between spouses are binding","Yes: the commercial presumption applies to money","No: spouses lack capacity to contract together"],"e":"**Domestic and social agreements are presumed not to be intended as binding** (Balfour); the presumption is rebutted where spouses are separating (Merritt)."},{"q":"Two parties agree to 'negotiate in good faith' for the sale of a business, with no time limit. Is the agreement enforceable?","o":["No: an agreement to negotiate is too uncertain","Yes: the good faith duty makes it certain","Yes: it is an enforceable lock-out agreement","Only if the buyer has paid for the promise"],"e":"An **agreement to negotiate in good faith is unenforceable** for uncertainty; a lock-out for a **fixed period** can be enforceable (Walford v Miles)."},{"q":"A contract to buy a van 'on hire purchase terms' does not say what those terms are, and no trade custom or previous dealing fills the gap. Is there a binding contract?","o":["No: the term is too vague, so there is no contract","Yes: the court will imply reasonable terms","Yes: a reasonable price will be implied","Yes: the SGA 1979 supplies the missing terms"],"e":"Where an **essential term is too vague** and cannot be made certain, there is **no contract** (Scammell v Ouston)."},{"q":"What must a minor pay for necessaries under s.3 SGA 1979?","o":["A reasonable price","The contract price","Nothing at all","Cost price only"],"e":"**s.3 SGA 1979** (Nash v Inman): a **reasonable price** for necessaries suitable to their condition and needs."}]},{"id":"contract/privity-third-parties","name":"Contract Law 4: Privity and third party rights","qs":[{"q":"Under s.1(3) of the 1999 Act, how must a third party be identified?","o":["By name, class or description","By name only","By signature on the contract","By separate written notice"],"e":"**s.1(3)**: name, class or description in the contract; they need not exist when it is made."},{"q":"When does a third party's communicated assent under s.2 of the 1999 Act take effect?","o":["On receipt by the promisor","On posting","On signing the contract","On performance"],"e":"**s.2**: assent must be communicated to the promisor; the **postal rule does not apply**."},{"q":"What does s.136 LPA 1925 require for a legal assignment of contractual rights?","o":["Absolute, signed writing, written notice to debtor","Deed and consent of the debtor","Oral agreement and notice to debtor","Writing signed by the debtor"],"e":"**s.136 LPA 1925**: absolute, in writing signed by the assignor, with **written notice** to the debtor."},{"q":"A manufacturer sells tyres to a wholesaler, who resells them to a retailer under a contract in which the retailer promises not to sell below list price. The retailer undercuts. Ignoring the 1999 Act, can the manufacturer sue the retailer?","o":["No: it is not a party to that contract","Yes: it is the intended beneficiary","Yes: it supplied the goods originally","Yes, but only for nominal damages"],"e":"**Privity**: only a party to a contract can enforce it (Dunlop v Selfridge); the 1999 Act is the main statutory exception."}]},{"id":"contract/express-terms","name":"Contract Law 5: Express terms: incorporation, interpretation, classification and variation","qs":[{"q":"A guest books a hotel room at reception. A notice in the bedroom excludes liability for stolen property. Is the exclusion incorporated?","o":["No: the notice came after the contract was made","Yes: it was clearly displayed in the room","Yes: guests are bound by hotel rules","Yes: the guest stayed after seeing it"],"e":"Terms must be notified **before or at the time of contracting**; later notice is too late (Olley v Marlborough Court)."},{"q":"A term is not classified as a condition or a warranty. Its breach is serious but does not deprive the innocent party of substantially the whole benefit. What remedy is available?","o":["Damages only","Termination and damages","Termination only","Rescission from the start"],"e":"For an **innominate term**, termination is available only if the breach deprives the innocent party of **substantially the whole benefit**; otherwise damages only (Hong Kong Fir)."},{"q":"A contract says variations must be in signed writing. The parties later agree a variation orally. Is the variation effective?","o":["No: the no oral modification clause is valid","Yes: parties can always vary orally","Yes: the later agreement overrides the clause","Only if the variation benefits both parties"],"e":"A **no oral modification clause is effective**; an oral variation fails unless the clause's formalities are met (Rock Advertising v MWB)."},{"q":"An unsigned delivery note contains an unusual and very onerous term in small print. What is needed for that term to be incorporated?","o":["Especially prominent notice drawing attention to it","Ordinary reasonable notice, as for any term","Nothing: delivery notes are always contractual","Court approval of the term as reasonable"],"e":"The more **onerous or unusual** a term, the **more notice** is needed: the 'red hand' rule (J Spurling v Bradshaw; Interfoto)."}]},{"id":"contract/implied-terms","name":"Contract Law 6: Implied terms: common law and statute","qs":[{"q":"A jetty owner contracts to let a ship moor at its jetty, but the contract is silent on whether the berth is safe. Will a term be implied that the owner takes reasonable care over the berth's safety?","o":["Yes: it is necessary to give the contract business efficacy","No: courts never imply terms the parties did not write","No: terms can only be implied by statute","Yes: because it would be fair and reasonable"],"e":"A term is implied in fact if **necessary for business efficacy** (The Moorcock) or so obvious it goes without saying; mere reasonableness is not enough."},{"q":"Which SGA 1979 section implies that goods are of satisfactory quality?","o":["s.14(2)","s.13","s.14(3)","s.12"],"e":"**s.14(2)**, where the seller sells in the course of a business. s.13 is description, s.14(3) fitness, s.12 title."},{"q":"How long is the consumer's short-term right to reject goods under the CRA 2015?","o":["30 days","14 days","6 months","28 days"],"e":"**CRA 2015 s.22**: 30 days from delivery, with a full refund."},{"q":"Which CRA 2015 section requires a trader to perform a service with reasonable care and skill?","o":["s.49","s.9","s.51","s.55"],"e":"**s.49**. s.51 is reasonable price; s.55 is repeat performance; s.9 is satisfactory quality of goods."}]},{"id":"contract/exemption-clauses","name":"Contract Law 7: Exemption clauses and unfair terms (UCTA 1977 and CRA 2015 Part 2)","qs":[{"q":"Under UCTA 1977 s.2(1), what is the effect of excluding liability for negligently caused death?","o":["Void","Subject to reasonableness","Valid if incorporated","Valid between businesses only"],"e":"**UCTA s.2(1)**: liability for death or personal injury from negligence cannot be excluded."},{"q":"Under UCTA s.11, when is the reasonableness of a term judged?","o":["When the contract was made","When the breach occurred","When the claim is issued","At trial"],"e":"**s.11(1)**: judged at the time of contracting, and the burden is on the party relying on the clause."},{"q":"Which CRA 2015 section contains the fairness test for consumer contract terms?","o":["s.62","s.65","s.31","s.71"],"e":"**s.62**: unfair if, contrary to good faith, it causes a significant imbalance to the consumer's detriment."},{"q":"An ambiguous clause limits (rather than wholly excludes) liability to £1,000. How strictly will contra proferentem be applied to it?","o":["Less strictly than to a total exclusion clause","More strictly than to a total exclusion clause","Exactly as strictly as to any exclusion clause","Not at all: limitation clauses are always valid"],"e":"**Limitation clauses** are construed **less strictly** than total exclusion clauses (Ailsa Craig)."}]},{"id":"contract/misrepresentation","name":"Contract Law 8: Misrepresentation","qs":[{"q":"A seller of farmland never used for sheep honestly tells the buyer it could carry 2,000 sheep. It cannot. Is this an actionable misrepresentation?","o":["No: it is an honest opinion, not a statement of fact","Yes: any false statement inducing a contract is actionable","Yes: the seller should have checked the capacity","No: statements about land are never actionable"],"e":"An honest **opinion** by someone with **no special knowledge** is not a statement of fact (Bisset v Wilkinson); an expert's opinion may be (Esso v Mardon)."},{"q":"A claimant proves a negligent misrepresentation under s.2(1) Misrepresentation Act 1967. How are damages assessed?","o":["As for fraud: all direct loss, even if unforeseeable","As for breach of contract: expectation loss","As for negligence: foreseeable loss only","At the court's discretion in lieu of rescission"],"e":"The **fiction of fraud** in s.2(1) means the **deceit measure** applies: all direct loss (Royscot Trust v Rogerson). Discretionary damages in lieu are s.2(2)."},{"q":"A seller of a medical practice truthfully states its income in January. By signing in May, income has collapsed, but the seller says nothing. Is there a misrepresentation?","o":["Yes: a statement that becomes false must be corrected","No: the statement was true when made","No: silence is never a misrepresentation","Only if the buyer asked again before signing"],"e":"A statement **true when made but falsified by a change of circumstances** before the contract must be corrected (With v O'Flanagan)."},{"q":"Five years after buying a painting, a buyer discovers the seller's innocent statement that it was by Constable was false. Can the buyer rescind?","o":["No: lapse of time bars rescission","Yes: time runs only from discovery","Yes: innocent misrepresentation never bars rescission","Yes, if the painting is returned undamaged"],"e":"For non-fraudulent misrepresentation, **lapse of time** runs from the **contract**, not discovery; five years was too long (Leaf v International Galleries)."}]},{"id":"contract/mistake-duress-ui-illegality","name":"Contract Law 9: Mistake, duress, undue influence and illegality","qs":[{"q":"What is the effect on a contract of an operative mistake?","o":["Void","Voidable","Valid but unenforceable","Discharged by frustration"],"e":"Mistake makes a contract **void**, so third parties get no title. Duress and undue influence make it voidable."},{"q":"A rogue signs a written hire purchase agreement in the name of a real person whose identity he has stolen. What is the effect on the written agreement?","o":["Void: the hirer is the person named in it","Voidable for fraud until rescinded","Valid: the finance company dealt with the rogue","Valid but unenforceable against the rogue"],"e":"In a **written contract**, the other party is the **person named**, so the agreement with the rogue is **void** (Shogun Finance); face-to-face deals are presumed voidable only (Lewis v Averay)."},{"q":"A supplier, without lawful justification, threatens to stop deliveries unless the buyer agrees to a price rise. The buyer agrees. What must the buyer prove for economic duress?","o":["Illegitimate pressure that was a significant cause of agreeing","Any commercial pressure at all","That the threat was to commit a crime","That the contract was at an undervalue"],"e":"Economic duress: **illegitimate pressure** that was a **significant cause** of entering the contract, leaving no practical choice (DSND Subsea)."},{"q":"A wife guarantees her husband's business debts to a bank. What must the bank normally do to avoid being affected by any undue influence by the husband?","o":["Ensure she receives independent legal advice","Nothing: guarantees are always enforceable","Ensure she signs before a magistrate","Lend no more than half the property's value"],"e":"In a non-commercial surety case the bank is **put on inquiry** and must take reasonable steps, chiefly getting a **solicitor's confirmation** that she was advised (RBS v Etridge (No 2))."}]},{"id":"contract/discharge-frustration","name":"Contract Law 10: Termination: performance, breach and frustration","qs":[{"q":"A decorator completes a £750 job, but the work has minor defects costing £55 to put right. What can the decorator recover?","o":["£695: the price less the cost of remedying defects","Nothing: performance must be exact","£750: minor defects are irrelevant","Only a reasonable sum on a quantum meruit"],"e":"**Substantial performance**: the price less the cost of remedying defects (Hoenig v Isaacs); not where defects are serious (Bolton v Mahadeva)."},{"q":"A seller must ship goods from Sudan to Hamburg. The Suez Canal closes, so the voyage round the Cape is twice as long and much costlier. Is the contract frustrated?","o":["No: more onerous or costly performance is not frustration","Yes: the usual route is impossible","Yes: the costs have doubled","Yes: delay always frustrates a sale of goods"],"e":"Frustration needs a **radically different** obligation; a **longer, costlier route** is not enough (Tsakiroglou)."},{"q":"A flat is hired for two days solely, as both parties know, to watch a royal procession. The procession is cancelled. Is the contract frustrated?","o":["Yes: the foundation of the contract has gone","No: the flat can still be used","No: cancellation was foreseeable","Only if the hirer has not yet paid"],"e":"A contract is frustrated where the **common purpose** forming its **foundation** is destroyed (Krell v Henry); contrast Herne Bay, where some purpose remained."},{"q":"Under the 1943 Act, up to what limit can a payee keep money for expenses?","o":["Sums paid or payable before frustration","The full value of expenses","The value of any benefit conferred","Half the contract price"],"e":"**s.1(2)**: expenses only up to sums paid or payable before the frustrating event."}]},{"id":"contract/damages","name":"Contract Law 11: Remedies: damages, remoteness, mitigation and penalties","qs":[{"q":"A carrier delays delivery of a mill's broken crankshaft. It did not know the mill had no spare and would stand idle. Can the mill recover its lost profits?","o":["No: the loss was not within the parties' reasonable contemplation","Yes: all losses caused by breach are recoverable","Yes: lost profit is always a natural loss","Only if the carrier was negligent"],"e":"**Remoteness**: loss must arise **naturally** or be within **both parties' contemplation** from special facts made known (Hadley v Baxendale)."},{"q":"A builder makes a pool six inches shallower than agreed. It is safe and its value is unaffected, but rebuilding would cost £21,000. What will the court award?","o":["A modest sum for loss of amenity","The full £21,000 cost of cure","Nothing, as there is no loss of value","A refund of the whole contract price"],"e":"Cost of cure is refused if **wholly disproportionate** to the benefit; a **loss of amenity** award may be made instead (Ruxley v Forsyth)."},{"q":"A clause requires a party in breach to pay a fixed sum. When is it an unenforceable penalty?","o":["When the detriment is out of all proportion to the innocent party's legitimate interest","Whenever the sum exceeds the actual loss","Whenever the contract calls it a penalty","Whenever it is not a precise estimate of loss"],"e":"Modern test: a secondary obligation imposing a **detriment out of all proportion** to any **legitimate interest** of the innocent party (Cavendish v Makdessi; ParkingEye)."},{"q":"Who bears the burden of proving a failure to mitigate loss?","o":["The defendant","The claimant","The court","Both parties equally"],"e":"The **defendant** must prove the claimant failed to take reasonable steps to mitigate."}]},{"id":"contract/equitable-remedies-guarantees","name":"Contract Law 12: Specific performance, injunctions, indemnities and guarantees","qs":[{"q":"A supermarket tenant covenants to keep trading but closes its store. Will the court order specific performance to keep it trading?","o":["No: it would require constant supervision","Yes: damages are inadequate for landlords","Yes: specific performance is available as of right","No: leases can never be specifically enforced"],"e":"Courts will not order a business to be **carried on**, as it would need **constant supervision** (Co-operative Insurance v Argyll)."},{"q":"Which statute requires a guarantee to be evidenced in signed writing?","o":["Statute of Frauds 1677 s.4","LP(MP)A 1989 s.2","Law of Property Act 1925 s.136","Sale of Goods Act 1979 s.52"],"e":"**Statute of Frauds 1677 s.4**. An indemnity needs no writing."},{"q":"A lender and borrower agree a substantial increase in the loan without the guarantor's consent. What is the effect on the guarantee?","o":["The guarantor is discharged","The guarantor remains liable in full","The guarantor becomes liable for the increase too","The guarantee becomes an indemnity"],"e":"A **material variation** of the principal contract **without the guarantor's consent** discharges them, unless plainly insubstantial or beneficial (Holme v Brunskill)."},{"q":"A claimant seeks an interim injunction before trial. What must it first show?","o":["A serious question to be tried","A strong prima facie case","That it will certainly win at trial","That loss has already been suffered"],"e":"**Serious question to be tried**, then adequacy of damages and the **balance of convenience** (American Cyanamid)."}]}]},{"id":"crimlaw","name":"Criminal Liability","topics":[{"id":"crimlaw/actus-reus","name":"Criminal Liability 1: Actus reus: acts, omissions and causation","qs":[{"q":"A level-crossing keeper employed to shut the gate leaves it open at lunch, and a cart is hit by a train. What is the basis of his liability for failing to act?","o":["A contractual duty to act","A duty from creating the danger","A duty from a family relationship","No liability: omissions never count"],"e":"A **contractual duty** can found omission liability where others rely on it being performed (R v Pittwood)."},{"q":"Under R v Miller, what must D do after accidentally creating a dangerous situation he becomes aware of?","o":["Take reasonable steps to deal with it","Nothing, as there is no duty to act","Remove every possible risk of harm","Only inform the owner of the property"],"e":"Creating a danger gives a **duty to take reasonable steps** once D realises it (cigarette on the mattress)."},{"q":"D stabs V, who refuses a blood transfusion on religious grounds and dies. Does V's refusal break the chain of causation?","o":["No: D must take the victim as found","Yes: the refusal was unreasonable","Yes: it was a free, voluntary act","Only if the refusal was foreseeable"],"e":"Under the **thin skull rule** D takes the victim as found, including religious beliefs (R v Blaue)."},{"q":"In R v Kennedy (No 2), why was the drug supplier not liable for manslaughter?","o":["V freely and knowingly self-injected","The drug was not a controlled drug","Medical treatment broke the chain","D had no duty of care"],"e":"A **free, deliberate and informed act** of V (self-injection) breaks the chain of causation."},{"q":"Which test decides factual causation?","o":["The 'but for' test","The operating and substantial cause test","The thin skull rule","The 'daft' reaction test"],"e":"Factual cause uses the **'but for' test** (R v White). 'Operating and substantial' is the test for **legal** causation."}]},{"id":"crimlaw/mens-rea","name":"Criminal Liability 2: Mens rea: intention, recklessness, negligence and strict liability","qs":[{"q":"Under R v Woollin, how likely must the result be for a jury to find oblique intention?","o":["A virtual certainty","Highly probable","Likely","A real and substantial risk"],"e":"The result must be a **virtual certainty** and D must **appreciate** that. 'Highly probable' is not enough."},{"q":"D, aged 11, sets fire to papers without realising the fire could spread, though a reasonable adult would have seen the risk. Is D reckless as to the damage?","o":["No: D must be aware of the risk","Yes: the risk was obvious","Yes: an objective test applies","Only if D was over 14"],"e":"Recklessness is **subjective**: D must be **aware of the risk** and unreasonably take it (R v G, overruling Caldwell)."},{"q":"D throws a stone at people, misses, and breaks a window he never foresaw hitting. Can his mens rea for assault be transferred to criminal damage?","o":["No: malice transfers only within the same offence","Yes: transferred malice always applies","Yes: if the damage was likely","Yes: an intent to harm covers any harm"],"e":"**Transferred malice** works only where the harm is of the **same kind** as that intended (R v Pembliton)."},{"q":"D accidentally drives onto a police officer's foot, then refuses to move when told. How can actus reus and mens rea coincide?","o":["The act continues until D forms mens rea","They cannot: the act was complete at once","Only if D intended it from the start","Only if the officer was injured"],"e":"A **continuing act**: the actus reus continued while D left the car there, so it coincided with his later mens rea (Fagan v MPC)."}]},{"id":"crimlaw/non-fatal-offences","name":"Criminal Liability 3: Non-fatal offences against the person","qs":[{"q":"What mens rea is required for assault occasioning ABH under s.47 OAPA 1861?","o":["Only the mens rea for the assault or battery","Intention to cause ABH","Foresight of ABH","Intention to cause GBH"],"e":"**R v Savage; R v Roberts**: no foresight of ABH is needed, only the MR of the **assault or battery**."},{"q":"For s.20 OAPA 1861, what must D have intended or foreseen?","o":["Some harm, even minor","Wounding or GBH","Really serious harm","Death"],"e":"**R v Mowatt; R v Parmenter**: 'maliciously' means foresight of **some harm**; D need not foresee GBH."},{"q":"What is a 'wound' for the purposes of ss.18 and 20 OAPA 1861?","o":["A break in both layers of the skin","Any injury causing bleeding","Internal bleeding","A broken bone"],"e":"**JCC v Eisenhower**: both layers of skin must be broken. Internal bleeding or a fracture alone is not a wound."},{"q":"What is the maximum sentence for s.18 wounding or causing GBH with intent?","o":["Life","5 years","10 years","14 years"],"e":"s.18 is **indictable only** with a maximum of **life**; s.20 and s.47 carry 5 years."},{"q":"V asks D to whip him during sexual activity, and D causes ABH. Is V's consent a defence?","o":["No: consent is no defence to ABH or worse","Yes: an adult's consent is always a defence","Yes: if V asked for it in advance","Only if the injury was not permanent"],"e":"Consent is **no defence to ABH or above** unless a recognised exception applies, e.g. sport, surgery or tattooing (R v Brown)."}]},{"id":"crimlaw/theft","name":"Criminal Liability 4: Theft (Theft Act 1968 ss.1-6)","qs":[{"q":"What is the maximum sentence for theft?","o":["7 years","10 years","5 years","14 years"],"e":"Theft is **either way** with a maximum of **7 years**."},{"q":"D genuinely believes his conduct is honest, but ordinary decent people would regard it as dishonest. Is D dishonest for theft?","o":["Yes: the standard is objective","No: his genuine belief is enough","No: he must realise others see it as dishonest","Only if he knew it was illegal"],"e":"**Ivey** test: find D's actual knowledge or belief, then apply the **objective standards of ordinary decent people**; D need not realise it is dishonest (R v Barton and Booth)."},{"q":"Which of these cannot be stolen under s.4 Theft Act 1968?","o":["Confidential information","A bank credit balance","A mobile phone","Cash"],"e":"**Oxford v Moss**: confidential information is not property. Bank credit is a **thing in action** and can be stolen."},{"q":"Under s.2(1)(b), D is not dishonest if he believed what?","o":["The owner would consent if he knew the circumstances","He would pay for the property later","Ordinary people would not think it dishonest","He was owed a moral debt"],"e":"s.2(1)(b) covers belief in the **owner's consent**. Willingness to pay does not prevent dishonesty (s.2(2))."},{"q":"D borrows a cinema's films, copies them overnight, and returns them unharmed. Does D intend to permanently deprive under s.6?","o":["No: their goodness or virtue was not exhausted","Yes: any unauthorised borrowing is enough","Yes: because he made a profit","Only if the cinema lost takings"],"e":"Borrowing counts under **s.6** only if equivalent to an outright taking, i.e. the thing's **goodness or virtue** is gone (R v Lloyd)."}]},{"id":"crimlaw/robbery-burglary","name":"Criminal Liability 5: Robbery, burglary and aggravated burglary","qs":[{"q":"What is the maximum sentence for robbery?","o":["Life","14 years","10 years","7 years"],"e":"Robbery (s.8) is **indictable only** with a maximum of **life**."},{"q":"Which ulterior intent appears in s.9(1)(a) burglary but not as an act under s.9(1)(b)?","o":["Doing unlawful damage","Stealing","Inflicting GBH","Attempting to steal"],"e":"**Unlawful damage** is only an ulterior intent under **s.9(1)(a)**; (1)(b) covers theft and GBH only."},{"q":"What is the maximum sentence for burglary of a dwelling?","o":["14 years","10 years","Life","7 years"],"e":"Burglary of a **dwelling**: **14 years**; otherwise 10 years. Aggravated burglary carries life."},{"q":"For aggravated burglary based on s.9(1)(b), when must D have the weapon with him?","o":["When stealing or inflicting GBH","At the time of entry","When leaving the building","At any point that day"],"e":"**R v O'Leary**: for (1)(b) the time is the **theft or GBH**, so a knife picked up inside counts."}]},{"id":"crimlaw/criminal-damage","name":"Criminal Liability 6: Criminal damage and arson (Criminal Damage Act 1971)","qs":[{"q":"At or below what value of damage is criminal damage tried summarily under s.22 MCA 1980?","o":["£5,000","£200","£10,000","£2,000"],"e":"Damage of **£5,000 or less** is tried summarily with no right to elect (max 3 months). Not for arson."},{"q":"D, drunk, breaks a window of a house she honestly believes is her friend's, believing the friend would consent. Can she rely on lawful excuse under s.5(2)(a) CDA 1971?","o":["Yes: an honest belief suffices, even if drunk","No: voluntary intoxication is no defence","No: the belief must be reasonable","Only if the owner really would consent"],"e":"The s.5 belief need only be **honestly held**, even if caused by voluntary intoxication (Jaggard v Dickinson)."},{"q":"Under R v Steer, the danger to life in s.1(2) must come from what?","o":["The damage itself","The act causing the damage","The value of the property","The defendant's own property"],"e":"In **Steer** the danger came from the bullets, not the broken window, so s.1(2) failed."},{"q":"To which offence does the s.5 lawful excuse defence apply?","o":["Simple criminal damage under s.1(1)","Aggravated criminal damage under s.1(2)","Aggravated arson","Every Criminal Damage Act offence"],"e":"Lawful excuse under s.5 applies to **s.1(1) only**, not to aggravated (life-endangering) damage."}]},{"id":"crimlaw/murder","name":"Criminal Liability 7: Murder","qs":[{"q":"What is 'implied malice' for murder?","o":["Intention to cause GBH","Foresight of probable death","Recklessness as to death","Intention to cause ABH"],"e":"Malice aforethought is intention to kill (express) or **intention to cause GBH** (implied: R v Vickers)."},{"q":"D kills V because a gang credibly threatens to kill D's child unless he does. Can D rely on duress?","o":["No: duress is never a defence to murder","Yes: the threat was of death","Yes: if a reasonable person would yield","It reduces murder to manslaughter"],"e":"Duress is **no defence to murder**, nor to attempted murder (R v Howe; R v Gotts)."},{"q":"What sentence must be imposed on an adult convicted of murder?","o":["Mandatory life","Discretionary life","Minimum of 15 years","Extended determinate sentence"],"e":"Murder carries a **mandatory life sentence**, with a minimum term set by Schedule 21."},{"q":"AG's consent is needed to prosecute for murder where death occurs more than how long after the injury?","o":["3 years","A year and a day","5 years","12 months"],"e":"The year and a day rule was abolished in 1996; **3 years** now triggers the **AG's consent** requirement."}]},{"id":"crimlaw/voluntary-manslaughter","name":"Criminal Liability 8: Voluntary manslaughter: loss of control and diminished responsibility","qs":[{"q":"Who bears the burden of proving diminished responsibility, and to what standard?","o":["D, on the balance of probabilities","Prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt","D, beyond reasonable doubt","D, evidential burden only"],"e":"**s.2(2) Homicide Act 1957**: D proves DR on the **balance of probabilities**. Loss of control is disproved by the prosecution."},{"q":"D kills his wife after finding out about her affair and her taunts that he cannot cope. How is the infidelity treated for loss of control?","o":["Disregarded as a trigger, but may be context","A qualifying trigger on its own","Wholly irrelevant to the defence","An automatic bar to the defence"],"e":"s.55(6)(c): **sexual infidelity** is disregarded as a trigger itself but can be **context** for another trigger (R v Clinton)."},{"q":"What excludes the loss of control defence under s.54(4) Coroners and Justice Act 2009?","o":["A considered desire for revenge","A loss of control that was not sudden","Fear of serious violence","Things said rather than done"],"e":"**s.54(4)** excludes acting in a **considered desire for revenge**; the loss need not be sudden (s.54(2))."},{"q":"For diminished responsibility, when is D's ability 'substantially impaired'?","o":["When impaired to an important or weighty degree","When impaired more than trivially","Only when totally destroyed","When a reasonable person would think so"],"e":"'Substantially' means **important or weighty**, not merely more than trivial (R v Golds)."}]},{"id":"crimlaw/involuntary-manslaughter","name":"Criminal Liability 9: Involuntary manslaughter: unlawful act and gross negligence","qs":[{"q":"For unlawful act manslaughter, how is it decided whether D's unlawful act was dangerous?","o":["Sober, reasonable people would see a risk of some harm","D himself foresaw a risk of harm","Sober, reasonable people would foresee death","Sober, reasonable people would foresee serious harm"],"e":"Dangerousness is **objective**: all sober and reasonable people would recognise a risk of **some harm** (R v Church)."},{"q":"In gross negligence manslaughter, what must there be a serious and obvious risk of?","o":["Death","Serious injury","Some harm","Any physical injury"],"e":"GNM needs an obvious risk of **death** (Misra and Srivastava); UAM needs only risk of **some harm**."},{"q":"A father wilfully neglects his baby, who dies. Can his neglect be the basis of unlawful act manslaughter?","o":["No: UAM needs a positive act","Yes: wilful neglect is an unlawful act","Yes: if death was foreseeable","Only if he intended harm"],"e":"Unlawful act manslaughter needs an unlawful **act**, not an omission; omissions are dealt with as **gross negligence manslaughter** (R v Lowe)."},{"q":"An anaesthetist fails to notice a disconnected oxygen tube and the patient dies. Beyond duty, breach and causation, what must the jury find for gross negligence manslaughter?","o":["The negligence was so bad it was criminal","D foresaw a risk of death","D's act was unlawful and dangerous","D intended serious harm"],"e":"Duty, breach causing death, and negligence so **gross** that the jury consider it **criminal** (R v Adomako)."}]},{"id":"crimlaw/fraud","name":"Criminal Liability 10: Fraud (Fraud Act 2006)","qs":[{"q":"What is the maximum sentence for fraud under the Fraud Act 2006?","o":["10 years","7 years","14 years","5 years"],"e":"Fraud under **s.1** is either way with a maximum of **10 years**."},{"q":"Which section of the Fraud Act 2006 covers fraud by failing to disclose information?","o":["Section 3","Section 2","Section 4","Section 11"],"e":"**s.3** requires a **legal duty** to disclose. s.2 is false representation, s.4 abuse of position."},{"q":"Which of these is a result offence that needs services actually to be obtained?","o":["Obtaining services dishonestly (s.11)","Fraud by false representation (s.2)","Fraud by abuse of position (s.4)","Fraud by failing to disclose (s.3)"],"e":"**s.11** is a **result** offence; ss.2-4 are conduct crimes needing no actual gain or loss."},{"q":"Under s.5 Fraud Act 2006, gain and loss are limited to what?","o":["Money or other property","Any advantage at all","Services only","Money only"],"e":"s.5 confines gain and loss to **money or other property**, temporary or permanent."}]},{"id":"crimlaw/general-defences","name":"Criminal Liability 11: General defences: intoxication and self-defence","qs":[{"q":"D gets voluntarily drunk and punches V, causing ABH, without forming any intent or foresight. Can D rely on intoxication?","o":["No: no defence to a basic intent offence","Yes: he lacked the mens rea","Yes: if he was very drunk","Only if he rarely drinks"],"e":"Voluntary intoxication is **no defence** to **basic intent** offences such as s.47 ABH (DPP v Majewski)."},{"q":"D decides to kill V, drinks a bottle of whisky for courage, and kills V while very drunk. Can intoxication help D?","o":["No: he formed the intent before drinking","Yes: he lacked intent at the time","Yes: murder is a specific intent offence","It reduces murder to manslaughter"],"e":"**Dutch courage**: where intent is formed before drinking, intoxication gives no defence (A-G for NI v Gallagher)."},{"q":"D, sober, honestly but unreasonably believes V is attacking someone and uses force. On what facts is self-defence judged?","o":["The facts as D honestly believed them","The facts as a reasonable person would see them","The actual facts","The facts as V believed them"],"e":"**s.76(3)-(4) CJIA 2008**: D is judged on the facts as he **honestly believed** them; unreasonableness is only evidence (R v Gladstone Williams)."},{"q":"In a householder case, what degree of force is never reasonable?","o":["Grossly disproportionate force","Disproportionate force","Pre-emptive force","Force used without retreating"],"e":"**s.76(5A)**: **grossly disproportionate** force is never reasonable; merely disproportionate force may be."},{"q":"Which provision stops D relying on a mistaken belief in self-defence caused by voluntary intoxication?","o":["s.76(5) CJIA 2008","s.3 Criminal Law Act 1967","s.5 Criminal Damage Act 1971","s.54 Coroners and Justice Act 2009"],"e":"**s.76(5) CJIA 2008** (codifying O'Grady and Hatton) excludes voluntarily intoxicated mistakes."}]},{"id":"crimlaw/parties","name":"Criminal Liability 12: Parties to crime: principals and accessories","qs":[{"q":"D and P set out to burgle; D foresees P might use a knife but does not intend to assist any violence. P kills. Is D's foresight enough for him to be an accessory to murder?","o":["No: D must intend to assist or encourage","Yes: foresight of a possibility suffices","Yes: joint enterprise makes D liable","Only if D knew of the knife"],"e":"The accessory must **intend to assist or encourage** the crime; foresight is only **evidence** of intent (R v Jogee)."},{"q":"Which statute makes accessories to indictable offences liable to be tried and punished as principals?","o":["Accessories and Abettors Act 1861 s.8","Magistrates' Courts Act 1980 s.44","Criminal Attempts Act 1981 s.1","Criminal Law Act 1967 s.3"],"e":"**s.8 AAA 1861** covers indictable offences; s.44 MCA 1980 covers summary ones."},{"q":"During a burglary, D shouts 'come on, let's go' and leaves just before P stabs the householder. Has D effectively withdrawn?","o":["No: it was too late and not enough","Yes: he communicated withdrawal","Yes: he left before the stabbing","Only if P heard him"],"e":"Withdrawal must be **timely and unequivocal**; once violence is imminent, words and leaving may not be enough (R v Becerra)."},{"q":"What does 'procuring' an offence require?","o":["A causal link to the offence","A meeting of minds","Presence at the scene","A prior agreement"],"e":"**A-G's Ref (No 1 of 1975)**: procuring needs **causation** but no meeting of minds (spiked drink)."}]},{"id":"crimlaw/attempts","name":"Criminal Liability 13: Attempts (Criminal Attempts Act 1981)","qs":[{"q":"What is the actus reus test for an attempt under s.1(1) Criminal Attempts Act 1981?","o":["An act more than merely preparatory","The last act before the offence","A substantial step towards the offence","Reaching the point of no return"],"e":"The act must be **more than merely preparatory**: D has 'embarked on the crime proper' (R v Gullefer)."},{"q":"What mens rea is required for attempted murder?","o":["Intention to kill","Intention to cause GBH","Recklessness as to death","Foresight of death as likely"],"e":"**R v Whybrow**: only an **intention to kill** will do, even though intent to cause GBH suffices for murder."},{"q":"D takes delivery of a package he believes is heroin to sell, but it is harmless vegetable matter. Can D be guilty of attempt?","o":["Yes: impossibility is no defence","No: the full offence was impossible","No: no drugs were involved","Only if he had dealt before"],"e":"**s.1(2)-(3) Criminal Attempts Act 1981**: D is judged on the facts as he believed them; **impossibility** is no defence (R v Shivpuri)."},{"q":"Which offences cannot be attempted under the Criminal Attempts Act 1981?","o":["Summary-only offences","Either-way offences","Indictable-only offences","Offences of dishonesty"],"e":"s.1 applies only to offences triable on **indictment**, so there is no attempted **summary-only** offence."}]}]},{"id":"crimprac","name":"Criminal Law and Practice","topics":[{"id":"crimprac/police-station-detention","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 1: Police station: rights and detention limits","qs":[{"q":"What minimum rank must authorise delaying a suspect's right to legal advice?","o":["Superintendent","Inspector","Sergeant","Chief constable"],"e":"Delay to **legal advice (s.58)** needs a **superintendent**; delay to having someone informed (s.56) needs an inspector."},{"q":"When must the first review of detention take place?","o":["Within 6 hours of detention being authorised","Within 6 hours of arrival","Within 9 hours of arrest","Within 24 hours of arrival"],"e":"The first review is within **6 hours of authorisation**, then at intervals of up to **9 hours**."},{"q":"What is the basic maximum period of detention without charge under s.41 PACE?","o":["24 hours","12 hours","36 hours","48 hours"],"e":"**24 hours** from the relevant time; a superintendent may extend to **36** for an indictable offence."},{"q":"What is the overall maximum detention without charge with a warrant of further detention (non-terrorism)?","o":["96 hours","36 hours","72 hours","14 days"],"e":"Magistrates can issue and extend warrants to a total of **96 hours** (ss.43-44). 14 days is for terrorism."}]},{"id":"crimprac/police-interview","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 2: Police interviews: advising the client, silence and vulnerable suspects","qs":[{"q":"Which section of the CJPOA 1994 allows inferences from failing to mention facts when questioned?","o":["s.34","s.35","s.36","s.37"],"e":"**s.34** covers failing to mention a fact later relied on; s.35 is silence at trial."},{"q":"How much continuous rest must a detainee have in any 24-hour period?","o":["8 hours","6 hours","10 hours","12 hours"],"e":"Code C requires a continuous **8 hours' rest**, normally at night."},{"q":"Who normally decides whether a solicitor should be removed from an interview?","o":["A superintendent","The custody officer","The interviewing officer","A sergeant"],"e":"A **superintendent** decides (an inspector only if none is readily available)."},{"q":"Up to what age is a suspect a juvenile needing an appropriate adult?","o":["Under 18","Under 16","Under 17","Under 14"],"e":"An **appropriate adult** is required for any juvenile **under 18**."}]},{"id":"crimprac/identification","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 3: Identification procedures (PACE Code D)","qs":[{"q":"Which identification procedure is normally the first choice under PACE Code D?","o":["Video identification","Identification parade","Group identification","Confrontation"],"e":"**Video identification** is the normal first choice; **confrontation** is the last resort."},{"q":"How many other people must appear alongside the suspect in a video identification?","o":["At least eight","At least five","At least six","At least twelve"],"e":"At least **eight** others who **resemble** the suspect."},{"q":"What is the minimum rank of the identification officer?","o":["Inspector","Sergeant","Superintendent","Chief inspector"],"e":"An **inspector** or above who is **not involved** in the investigation."},{"q":"Under which provision may evidence be excluded for a breach of Code D?","o":["s.78 PACE","s.76 PACE","s.58 PACE","s.34 CJPOA 1994"],"e":"**s.78 PACE** (adverse effect on fairness); exclusion is **not automatic** and a jury direction may suffice."}]},{"id":"crimprac/bail","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 4: Bail (Bail Act 1976)","qs":[{"q":"Which section of the Bail Act 1976 creates the general right to bail?","o":["Section 4","Section 3","Section 6","Section 7"],"e":"**s.4** gives the right to bail; s.3 deals with conditions; s.6 with failing to surrender."},{"q":"Within what time must the prosecution give written notice of appeal against a grant of bail?","o":["2 hours","24 hours","48 hours","1 hour"],"e":"Oral notice at the end of the hearing, then **written notice within 2 hours**; hearing within 48 hours."},{"q":"What offence does a defendant commit by failing to surrender to court bail without reasonable cause?","o":["s.6 Bail Act 1976","s.7 Bail Act 1976","s.3 Bail Act 1976","s.25 CJPOA 1994"],"e":"Absconding is a **s.6** offence. Breaching a condition is **not** an offence: D is arrested under s.7."},{"q":"Who may grant bail to a defendant charged with murder?","o":["A Crown Court judge","The magistrates' court","The custody officer","A youth court"],"e":"In murder cases only a **Crown Court judge** can grant bail."},{"q":"Where does the defence appeal against a magistrates' refusal of bail?","o":["The Crown Court","The High Court","The Court of Appeal","The Supreme Court"],"e":"To the **Crown Court** (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.81), with a **certificate of full argument**."}]},{"id":"crimprac/first-hearings","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 5: First hearings, funding and classification of offences","qs":[{"q":"What is the maximum guilty plea reduction if a plea is indicated at the first stage of proceedings?","o":["One third","One quarter","One tenth","One half"],"e":"**One third** at the first stage; it then drops to one quarter and to one tenth on the first day of trial."},{"q":"In which court is the interests of justice test for legal aid automatically satisfied?","o":["The Crown Court","The magistrates' court","The youth court","No court: it is always assessed"],"e":"In the **Crown Court** the interests of justice test is **automatically satisfied**; only means is assessed."},{"q":"What must the prosecution serve at or before the first hearing?","o":["Initial details of the prosecution case","A defence statement","The schedule of sensitive material","A certificate of full argument"],"e":"The **IDPC**: a summary, relevant witness statements and previous convictions."},{"q":"How is wounding with intent under s.18 OAPA 1861 classified?","o":["Indictable only","Either way","Summary only","Summary with a right to elect"],"e":"s.18 is **indictable only**, so it is sent straight to the Crown Court."}]},{"id":"crimprac/allocation-sending","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 6: Plea before venue, allocation and sending","qs":[{"q":"What are the magistrates' maximum custodial powers for a single either-way offence at September 2026?","o":["12 months","6 months","18 months","24 months"],"e":"**12 months** for convictions on or after 18 November 2024. 18-24 months was only proposed."},{"q":"At or below what value is shoplifting 'low-value' under s.22A MCA 1980?","o":["£200","£5,000","£500","£1,000"],"e":"**£200 or less**: a summary offence, but an adult may still **elect** Crown Court trial."},{"q":"Which provision governs plea before venue for either-way offences?","o":["s.17A MCA 1980","s.19 MCA 1980","s.20 MCA 1980","s.51 CDA 1998"],"e":"**s.17A** is plea before venue; s.19 is the allocation decision; s.51 is sending."},{"q":"Under which provision is an adult charged with an indictable-only offence sent to the Crown Court?","o":["s.51 CDA 1998","s.19 MCA 1980","s.14 Sentencing Code","s.22 MCA 1980"],"e":"**s.51(1) CDA 1998**: indictable-only cases are **sent forthwith**."}]},{"id":"crimprac/case-management-disclosure","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 7: Case management and disclosure","qs":[{"q":"Within what period must a defence statement be served in the Crown Court?","o":["28 days","14 days","21 days","7 days"],"e":"**28 days** after initial disclosure. In the magistrates' court it is voluntary (14 days)."},{"q":"What is the test for initial disclosure of unused material under s.3 CPIA 1996?","o":["Might undermine the prosecution or assist the accused","Is relevant to any issue in the case","Is relied on by the prosecution","Has been requested by the defence"],"e":"Material **capable of undermining** the prosecution or **assisting** the accused must be disclosed."},{"q":"When can the defence make a s.8 CPIA application for specific disclosure?","o":["Only after serving a defence statement","Only after arraignment","Only with prosecution consent","Only at the PTPH"],"e":"A **s.8 application** requires a **defence statement** to have been served first."},{"q":"Within what period of sending is the PTPH usually held?","o":["28 days","14 days","56 days","7 days"],"e":"The **Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing** is usually within **28 days** of sending."}]},{"id":"crimprac/evidence-identification-silence","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 8: Evidence: identification (Turnbull) and adverse inferences","qs":[{"q":"In the Turnbull 'ADVOKATE' factors, what does 'T' stand for?","o":["Time lapse before identification","Type of offence","Trial delay","Training of the witness"],"e":"**T** is the **time lapse** between observation and later identification."},{"q":"Which section allows inferences from failure to account for objects, substances or marks?","o":["s.36 CJPOA 1994","s.34 CJPOA 1994","s.35 CJPOA 1994","s.37 CJPOA 1994"],"e":"**s.36** (objects, substances, marks) and s.37 (presence) both need a **special warning**."},{"q":"Which provision prevents a conviction based solely on an adverse inference?","o":["s.38(3) CJPOA 1994","s.78 PACE","s.34(2A) CJPOA 1994","s.76 PACE"],"e":"**s.38(3)**: D cannot be convicted **solely** on an inference; a case to answer is needed first."},{"q":"What should the judge do with poor-quality, unsupported identification evidence?","o":["Withdraw the case from the jury","Give a Turnbull warning only","Order a new identification parade","Admit it with a good character direction"],"e":"Under **Turnbull**, weak unsupported identification means the case should be **withdrawn**."}]},{"id":"crimprac/hearsay-confessions","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 9: Hearsay, confessions and exclusion of evidence","qs":[{"q":"Under s.76(2)(a) PACE, which police conduct amounts to 'oppression'?","o":["Exercising power in a burdensome, harsh or wrongful way","Any questioning that makes D uncomfortable","Any breach of the PACE Codes","Any interview without a solicitor"],"e":"**Oppression** means the exercise of authority in a **burdensome, harsh or wrongful** manner (R v Fulling)."},{"q":"Once a s.76(2) issue is raised, who must prove the confession was properly obtained, and to what standard?","o":["Prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt","Defence, on the balance of probabilities","Prosecution, on the balance of probabilities","Defence, beyond reasonable doubt"],"e":"The **prosecution** must prove it **beyond reasonable doubt**, or the court **must exclude** it."},{"q":"Which s.116 CJA 2003 ground of unavailability needs the court's leave?","o":["Fear","Death","Being outside the UK","Cannot be found"],"e":"The **fear** ground needs **leave** in the interests of justice."},{"q":"Which section of the CJA 2003 makes business documents admissible as hearsay?","o":["s.117","s.116","s.118","s.124"],"e":"**s.117**: business documents. s.116 is unavailable witnesses; s.118 preserves common law rules."}]},{"id":"crimprac/bad-character","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 10: Bad character (CJA 2003)","qs":[{"q":"Which s.101(1) gateway can only be used by a co-defendant?","o":["Gateway (e)","Gateway (d)","Gateway (f)","Gateway (g)"],"e":"**Gateway (e)**: substantial probative value on an issue between D and a **co-defendant**."},{"q":"To which gateways does the s.101(3) fairness exclusion apply?","o":["(d) and (g)","(c) and (e)","(a) and (b)","(f) and (g)"],"e":"**s.101(3)** applies only to gateways **(d) and (g)**, on D's application."},{"q":"Which gateway opens if D alleges a prosecution witness invented the allegation?","o":["Gateway (g)","Gateway (f)","Gateway (c)","Gateway (b)"],"e":"Attacking another person's character opens **gateway (g)** (s.106)."},{"q":"The prosecution seeks to admit D's previous convictions under gateway (d) to show propensity. Which question must the court ask?","o":["Does the propensity make guilt more likely?","Has D attacked a witness's character?","Did D give evidence at trial?","Were the convictions within the last year?"],"e":"The three questions: does the history show **propensity**, does it make guilt **more likely**, and is it **just** to rely on it (R v Hanson)."},{"q":"Which section governs the bad character of non-defendants?","o":["s.100 CJA 2003","s.101 CJA 2003","s.98 CJA 2003","s.103 CJA 2003"],"e":"**s.100** needs the court's **leave** unless all parties agree."}]},{"id":"crimprac/trial-procedure","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 11: Trial procedure, witnesses and the Welsh language","qs":[{"q":"At the close of the prosecution case, the evidence is so tenuous that a properly directed jury could not properly convict. What should the judge do on a submission?","o":["Uphold the submission of no case to answer","Leave it to the jury to weigh","Give a Turnbull warning and continue","Discharge the jury and order a retrial"],"e":"**No case to answer**: no evidence, or evidence so **tenuous** that a properly directed jury could not properly convict (R v Galbraith)."},{"q":"What is the minimum deliberation time before a majority verdict can be accepted?","o":["2 hours 10 minutes","1 hour","2 hours","3 hours"],"e":"At least **2 hours 10 minutes**; majorities of 10-2 (or 10-1/9-1)."},{"q":"How is a Circuit Judge addressed in the Crown Court?","o":["Your Honour","My Lord or My Lady","Sir or Madam","Your Worship"],"e":"Circuit Judges and Recorders are **'Your Honour'**; High Court judges are 'My Lord/My Lady'."},{"q":"Which statute lets any party or witness speak Welsh in legal proceedings in Wales?","o":["Welsh Language Act 1993","Government of Wales Act 2006","Criminal Procedure Act 1865","Youth Justice and Criminal Evidence Act 1999"],"e":"**s.22 Welsh Language Act 1993**; prior **notice** lets an interpreter be arranged."},{"q":"Witnesses under what age give unsworn evidence?","o":["Under 14","Under 16","Under 18","Under 10"],"e":"Witnesses **under 14** give **unsworn** evidence under the YJCEA 1999."}]},{"id":"crimprac/sentencing","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 12: Sentencing","qs":[{"q":"What guilty plea reduction is normally given for a plea on the first day of trial?","o":["One tenth","One third","One quarter","One sixth"],"e":"Sliding scale: **one third** at first stage, up to **one quarter** after, **one tenth** on the first day of trial."},{"q":"How many hours of unpaid work can a community order require?","o":["40 to 300 hours","20 to 200 hours","50 to 500 hours","100 to 400 hours"],"e":"Unpaid work requirements range from **40 to 300 hours**."},{"q":"Under the Sentencing Act 2026, the presumption of suspension applies to custodial terms of what length?","o":["12 months or less","6 months or less","2 years or less","3 years or less"],"e":"For adults, custody of **12 months or less** must be suspended unless **exceptional circumstances**."},{"q":"If D's account is rejected at a Newton hearing, what normally happens to the plea reduction?","o":["It is halved","It is lost entirely","It is unaffected","It is reduced to one tenth"],"e":"Guideline: the guilty plea reduction is normally **halved**."},{"q":"What is the maximum term that can be suspended where D is convicted on or after 22 March 2026?","o":["3 years","2 years","12 months","5 years"],"e":"**Sentencing Act 2026 s.2** raised the maximum from 2 to **3 years** for offenders **convicted on or after 22 March 2026**."}]},{"id":"crimprac/appeals","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 13: Appeals","qs":[{"q":"Within what time must notice of appeal from the magistrates' court to the Crown Court be given?","o":["15 business days after sentence","21 days after the decision","28 days after conviction","14 days after sentence"],"e":"**15 business days** after sentence (CrimPR Part 34); no leave needed if in time."},{"q":"To which court does an appeal by way of case stated from the magistrates go?","o":["The High Court","The Crown Court","The Court of Appeal","The Supreme Court"],"e":"Case stated (s.111 MCA 1980) goes to the **High Court** (Divisional Court) on a point of **law**."},{"q":"What is the only ground for an appeal against conviction to the Court of Appeal?","o":["The conviction is unsafe","The verdict was against the weight of evidence","The sentence was manifestly excessive","The jury was not unanimous"],"e":"**s.2 Criminal Appeal Act 1968**: the conviction must be **unsafe**."},{"q":"Who may refer an unduly lenient sentence to the Court of Appeal?","o":["The Attorney General","The prosecutor","The trial judge","The victim"],"e":"The **Attorney General** (s.36 CJA 1988), within **28 days** of sentence."},{"q":"Which court can increase sentence on a defendant's own appeal?","o":["The Crown Court on appeal from magistrates","The Court of Appeal","The Supreme Court","The High Court on case stated"],"e":"The **Crown Court** can impose any sentence the magistrates could, even a **more severe** one."}]},{"id":"crimprac/youth-court","name":"Criminal Law and Practice 14: Youth court and sentencing children","qs":[{"q":"What age range does the youth court deal with?","o":["10 to 17","12 to 17","10 to 18","14 to 17"],"e":"The youth court handles **10 to 17** year olds; criminal responsibility starts at **10**."},{"q":"What is the maximum length of a detention and training order?","o":["24 months","12 months","36 months","18 months"],"e":"DTOs come in fixed lengths from **4 to 24 months**, half in detention and half under supervision."},{"q":"How long can a referral order last?","o":["3 to 12 months","6 to 24 months","1 to 6 months","12 to 36 months"],"e":"Referral orders last **3 to 12 months** with a **youth offender panel** contract."},{"q":"For a 12 to 14 year old, a DTO is only available if the child is what?","o":["A persistent offender","Convicted of a grave crime","Jointly charged with an adult","Already on a referral order"],"e":"DTOs for **12-14** year olds require the child to be a **persistent offender**."},{"q":"Which provision governs long-term detention for 'grave crimes' committed by children?","o":["s.250 Sentencing Code","s.51 CDA 1998","s.49 CYPA 1933","s.37 CDA 1998"],"e":"**s.250**: offences carrying **14 years or more** for an adult; sent only if well beyond the 2-year DTO maximum is likely."}]}]},{"id":"dr","name":"Dispute Resolution","topics":[{"id":"dr/dr-options","name":"Dispute Resolution 1: Options: arbitration, mediation and litigation","qs":[{"q":"Within what period must a court challenge to an arbitration award under ss.67-69 Arbitration Act 1996 be made?","o":["28 days of the award","14 days of the award","21 days of the award","3 months of the award"],"e":"Challenges for lack of jurisdiction (s.67), serious irregularity (s.68) or on a point of law (s.69) must be brought within **28 days** of the award."},{"q":"Under the Arbitration Act 2025, absent an express choice, which law governs the arbitration agreement?","o":["The law of the seat","The law governing the main contract","The law of the claimant's residence","The law where the award is enforced"],"e":"New **s.6A**: the **law of the seat** applies unless the parties **expressly** choose a law for the arbitration agreement itself. A governing law clause in the main contract is not enough."},{"q":"A party refuses to mediate, arguing the court cannot force it into ADR. Can the court order the parties to engage in ADR?","o":["Yes: if proportionate and it does not impair the right to a trial","No: ADR is always voluntary","No: the court can only penalise in costs","Only if both parties agree to it"],"e":"The court **can order** ADR if it is **proportionate** and does not impair the essence of the **right to a judicial hearing** (Churchill v Merthyr Tydfil CBC)."},{"q":"Which section of the Arbitration Act 1996 gives a mandatory stay of court proceedings brought in breach of an arbitration clause?","o":["Section 9","Section 44","Section 67","Section 69"],"e":"**s.9** gives a mandatory stay, provided the applicant has not taken a step to answer the claim on its merits."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-limitation","name":"Dispute Resolution 2: Limitation periods","qs":[{"q":"What is the limitation period for a breach of contract claim where the contract is a simple contract?","o":["6 years from breach","6 years from damage","3 years from breach","12 years from breach"],"e":"**s.5 LA 1980**: **6 years** from the **breach**, even if loss is discovered later. A contract by **deed** has 12 years."},{"q":"What is the limitation period for a personal injury claim?","o":["3 years from injury or date of knowledge, if later","6 years from the injury","3 years from the injury only","1 year from date of knowledge"],"e":"**ss.11 and 14**: **3 years** from the injury or the **date of knowledge**, whichever is later, with a s.33 discretion to disapply."},{"q":"What is the longstop for latent damage claims in negligence under s.14B Limitation Act 1980?","o":["15 years from the negligent act","10 years from the negligent act","15 years from the damage","12 years from date of knowledge"],"e":"s.14A gives 6 years from damage or 3 years from knowledge, but **s.14B** imposes an absolute **15-year longstop** from the **negligent act**."},{"q":"A child is injured at age 15. By when must a personal injury claim be issued?","o":["Their 21st birthday","Their 18th birthday","Their 25th birthday","3 years from the injury"],"e":"Time does not run while the claimant is under **18**; the 3-year period then runs to the **21st birthday**."},{"q":"What is the limitation period for defamation?","o":["1 year","3 years","6 years","2 years"],"e":"Defamation has a **1-year** period, with a discretion to extend."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-pre-action","name":"Dispute Resolution 3: Pre-action conduct and protocols","qs":[{"q":"How long does a debtor have to reply to a letter of claim under the Debt Claims Pre-Action Protocol?","o":["30 days","14 days","21 days","28 days"],"e":"The **Debt Claims PAP** gives **30 days**, and applies only where the debtor is an **individual** (including a sole trader)."},{"q":"Under the Personal Injury Protocol, how long does the defendant have to investigate and respond on liability after acknowledging?","o":["3 months","21 days","28 days","6 months"],"e":"The defendant acknowledges within **21 days**, then has **3 months** to investigate and say whether liability is admitted."},{"q":"Up to what enhanced interest rate may the court order against a defendant who breached a pre-action protocol?","o":["10% above base rate","5% above base rate","8% flat rate","15% above base rate"],"e":"Where the **defendant** is at fault, the court can award interest at up to **10% above base rate**."},{"q":"Under the PD-PAC, what is a reasonable time to respond to a letter before claim in a simple case?","o":["14 days","7 days","30 days","3 months"],"e":"**14 days** in a simple case, up to **3 months** in a very complex one."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-parties-law","name":"Dispute Resolution 4: Parties, causes of action, applicable law and Welsh language","qs":[{"q":"Under Rome II, which law generally governs a tort claim?","o":["Law of the country where damage occurs","Law of the country of the wrongful act","Law of the forum court","Law of the claimant's nationality"],"e":"**Art 4(1) Rome II**: the law where the **damage occurs**, subject to common habitual residence (Art 4(2)) and the escape clause."},{"q":"Under Rome I, absent choice, which law governs a contract for the sale of goods?","o":["The seller's habitual residence","The buyer's habitual residence","Where the goods are delivered","Where the contract was signed"],"e":"**Art 4 Rome I**: sale of goods is governed by the **seller's** habitual residence."},{"q":"Through whom does a child conduct civil proceedings?","o":["A litigation friend","A personal representative","The Official Solicitor only","A guardian ad litem appointed by the claimant"],"e":"Children and protected parties act through a **litigation friend**, and any settlement needs **court approval**."},{"q":"In whose name is a partnership sued?","o":["The firm name","Each partner's own name only","The senior partner's name","The registered office name"],"e":"Partnerships sue and are sued in the **firm name**."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-where-to-start","name":"Dispute Resolution 5: Where to start: High Court or county court","qs":[{"q":"Below what value must a non-personal injury money claim start in the County Court?","o":["£100,000 or less","£25,000 or less","£50,000 or less","£150,000 or less"],"e":"Money claims worth **£100,000 or less** must start in the **County Court**."},{"q":"What minimum value must a personal injury claim have to start in the High Court?","o":["£50,000","£25,000","£100,000","£10,000"],"e":"Personal injury claims can only start in the High Court if worth **£50,000 or more**."},{"q":"Which specialist court hears building, engineering and IT disputes?","o":["Technology and Construction Court","Commercial Court","Circuit Commercial Court","Intellectual Property Enterprise Court"],"e":"The **TCC** (CPR 60) in the Business and Property Courts hears construction, engineering and IT disputes."},{"q":"When valuing a claim for court choice, which item is taken into account?","o":["What the claimant reasonably expects to recover","Interest claimed","Costs","Any counterclaim"],"e":"Value is what the claimant **reasonably expects to recover**, ignoring **interest**, **costs**, contributory negligence and counterclaims."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-issue-service","name":"Dispute Resolution 6: Issuing and serving proceedings; changing parties","qs":[{"q":"Within what period must the step for serving a claim form within the jurisdiction be completed?","o":["4 months after issue","6 months after issue","14 days after issue","2 months after issue"],"e":"**CPR 7.5**: the step must be completed before midnight on the calendar day **4 months after issue** (6 months outside the jurisdiction)."},{"q":"When is a claim form deemed served?","o":["Second business day after the step","Day after posting","Second day after posting, any day","Same day if before 4.30pm"],"e":"**CPR 6.14**: the claim form is deemed served on the **second business day** after the step, whatever the method."},{"q":"A claimant emails the claim form to the defendant's solicitors, who never agreed in writing to accept email service. Limitation then expires. Will the court validate service because the solicitors knew of the claim?","o":["No: knowledge of the claim alone is not a good reason","Yes: the purpose of service was achieved","Yes: litigants in person get special leniency","Yes: email is always a permitted method"],"e":"Email service needs **prior written agreement**; the defendant merely **knowing** of the claim is not a good reason to validate (Barton v Wright Hassall)."},{"q":"Within what time must separately served particulars of claim follow the claim form?","o":["14 days of service of the claim form","28 days of service of the claim form","7 days of issue","21 days of issue"],"e":"Particulars must be served within **14 days** of service of the claim form, and no later than the last date for serving it."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-responding","name":"Dispute Resolution 7: Responding to a claim, default judgment, discontinuance and settlement","qs":[{"q":"When is a defence due if the defendant has filed an acknowledgment of service?","o":["28 days after service of particulars","14 days after service of particulars","28 days after filing the AoS","21 days after service of particulars"],"e":"Defence is due in **14 days**, or **28 days** from service of the particulars if an **AoS** is filed."},{"q":"By how long may the parties agree to extend time for a defence without a court order?","o":["Up to 28 days","Up to 14 days","Up to 21 days","Up to 56 days"],"e":"Up to **28 days** by agreement, and the defendant must **tell the court in writing**."},{"q":"What must a defendant show for a discretionary set-aside of default judgment under CPR 13.3?","o":["Real prospect of successfully defending","Judgment was wrongly entered","Exceptional circumstances","A good defence on the balance of probabilities"],"e":"**CPR 13.3**: a **real prospect of successfully defending** or some other good reason. Wrongly entered judgment falls under mandatory CPR 13.2."},{"q":"Which order stays a claim on terms set out in a confidential schedule?","o":["Tomlin order","Consent order","Unless order","Charging order"],"e":"A **Tomlin order** stays the claim on terms in a **confidential schedule**, with **liberty to apply** to enforce."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-statements-of-case","name":"Dispute Resolution 8: Statements of case","qs":[{"q":"How is an allegation in the particulars treated if the defence fails to deal with it and sets out no case on that issue?","o":["Admitted","Denied","Requiring proof","Struck out"],"e":"**CPR 16.5**: an allegation not dealt with is **treated as admitted**, unless the defence sets out the defendant's case on it."},{"q":"After limitation expires, which amendment can CPR 17.4 permit?","o":["New claim from substantially the same facts","Any new claim with the court's permission","A new claim on different facts by consent","Adding unrelated causes of action"],"e":"**CPR 17.4** allows a new claim arising from the **same or substantially the same facts**, correcting a name mistake, or a change of capacity."},{"q":"Which CPR Part governs requests for further information?","o":["Part 18","Part 17","Part 20","Part 22"],"e":"**Part 18** covers requests for clarification or additional information, verified by a statement of truth."},{"q":"When is a reply filed, if one is used?","o":["With the directions questionnaire","Within 14 days of the defence","With the pre-trial checklist","Within 28 days of the defence"],"e":"A reply is **optional** and is filed with the **directions questionnaire**. Not replying is not an admission."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-interim","name":"Dispute Resolution 9: Interim applications, summary judgment, interim payments and injunctions","qs":[{"q":"What is the test for summary judgment under Part 24?","o":["No real prospect and no compelling reason for trial","No reasonable grounds for the claim","Balance of probabilities favours applicant","Serious question to be tried"],"e":"The party must have **no real prospect of succeeding** and there must be **no other compelling reason** for a trial."},{"q":"C seeks an interim injunction. There is a serious question to be tried, and damages would adequately compensate C. Will the injunction normally be granted?","o":["No: adequate damages normally means no injunction","Yes: a serious question to be tried is enough","Yes, if C has a strong prima facie case","Only if C gives no cross-undertaking"],"e":"**American Cyanamid**: after a serious question to be tried, if **damages are adequate** for the applicant, the injunction is normally refused; otherwise the cross-undertaking and **balance of convenience** are considered."},{"q":"How much notice of a summary judgment hearing must the respondent receive?","o":["At least 14 days","At least 3 clear days","At least 7 days","At least 21 days"],"e":"At least **14 days' notice**; respondent's evidence **7 days** before; reply evidence **3 days** before."},{"q":"What evidence is needed for a freezing injunction?","o":["Affidavit","Witness statement","Statement of case","Expert report"],"e":"**Freezing injunctions** and **search orders** require **affidavit** evidence."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-case-management","name":"Dispute Resolution 10: Case management, tracks and sanctions","qs":[{"q":"What is the normal financial limit of the small claims track?","o":["£10,000","£5,000","£25,000","£1,000"],"e":"The small claims track covers claims up to **£10,000** (with lower limits for personal injury and housing disrepair)."},{"q":"What is the maximum trial length on the intermediate track?","o":["3 days","1 day","2 days","5 days"],"e":"The **intermediate track** (£25,000 to £100,000) has trials of **no more than three days**."},{"q":"A party misses a court deadline, a sanction applies, and it seeks relief under CPR 3.9. What does the court consider first?","o":["The seriousness and significance of the breach","Why the default occurred","All the circumstances of the case","Whether the other side was prejudiced"],"e":"**Denton** three stages: (1) **seriousness and significance** of the breach, (2) why it happened, (3) all the circumstances."},{"q":"If a party fails to file a costs budget, what is its budget treated as limited to?","o":["Court fees","Fixed recoverable costs","Nil","Half its incurred costs"],"e":"**CPR 3.14**: a party who fails to file a budget is treated as having filed one limited to its **court fees**, unless relieved."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-evidence","name":"Dispute Resolution 11: Evidence: witnesses, hearsay and experts","qs":[{"q":"What is the civil standard of proof, including for fraud allegations?","o":["Balance of probabilities","Beyond reasonable doubt","Clear and convincing evidence","Real prospect of success"],"e":"The civil standard is the **balance of probabilities**, even for serious allegations such as fraud."},{"q":"Within what period may written questions be put to an opponent's expert?","o":["28 days of service of the report","14 days of service of the report","21 days of service of the report","7 days before trial"],"e":"Written questions: **once**, within **28 days** of service, and only to **clarify** the report."},{"q":"What is the effect of failing to give hearsay notice in civil proceedings?","o":["Affects weight and costs only","Evidence becomes inadmissible","Claim is struck out","Witness must attend"],"e":"Under the **Civil Evidence Act 1995**, failure to give notice goes to **weight** and **costs**, not admissibility."},{"q":"To whom does an expert witness owe an overriding duty?","o":["The court","The instructing party","The instructing solicitor","Both parties equally"],"e":"Under **Part 35** the expert's **overriding duty is to the court**, not the party paying them."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-disclosure","name":"Dispute Resolution 12: Disclosure, privilege and without prejudice","qs":[{"q":"Which CPR rule governs pre-action disclosure?","o":["CPR 31.16","CPR 31.17","CPR 31.12","CPR 31.20"],"e":"**CPR 31.16** is pre-action disclosure; 31.17 non-party; 31.12 specific; 31.20 inadvertent disclosure."},{"q":"Which privilege can cover communications with a third party such as an expert?","o":["Litigation privilege","Legal advice privilege","Without prejudice privilege","Common interest only"],"e":"**Litigation privilege** covers third-party communications when litigation is **reasonably in contemplation**, for its **dominant purpose**."},{"q":"Who can waive legal professional privilege?","o":["The client","The solicitor","The court","The opposing party"],"e":"Privilege belongs to the **client**, and only the client can waive it."},{"q":"What form is used for a list of documents in standard disclosure?","o":["N265","N244","N180","N170"],"e":"Disclosure is by a list of documents on **N265** with a signed **disclosure statement**."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-trial","name":"Dispute Resolution 13: Trial preparation, procedure and judgment","qs":[{"q":"How should a Circuit Judge be addressed?","o":["Your Honour","My Lord or My Lady","Sir or Madam","Master"],"e":"Circuit Judges and Recorders are **'Your Honour'**; High Court Judges are 'My Lord/My Lady'."},{"q":"How long before attendance must a witness summons be served to be binding?","o":["At least 7 days","At least 14 days","At least 3 days","At least 21 days"],"e":"**CPR 34**: served at least **7 days** before, with **conduct money** offered."},{"q":"In which stage of questioning are leading questions allowed?","o":["Cross-examination","Examination in chief","Re-examination","Opening speech"],"e":"Leading questions are **allowed in cross-examination** but not in chief or re-examination, except on undisputed matters."},{"q":"Who usually prepares and files the trial bundle?","o":["The claimant","The defendant","The court","Both parties jointly by default"],"e":"The **claimant** prepares and files the bundle, normally between **7 and 3 days** before trial."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-costs","name":"Dispute Resolution 14: Costs, Part 36 offers and security for costs","qs":[{"q":"What is the minimum relevant period for a Part 36 offer?","o":["21 days","14 days","28 days","7 days"],"e":"A Part 36 offer must specify a **relevant period** of at least **21 days**."},{"q":"On the standard basis, in whose favour is doubt about costs resolved?","o":["The paying party","The receiving party","The court","Neither party"],"e":"**Standard basis**: doubt goes to the **paying party**; on the indemnity basis, to the receiving party."},{"q":"What additional amount is awarded when a claimant beats its own Part 36 offer?","o":["10% of damages up to £500,000","5% of damages up to £500,000","25% of damages","35% of damages"],"e":"An **additional 10%** of damages up to £500,000 (5% above), plus enhanced interest and indemnity costs."},{"q":"What does a 'costs in the case' order mean?","o":["Winner at trial gets these costs","Named party gets costs regardless","Each party bears its own costs","Decision is postponed"],"e":"**Costs in the case**: whoever wins at trial gets the costs of the application."},{"q":"Against whom can security for costs be ordered?","o":["A claimant","A defendant","Any witness","The defendant's insurer"],"e":"Security for costs is ordered only against a **claimant** (or a counterclaiming defendant), on application by the defendant."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-appeals","name":"Dispute Resolution 15: Appeals","qs":[{"q":"Within what time must an appellant's notice be filed?","o":["21 days after the decision","14 days after the decision","28 days after the decision","21 days after the sealed order"],"e":"**21 days** after the date of the **decision**, unless the lower court directs otherwise."},{"q":"To whom does an appeal from a County Court District Judge go?","o":["A Circuit Judge","A High Court Judge","The Court of Appeal","Another District Judge"],"e":"The 'next judge up': a District Judge's decision goes to a **Circuit Judge**."},{"q":"What is the test for permission to appeal on a first appeal?","o":["Real prospect of success or compelling reason","Important point of principle","Decision plainly wrong","Serious question to be tried"],"e":"Permission is granted where there is a **real prospect of success** or **some other compelling reason**."},{"q":"Within what time must a respondent's notice be filed?","o":["14 days","7 days","21 days","28 days"],"e":"A respondent's notice is filed within **14 days**."}]},{"id":"dr/dr-enforcement","name":"Dispute Resolution 16: Enforcement of money judgments","qs":[{"q":"Which enforcement method suits a debtor with money in a bank account?","o":["Third party debt order","Charging order","Attachment of earnings order","Writ of control"],"e":"A **third party debt order** (CPR 72) freezes and transfers money owed to the debtor by a bank."},{"q":"Which enforcement method is available only in the County Court?","o":["Attachment of earnings order","Charging order","Third party debt order","Order to obtain information"],"e":"**Attachment of earnings** is a County Court remedy for **employed** debtors only."},{"q":"Which method secures a judgment debt on the debtor's land?","o":["Charging order","Third party debt order","Warrant of control","Attachment of earnings order"],"e":"A **charging order** (CPR 73) secures the debt on land, with a possible later **order for sale**."},{"q":"How long before the hearing must an order to obtain information be personally served?","o":["At least 14 days","At least 7 days","At least 28 days","At least 3 days"],"e":"**CPR 71**: personal service at least **14 days** before the hearing."}]}]},{"id":"ethics","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct","topics":[{"id":"ethics/principles-competence","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct 1: The SRA Principles, the Codes and the Statement of Solicitor Competence","qs":[{"q":"How many SRA Principles are there?","o":["Seven","Five","Ten","Twelve"],"e":"There are **seven** Principles, from the rule of law to the best interests of each client."},{"q":"Which Principles prevail when the Principles conflict?","o":["Those protecting the wider public interest","Best interests of each client","Independence","Equality, diversity and inclusion"],"e":"Principles safeguarding the **wider public interest** take precedence over the client's interests."},{"q":"A solicitor backdates a document, knowing the facts but believing it is acceptable. Ordinary decent people would see it as dishonest. Is the solicitor dishonest?","o":["Yes: honesty is judged objectively on their actual state of mind","No: the solicitor did not realise it was dishonest","No: dishonesty requires an intent to gain","Only if the client lost money"],"e":"**Dishonesty test**: find the person's **actual knowledge or belief** as to the facts, then apply the standards of **ordinary decent people**; they need not realise it is dishonest (Ivey v Genting)."},{"q":"How many areas does the Statement of Solicitor Competence have?","o":["Four","Three","Six","Seven"],"e":"**Four**: ethics; technical legal practice; working with other people; managing themselves and their work."}]},{"id":"ethics/trust-fairness-undertakings","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct 2: Maintaining trust, acting fairly and undertakings","qs":[{"q":"Must an undertaking be in writing to be binding?","o":["No, it can be oral","Yes, always","Only if over £10,000","Only if signed by a partner"],"e":"An undertaking can be **oral or written** and need not use the word \"undertake\"."},{"q":"Who can release a solicitor from an undertaking?","o":["The recipient","The client","The firm's COLP","The SRA"],"e":"Only the **recipient** can agree to vary or release it. The client cannot override it."},{"q":"If no time is agreed, when must an undertaking be performed?","o":["Within a reasonable time","Within 14 days","Within 28 days","Whenever convenient"],"e":"Code **para 1.3**: within the agreed time or a **reasonable time**."},{"q":"Which Code paragraph requires managers to challenge bullying and harassment?","o":["Para 1.5","Para 1.2","Para 3.5","Para 7.9"],"e":"**Para 1.5**: treat colleagues fairly; managers must **challenge** behaviour below this standard."}]},{"id":"ethics/duty-to-court","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct 3: Duty to the court and dispute resolution","qs":[{"q":"Which Code paragraph requires drawing adverse authorities to the court's attention?","o":["Para 2.7","Para 2.4","Para 1.4","Para 6.4"],"e":"**Para 2.7**: relevant cases, statutes and procedural irregularities, even if adverse."},{"q":"Can a solicitor whose client admits guilt continue to act on a not guilty plea?","o":["Yes, but not assert innocence","No, they must withdraw","Yes, and call an alibi","Only with the court's permission"],"e":"They can **put the prosecution to proof** but must not assert innocence or call false evidence."},{"q":"What should a solicitor tell the court when withdrawing because the client will mislead it?","o":["Professional reasons","The client's admission","The client's planned evidence","Nothing, just leave"],"e":"Confidentiality means saying only it is for **professional reasons**."},{"q":"Which payment to a witness is prohibited?","o":["One dependent on the outcome","Reasonable travel expenses","Compensation for lost earnings","An expert's agreed hourly fee"],"e":"Code **para 2.3** bans benefits **dependent on the evidence or outcome**."}]},{"id":"ethics/instructions-capacity-service","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct 4: Taking instructions, capacity, authority and service","qs":[{"q":"Which Act sets the test for mental capacity?","o":["Mental Capacity Act 2005","Mental Health Act 1983","Care Act 2014","Wills Act 1837"],"e":"The **Mental Capacity Act 2005**: presumption of capacity, decision-specific test."},{"q":"Under the MCA 2005, how is capacity assessed?","o":["Decision-specific, at the time","By medical diagnosis alone","By age over 75","By the wisdom of the decision"],"e":"Capacity is **decision-specific**. A diagnosis or unwise decision does not prove lack of capacity."},{"q":"On what basis can a solicitor end a retainer?","o":["Good reason and reasonable notice","Any reason, without notice","Only with client consent","Only with SRA permission"],"e":"A solicitor may end it only for **good reason** and on **reasonable notice**."},{"q":"Which Code paragraph limits acting to instructions from the client or someone authorised?","o":["Para 3.1","Para 8.1","Para 6.1","Para 4.2"],"e":"**Para 3.1**: act only on instructions from the client or someone **properly authorised**."}]},{"id":"ethics/client-money","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct 5: Client money and assets","qs":[{"q":"How often must client account reconciliations be carried out?","o":["At least every five weeks","Every month","Every quarter","Once a year"],"e":"SRA Accounts Rules: reconciliations at least every **five weeks**."},{"q":"When can a firm transfer its costs out of client account?","o":["After a bill or written notification","As soon as work is done","At the end of the accounting period","When the client pays the next invoice"],"e":"Costs can be taken only once a **bill or written notification** has been given."},{"q":"What must a firm do on finding a shortfall caused by an error in client account?","o":["Replace it immediately from firm money","Wait for the client to repay","Report to LeO","Correct it at year end"],"e":"Breaches must be **corrected promptly**, and money improperly withdrawn must be **replaced immediately** from the firm's own money."},{"q":"Within what period must an accountant's report be obtained, unless exempt?","o":["Six months of period end","Three months of period end","Twelve months of period end","One month of period end"],"e":"Within **six months** of the end of the accounting period."}]},{"id":"ethics/business-referrals-publicity","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct 6: Business requirements: referrals, fee sharing, introducers and publicity","qs":[{"q":"In which matters are referral payments banned by the Code?","o":["Criminal proceedings","Residential conveyancing","Wills and probate","Commercial contracts"],"e":"Code **para 5.1** bans referral payments for clients in **criminal** proceedings. PI referral fees are banned by LASPO."},{"q":"How must a fee-sharing agreement be made?","o":["In writing","Orally","By deed","With SRA approval"],"e":"Fee-sharing agreements must be **in writing**."},{"q":"Who may a solicitor approach unsolicited to advertise legal services?","o":["Current or former clients","Accident victims in hospital","Any homeowner","People at a police station"],"e":"Para **8.9** bans unsolicited approaches to the public, except **current or former clients**."},{"q":"Which statute bans referral fees in personal injury claims?","o":["LASPO 2012","Legal Services Act 2007","Solicitors Act 1974","Courts and Legal Services Act 1990"],"e":"**LASPO 2012** bans referral fees in personal injury and death claims."}]},{"id":"ethics/conflicts-of-interest","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct 7: Conflict of interests: own interest and client conflicts","qs":[{"q":"Can client consent cure an own interest conflict?","o":["No, never","Yes, if in writing","Yes, with safeguards","Yes, if the client is sophisticated"],"e":"There are **no exceptions** to para 6.1."},{"q":"Which situation fits the competing for the same objective exception?","o":["Two bidders for one business","Buyer and seller of a house","Joint buyers of a house","Co-founders of a company"],"e":"**Competing for the same objective**: two clients seeking the same asset, e.g. two bidders."},{"q":"What should a solicitor do if a client wants to leave them a significant gift and refuses independent advice?","o":["Decline to act","Act, with a file note","Act, with a colleague as witness","Act, but reduce the gift"],"e":"A significant gift is an **own interest conflict**. If the client won't get independent advice, **don't act**."},{"q":"How must consent under the para 6.2 exceptions be given?","o":["Informed, given or evidenced in writing","Orally","By deed","Implied from conduct"],"e":"All clients must give **informed consent, given or evidenced in writing**."}]},{"id":"ethics/confidentiality-disclosure","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct 8: Confidentiality and disclosure","qs":[{"q":"Does the duty of confidentiality survive the client's death?","o":["Yes","No","Only for one year","Only if privileged"],"e":"It continues after the retainer ends and after **death**."},{"q":"Which Code paragraph sets out the duty of disclosure to the client?","o":["Para 6.4","Para 6.3","Para 6.5","Para 7.7"],"e":"**Para 6.4**: tell the client all material information, subject to four exceptions."},{"q":"A firm wants to act against a former client and holds that client's material confidential information. Without the former client's consent, when may it act behind an information barrier?","o":["Only if effective measures leave no real risk of disclosure","Whenever the former client is told","Whenever a partner signs a confidentiality undertaking","Never: acting against former clients is banned"],"e":"Para 6.5: the firm may act if **effective measures** mean there is **no real risk** of disclosure, or with informed written consent (Prince Jefri Bolkiah v KPMG)."},{"q":"Must a solicitor tell their client the contents of a privileged document sent by mistake?","o":["No","Yes, always","Only if helpful","Only if the court agrees"],"e":"Para **6.4(d)** excludes privileged documents known only through **mistaken disclosure**."}]},{"id":"ethics/cooperation-reporting","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct 9: Cooperation, accountability and reporting duties","qs":[{"q":"Which Code paragraph requires reporting a serious breach to the SRA?","o":["Para 7.7","Para 7.3","Para 7.11","Para 8.4"],"e":"**Para 7.7**: report promptly facts reasonably believed capable of a serious breach, including your own."},{"q":"When does telling the COLP satisfy your reporting duty?","o":["On the understanding they will report","Always","Never","Only if in writing"],"e":"Para **7.12**: only on the understanding the COLP or COFA will pass it on."},{"q":"Which Code paragraph protects whistleblowers from detrimental treatment?","o":["Para 7.9","Para 7.5","Para 1.5","Para 3.5"],"e":"**Para 7.9**, whether or not the SRA investigates."},{"q":"Which event must a solicitor notify to the SRA under para 7.6?","o":["Being charged with a criminal offence","Changing practice area","Taking annual leave","Receiving a client complaint"],"e":"Para **7.6**: criminal charge, conviction or caution, insolvency events and material changes."}]},{"id":"ethics/complaints-legal-ombudsman","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct 10: Client identification, complaints handling and the Legal Ombudsman","qs":[{"q":"Since April 2023, what is the basic Legal Ombudsman time limit from the act complained of?","o":["One year","Six years","Three years","Six months"],"e":"**One year** from the act or omission, or one year from when the complainant should have known."},{"q":"How long must a firm normally have to resolve a complaint before LeO steps in?","o":["Eight weeks","Four weeks","Six months","Twelve weeks"],"e":"LeO normally waits for the firm's **final response** or **eight weeks**."},{"q":"What is the maximum value of remedies the Legal Ombudsman can award?","o":["£50,000","£25,000","£100,000","£10,000"],"e":"The total value of remedies is capped at **£50,000**."},{"q":"Within what time of the firm's final response must the complaint go to LeO?","o":["Six months","Eight weeks","One year","Three months"],"e":"**Six months**, provided the final response told the client about LeO and the limit."}]},{"id":"ethics/firms-compliance-protections","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct 11: Code for Firms, compliance officers and client protections","qs":[{"q":"Which compliance officer is responsible for the Accounts Rules?","o":["COFA","COLP","MLRO","Managing partner"],"e":"The **COFA** covers the Accounts Rules; the **COLP** covers everything else."},{"q":"What is the usual maximum Compensation Fund grant per claim?","o":["£2 million","£50,000","£500,000","£3 million"],"e":"The usual maximum is **£2 million**."},{"q":"Who deals with negligence claims against a closed firm made after the six-year run-off period?","o":["The Solicitors Indemnity Fund, managed by the SRA","The Compensation Fund","The Legal Ombudsman","Nobody"],"e":"Post-six-year claims go to the **Solicitors Indemnity Fund (SIF)**, which the **SRA** has managed since **1 October 2023**."},{"q":"How are a firm's managers responsible for its compliance?","o":["Jointly and severally","Only the senior partner","Only the COLP","Only if they caused the breach"],"e":"Under the Code for Firms, managers are **jointly and severally** responsible."}]},{"id":"ethics/ndas-slapps","name":"Ethics and Professional Conduct 12: SRA warning notices: NDAs and SLAPPs","qs":[{"q":"Since October 2025, which Act voids NDA clauses stopping crime victims reporting to police?","o":["Victims and Prisoners Act 2024","Employment Rights Act 1996","Public Interest Disclosure Act 1998","Equality Act 2010"],"e":"**s.17 Victims and Prisoners Act 2024**, in force 1 October 2025."},{"q":"Which Act contains a statutory definition of a SLAPP?","o":["Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act 2023","Defamation Act 2013","Legal Services Act 2007","Human Rights Act 1998"],"e":"**ECCTA 2023 s.195**, though the SRA's expectations are wider."},{"q":"Which label is misused in SLAPP-type letters, according to the SRA?","o":["Without prejudice","Subject to contract","Open correspondence","Draft for approval"],"e":"The SRA warns against marking letters **\"without prejudice\"** (or \"not for publication\") when the conditions aren't met."},{"q":"What must an NDA never prevent a person from doing?","o":["Reporting misconduct to the SRA","Disclosing the settlement sum publicly","Posting about it on social media","Telling a journalist the terms"],"e":"NDAs must not stop **reporting to the SRA**, regulators or the police, or protected disclosures."}]}]},{"id":"land","name":"Land Law","topics":[{"id":"land/nature-of-land","name":"Land Law 1: The nature of land: estates and interests","qs":[{"q":"How many legal estates can exist under s.1(1) LPA 1925?","o":["Two","One","Three","Five"],"e":"Only the **fee simple absolute in possession** and the **term of years absolute** can be legal estates."},{"q":"Without agreement, a seller removes the fitted kitchen units and the carpets before completion. Which removal breaches the contract?","o":["The kitchen units only","The carpets only","Both","Neither"],"e":"Fitted kitchen units are **fixtures** that pass with the land; carpets are **chattels** (Botham v TSB)."},{"q":"Under s.1(3) LPA 1925, what is the status of a restrictive covenant?","o":["Equitable interest","Legal interest","Legal estate","Personal licence"],"e":"Restrictive covenants are not listed in s.1(2), so under **s.1(3)** they can only be **equitable**."},{"q":"Which fixtures test is now the more important one?","o":["Purpose of annexation","Degree of annexation","Value of the item","Date of installation"],"e":"The **purpose of annexation** (to improve the land permanently or to enjoy the object) is now the more important test."}]},{"id":"land/formalities","name":"Land Law 2: Formalities: deeds, contracts and s.53","qs":[{"q":"Which statute sets the formalities for a contract for the sale of land?","o":["s.2 LP(MP)A 1989","s.52 LPA 1925","s.53(1)(b) LPA 1925","s.27 LRA 2002"],"e":"**s.2 LP(MP)A 1989**: in writing, incorporating all agreed terms, signed by or on behalf of each party."},{"q":"What is the effect of a land contract that fails to comply with s.2 LP(MP)A 1989?","o":["Void: no contract at all","Valid but unenforceable","Voidable at the buyer's option","Valid if part performed"],"e":"Non-compliance means there is **no contract at all**, though collateral contracts or estoppel may sometimes help."},{"q":"What is the maximum length of a lease that can be legal without a deed under s.54(2) LPA?","o":["3 years","7 years","1 year","21 years"],"e":"**s.54(2)**: **3 years or less**, in possession, at the best rent, without a premium."},{"q":"What is the effect of a declaration of trust of land not evidenced in signed writing?","o":["Unenforceable, not void","Void","Valid and enforceable","Voidable"],"e":"**s.53(1)(b)**: a declaration of trust must be **evidenced** in signed writing; without it, it is **unenforceable**."},{"q":"A buyer holds a validly granted option and exercises it by a letter signed only by the buyer. Is the exercise valid under s.2 LP(MP)A 1989?","o":["Yes: only the grant must comply with s.2","No: the exercise needs both signatures","No: the exercise must be by deed","Only if the seller countersigns"],"e":"The **grant** of an option must comply with s.2; its **exercise** need not (Spiro v Glencrown)."}]},{"id":"land/registered-land","name":"Land Law 3: Registered title (LRA 2002)","qs":[{"q":"Within what time must an application for first registration be made?","o":["2 months","3 months","1 month","6 months"],"e":"**s.6 LRA 2002**: within **2 months**, or the legal estate reverts to the transferor on bare trust (s.7)."},{"q":"Above what length must the grant of a lease be registered?","o":["More than 7 years","More than 3 years","More than 21 years","More than 1 year"],"e":"A lease of **more than 7 years** is a registrable disposition (s.27 LRA)."},{"q":"Under Sch 3 LRA 2002, which paragraph covers interests of persons in actual occupation?","o":["Paragraph 2","Paragraph 1","Paragraph 3","Paragraph 4"],"e":"**Sch 3 para 2**: interests of persons in **actual occupation**, subject to the inquiry and obvious-occupation exceptions."},{"q":"How can a beneficial interest under a trust of registered land be protected on the register?","o":["By a restriction","By a notice","By a caution","By a land charge"],"e":"**s.33 LRA**: beneficial interests under a trust **cannot** be protected by notice; use a **restriction** (e.g. Form A)."},{"q":"In which part of the register are mortgages and restrictive covenants shown?","o":["Charges register","Property register","Proprietorship register","Index map"],"e":"Burdens such as **mortgages** and **restrictive covenants** appear in the **charges register**."}]},{"id":"land/unregistered-land","name":"Land Law 4: Unregistered title","qs":[{"q":"How old must a good root of title be?","o":["At least 15 years","At least 12 years","At least 30 years","At least 20 years"],"e":"**s.44 LPA 1925** (as amended): a root of title at least **15 years** old."},{"q":"Which land charge class protects an estate contract such as an option?","o":["C(iv)","C(i)","D(ii)","D(iii)"],"e":"**C(iv)** covers estate contracts: contracts to buy, options, rights of pre-emption."},{"q":"In unregistered land, A's option is not registered as a C(iv) land charge. B buys the legal estate for money, knowing all about the option. Is B bound?","o":["No: unregistered, it is void against B","Yes: B had actual notice","Yes: B acted in bad faith","Only if B paid full value"],"e":"An unregistered **C(iv)** charge is **void** against a purchaser of a legal estate for money or money's worth; knowledge is irrelevant (Midland Bank v Green)."},{"q":"Which land charge class protects post-1925 restrictive covenants?","o":["D(ii)","D(iii)","C(iv)","F"],"e":"**D(ii)**: restrictive covenants created after 1925 (not between landlord and tenant)."}]},{"id":"land/co-ownership","name":"Land Law 5: Co-ownership and trusts of land","qs":[{"q":"What is the maximum number of trustees who can hold a legal estate in land?","o":["Four","Two","Three","Six"],"e":"The legal estate is held by up to **four** trustees as **joint tenants**."},{"q":"Which statutory provision allows severance of a beneficial joint tenancy by written notice?","o":["s.36(2) LPA 1925","s.14 TLATA 1996","s.196 LPA 1925","s.27 LPA 1925"],"e":"**s.36(2) LPA 1925**: written notice to all other joint tenants showing an immediate intention to sever."},{"q":"Two trustees mortgage the house and the lender pays the loan money to both. A beneficiary in actual occupation claims priority. Is the lender bound?","o":["No: the interest is overreached","Yes: actual occupation makes it overriding","Yes: if the lender did not inspect","Only if the beneficiary consented"],"e":"Payment to **two trustees** overreaches beneficial interests, even of occupiers (City of London BS v Flegg)."},{"q":"After how long does s.335A Insolvency Act presume creditors' interests outweigh others unless exceptional?","o":["One year","Six months","Two years","Three years"],"e":"After **one year** from bankruptcy, creditors' interests prevail **unless circumstances are exceptional**."},{"q":"Can a will sever a beneficial joint tenancy?","o":["No: survivorship operates first","Yes: if made after purchase","Yes: if witnessed by two","Only with the other's consent"],"e":"A **will cannot sever**: survivorship operates at death, before the will takes effect."}]},{"id":"land/easements","name":"Land Law 6: Easements","qs":[{"q":"Which is a requirement for a right to be capable of being an easement?","o":["It must accommodate the dominant land","It must be registered at HM Land Registry","It must have been used for 20 years","It must be granted for value"],"e":"The four characteristics: dominant and servient tenements, the right **accommodates** the dominant land, different owners, and capable of grant (Re Ellenborough Park)."},{"q":"What period of use is required for prescription?","o":["20 years","12 years","15 years","10 years"],"e":"Prescription needs **20 years'** continuous use **as of right**: without force, secrecy or permission."},{"q":"Which method of implied easement applies only to grant, not reservation?","o":["Wheeldon v Burrows","Necessity","Common intention","Prescription"],"e":"**Wheeldon v Burrows** applies to grant only; necessity and common intention can work for grant or reservation."},{"q":"Which land charge class protects a post-1925 equitable easement in unregistered land?","o":["D(iii)","D(ii)","C(iv)","C(i)"],"e":"Post-1925 **equitable easements** are registered as **D(iii)** land charges."}]},{"id":"land/covenants","name":"Land Law 7: Freehold covenants","qs":[{"q":"B covenants with A to maintain a roof over A's neighbouring freehold. B sells to C. Can A enforce the covenant against C directly?","o":["No: the burden of a positive covenant does not run","Yes: C took with notice","Yes: if it is entered as a notice","Only in equity, not at law"],"e":"The burden of a **positive** freehold covenant does not run at law or in equity (Rhone v Stephens; Austerberry)."},{"q":"B covenants with A not to build on B's land, to benefit A's adjoining land; the covenant is properly protected. B sells to C, who builds. Can A get an injunction against C?","o":["Yes: the burden runs in equity","No: burdens never run with freehold","No: C was not a party to it","No: A can only sue B for damages"],"e":"The burden of a **restrictive** covenant runs in equity if negative, benefiting the covenantee's land, intended to run, and the successor has notice (Tulk v Moxhay)."},{"q":"A post-1925 restrictive covenant touches and concerns A's land but says nothing about successors. Does its benefit pass to A's buyer?","o":["Yes: s.78 LPA annexes it automatically","No: express annexation is needed","No: benefits never run in equity","Only if expressly assigned"],"e":"**s.78 LPA 1925** statutorily annexes the benefit of post-1925 covenants that touch and concern identifiable land (Federated Homes v Mill Lodge)."},{"q":"Which body can discharge or modify a restrictive covenant under s.84 LPA?","o":["Upper Tribunal (Lands Chamber)","High Court Chancery Division","HM Land Registry","First-tier Tribunal (Property Chamber)"],"e":"**s.84 LPA 1925**: the **Upper Tribunal (Lands Chamber)** may discharge or modify on grounds such as obsolescence."}]},{"id":"land/mortgages","name":"Land Law 8: Mortgages","qs":[{"q":"A wife charges the jointly owned home to secure her husband's business debts. What must the bank do to avoid being affected by any undue influence?","o":["Ensure she gets independent legal advice","Have her signature witnessed","Nothing: she is a co-owner","Insist that she benefits from the loan"],"e":"In a non-commercial surety case the bank is **put on inquiry** and must get a solicitor's confirmation that she was **advised independently** (RBS v Etridge (No 2))."},{"q":"Under s.103 LPA, after how long in arrears does unpaid interest make the power of sale exercisable?","o":["2 months","3 months","6 months","1 month"],"e":"**s.103**: interest **2 months in arrears**, 3 months after a notice to repay, or breach of another term."},{"q":"Which statute lets the court adjourn possession proceedings for a dwelling-house?","o":["s.36 Administration of Justice Act 1970","s.101 LPA 1925","s.48 LRA 2002","s.146 LPA 1925"],"e":"**s.36 AJA 1970** (with s.8 AJA 1973): if the borrower is likely to pay sums due within a reasonable period."},{"q":"A lender sells repossessed land without mentioning its valuable planning permission, getting a low price. Which duty has it breached?","o":["Reasonable care to get the true market value","A duty to wait for a better market","A duty to get the borrower's consent to the price","None: the lender may sell as it likes"],"e":"A selling lender must take **reasonable care** to obtain the **true market value** at the time it chooses to sell (Cuckmere Brick v Mutual Finance)."}]},{"id":"land/leases-nature","name":"Land Law 9: Leases: essential characteristics and creation","qs":[{"q":"An agreement called a 'licence' gives the occupier exclusive possession of a flat for a fixed term at a weekly rent. What has been created?","o":["A lease","A licence","A tenancy at will","An easement"],"e":"Exclusive possession for a term at a rent is a **lease**; the label does not matter (Street v Mountford)."},{"q":"A lease is granted 'until the end of the war'. Is it valid as a lease for that term?","o":["No: its maximum duration is uncertain","Yes: the war must end eventually","Yes: if a rent is reserved","Only if made by deed"],"e":"A term must have a **certain maximum duration** from the start (Lace v Chantler)."},{"q":"L and T sign a valid written contract for a 5-year lease, but no deed is executed and T moves in. What does T hold in equity?","o":["An equitable lease on the agreed terms","A legal 5-year lease","Only a licence","Only a tenancy at will"],"e":"Equity treats as done what ought to be done: a valid **contract for a lease** gives an **equitable lease** (Walsh v Lonsdale)."},{"q":"How far ahead may a reversionary lease start under s.149(3) LPA?","o":["21 years","7 years","3 years","99 years"],"e":"A reversionary lease must take effect within **21 years** of grant."}]},{"id":"land/leases-covenants","name":"Land Law 10: Leasehold covenants: liability and alienation","qs":[{"q":"Leases granted on or after which date are governed by the LTCA 1995?","o":["1 January 1996","1 January 1995","1 October 1996","13 October 2003"],"e":"Leases granted on or after **1 January 1996** are 'new' leases under the **Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995**."},{"q":"Within what time must a landlord serve a s.17 notice on a former tenant for a fixed charge?","o":["6 months of it falling due","3 months of it falling due","28 days of it falling due","12 months of it falling due"],"e":"**s.17 LTCA 1995**: notice within **6 months** of the sum becoming due."},{"q":"What is an outgoing tenant's guarantee of its immediate assignee called under the 1995 Act?","o":["Authorised guarantee agreement","Overriding lease","Deed of variation","Licence to assign"],"e":"An **AGA** (s.16) guarantees the **immediate assignee** only."},{"q":"Which statute implies that consent to assign is not to be unreasonably withheld?","o":["s.19(1)(a) LTA 1927","s.1 LTA 1988","s.5 LTCA 1995","s.11 LTA 1985"],"e":"**s.19(1)(a) LTA 1927** applies to qualified covenants; the 1988 Act adds duties on timing and reasons."}]},{"id":"land/leases-remedies","name":"Land Law 11: Remedies for breach and ending leases","qs":[{"q":"How many days' rent must be outstanding for CRAR?","o":["At least 7 days","At least 14 days","At least 28 days","At least 21 days"],"e":"**CRAR**: at least **7 days' rent** outstanding, with **7 clear days'** notice of enforcement."},{"q":"Which notice is needed before forfeiting for breaches other than non-payment of rent?","o":["s.146 LPA notice","s.17 LTCA notice","s.8 Housing Act notice","Formal demand for rent"],"e":"A **s.146 LPA 1925** notice specifying the breach, requiring remedy and any compensation."},{"q":"Under the Leasehold Property (Repairs) Act 1938, within what time may the tenant serve a counter-notice?","o":["28 days","14 days","21 days","3 months"],"e":"The tenant may serve a **counter-notice within 28 days**; the landlord then needs the court's leave."},{"q":"What caps a landlord's damages for breach of a repairing covenant?","o":["Diminution in value of the reversion","Cost of repairs plus 10%","Annual rent","Market value of the property"],"e":"**s.18(1) LTA 1927**: damages cannot exceed the **diminution in the value of the reversion**."},{"q":"What notice to quit is needed for a yearly periodic tenancy at common law?","o":["At least half a year","At least one year","At least one month","At least three months"],"e":"A yearly tenancy needs at least **half a year's** notice ending at the end of a period."}]}]},{"id":"legalsys","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law","topics":[{"id":"legalsys/courts-judiciary","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 1: Courts, the judicial function and rights of audience","qs":[{"q":"Which statute requires ministers to uphold judicial independence?","o":["Constitutional Reform Act 2005","Senior Courts Act 1981","Legal Services Act 2007","Human Rights Act 1998"],"e":"**s.3 Constitutional Reform Act 2005** imposes the duty to uphold the continued independence of the judiciary."},{"q":"Which part of the High Court hears most judicial review claims?","o":["The Administrative Court","The Commercial Court","The Chancery Division","The Family Division"],"e":"Judicial review is heard in the **Administrative Court**, part of the **King's Bench Division**."},{"q":"What does a newly admitted solicitor need before conducting a jury trial as advocate in the Crown Court?","o":["A higher courts advocacy qualification","Five years' post-qualification experience","Permission from the trial judge","A certificate from the Bar Standards Board"],"e":"Solicitors cannot exercise rights of audience in the **higher courts** without a **higher courts advocacy qualification**."},{"q":"Who is head of the judiciary of England and Wales?","o":["The Lord Chief Justice","The Lord Chancellor","The President of the Supreme Court","The Master of the Rolls"],"e":"Since the CRA 2005, the **Lord Chief Justice** heads the judiciary; the Lord Chancellor is no longer a judge."}]},{"id":"legalsys/precedent","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 2: The doctrine of precedent","qs":[{"q":"Which part of a judgment binds lower courts?","o":["The ratio decidendi","The obiter dicta","The dissenting judgment","The summary of counsel's arguments"],"e":"Only the **ratio decidendi** binds; obiter dicta are persuasive."},{"q":"The Court of Appeal (Civil Division) finds that two of its own earlier decisions conflict. What may it do?","o":["Choose which of the two to follow","It must follow the more recent one","It must follow the older one","It must refer the point to the Supreme Court"],"e":"Exceptions: **conflicting** CA decisions (choose one), a decision inconsistent with a **higher court**, or one made **per incuriam** (Young v Bristol Aeroplane)."},{"q":"Under what instrument may the Supreme Court depart from its own previous decisions?","o":["The 1966 Practice Statement","The Constitutional Reform Act 2005","The Senior Courts Act 1981","The Civil Procedure Rules"],"e":"The **1966 Practice Statement** allows departure **when it appears right to do so**."},{"q":"Which courts may depart from assimilated EU case law?","o":["The Supreme Court and Court of Appeal","Only the Supreme Court","Any court hearing the case","Only the CJEU"],"e":"Under **s.6 EUWA 2018** as amended, the **Supreme Court and Court of Appeal** (and equivalents) may depart."}]},{"id":"legalsys/legislation-structure","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 3: Structure of legislation and where it applies (England and Wales)","qs":[{"q":"If a Westminster Act says nothing about commencement, when does it come into force?","o":["Start of the day of Royal Assent","Start of the day after Royal Assent","Two months after Royal Assent","On a date set by regulations"],"e":"**s.4 Interpretation Act 1978**: the beginning of the day of **Royal Assent**."},{"q":"If an Act of Senedd Cymru says nothing about commencement, when does it come into force?","o":["Start of the day after Royal Assent","Start of the day of Royal Assent","One month after Royal Assent","When the Welsh Ministers decide"],"e":"**s.30 Legislation (Wales) Act 2019**: the beginning of the **day after** Royal Assent."},{"q":"What does the 'extent' of an Act describe?","o":["The legal systems it forms part of","The people it applies to","Its date of commencement","The powers it delegates"],"e":"**Extent** is the legal system (e.g. England and Wales); **application** is who or what it governs."},{"q":"Are Explanatory Notes part of an Act of Parliament?","o":["No, they are published alongside it","Yes, as a Schedule","Yes, as the long title","Only if Parliament approves them"],"e":"Explanatory Notes are an **extrinsic aid**, not part of the Act."}]},{"id":"legalsys/statutory-interpretation","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 4: Statutory interpretation (including Welsh legislation)","qs":[{"q":"Which rule of interpretation asks what mischief the Act was passed to remedy?","o":["The mischief rule","The literal rule","The golden rule","Ejusdem generis"],"e":"The **mischief rule** comes from **Heydon's Case**."},{"q":"Whose statements in Hansard can be used under Pepper v Hart?","o":["A minister or promoter of the Bill","Any MP who spoke in debate","Members of the House of Lords only","Select committee chairs"],"e":"Only clear statements by a **minister or other promoter** of the Bill, where the provision is ambiguous or absurd."},{"q":"Which rule limits general words after a list to things of the same kind?","o":["Ejusdem generis","Noscitur a sociis","Expressio unius","The golden rule"],"e":"**Ejusdem generis**: e.g. 'house, office, room or other place' meant indoor places (**Powell v Kempton Park**)."},{"q":"What standing do the English and Welsh texts of a Senedd Act have?","o":["Equal standing","The English text prevails","The Welsh text prevails","The earlier-drafted text prevails"],"e":"**s.156 GoWA 2006**: both texts are of **equal standing** and are read together."}]},{"id":"legalsys/sovereignty-rule-of-law","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 5: Parliamentary sovereignty, separation of powers, rule of law and conventions","qs":[{"q":"A later Act is inconsistent with an earlier 'constitutional statute' but does not mention it. What is the effect?","o":["No implied repeal: express or unmistakably clear words are needed","The later Act impliedly repeals it","The courts strike down the later Act","The earlier Act prevails only if entrenched"],"e":"**Constitutional statutes** are not subject to **implied repeal** (Thoburn v Sunderland CC)."},{"q":"A landowner claims a private Act was obtained by misleading Parliament. Will the court investigate how the Act was passed?","o":["No: courts will not look behind an enrolled Act","Yes: fraud unravels everything","Yes, if the Act takes away property rights","Only if the Speaker certifies an irregularity"],"e":"The **enrolled Act rule**: courts will not inquire into the **parliamentary procedure** behind an Act (Pickin v British Railways Board)."},{"q":"Do courts enforce constitutional conventions?","o":["No, they are political rules","Yes, like any other law","Only if written in statute","Only in the Supreme Court"],"e":"Conventions are **not legally enforceable** (**Miller 1**), even when mentioned in statute."},{"q":"The government sets employment tribunal fees so high that many people cannot afford to bring claims. Are the fees lawful?","o":["No: they unlawfully obstruct access to justice","Yes: ministers may set any fees by order","Yes: fees fall within the prerogative","No: tribunals must always be free"],"e":"The **rule of law** includes **access to the courts**; fees that effectively prevent access are unlawful (R (UNISON) v Lord Chancellor, 2017)."}]},{"id":"legalsys/crown-executive-prerogative","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 6: The Crown, central government, accountability and the royal prerogative","qs":[{"q":"In wartime the Crown takes over a hotel under the prerogative, avoiding a statute covering the same ground that requires compensation. Can it rely on the prerogative?","o":["No: the statute displaces the prerogative","Yes: the prerogative overrides statute in wartime","Yes: the Crown may choose either power","Only if Parliament is not sitting"],"e":"Where **statute covers the same ground**, the prerogative goes into **abeyance** and the statute must be used (AG v De Keyser's Royal Hotel)."},{"q":"A minister uses a prerogative power to change civil servants' conditions of service. Can the decision be judicially reviewed?","o":["Yes, if its subject matter is justiciable","No: prerogative powers are never reviewable","No: only statutory powers are reviewable","Only if Parliament approved the decision"],"e":"Reviewability depends on the **subject matter**, not the **source** of the power (CCSU v Minister for the Civil Service, GCHQ)."},{"q":"Which of these prerogative powers is non-justiciable?","o":["Making treaties","Issuing passports","Criminal injuries compensation","Aspects of the prerogative of mercy"],"e":"**Treaty-making** is an excluded category; passport decisions are reviewable (**Everett**)."},{"q":"Which Act revived the prerogative power to dissolve Parliament?","o":["Dissolution and Calling of Parliament Act 2022","Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011","Constitutional Reform Act 2005","Parliament Act 1949"],"e":"The **2022 Act** repealed the FTPA 2011 and contains an **ouster clause** in s.3."}]},{"id":"legalsys/parliament-legislation-privilege","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 7: Parliament: making and repealing legislation, and parliamentary privilege","qs":[{"q":"How long can the Lords delay a Money Bill under the Parliament Acts?","o":["One month","One year","Two sessions","Six months"],"e":"A certified **Money Bill** becomes law **one month** after reaching the Lords if not passed."},{"q":"Which Bill can never be passed using the Parliament Acts?","o":["One extending Parliament beyond five years","One changing the Lords' powers","One creating criminal offences","One on a manifesto commitment"],"e":"Bills to **extend the life of Parliament** beyond **five years** are excluded."},{"q":"Under the negative procedure, within how many days can either House annul an SI?","o":["40 days","21 days","28 days","60 days"],"e":"Negative-procedure SIs can be annulled within **40 days**."},{"q":"Which provision protects freedom of speech in proceedings in Parliament?","o":["Article 9 Bill of Rights 1689","Section 6 Human Rights Act 1998","Parliamentary Papers Act 1840","Section 1 Parliament Act 1911"],"e":"**Article 9 Bill of Rights 1689** gives absolute immunity for proceedings in Parliament."}]},{"id":"legalsys/devolution-wales","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 8: Devolution, the Senedd and the Sewel convention","qs":[{"q":"Which model of devolution has applied to Wales since the Wales Act 2017?","o":["Reserved powers model","Conferred powers model","Executive devolution only","Federal model"],"e":"The Senedd can legislate on anything **not reserved** to Westminster (Sch 7A GoWA 2006)."},{"q":"How many Members has Senedd Cymru had since the 2026 election?","o":["96","60","80","120"],"e":"The **Senedd Cymru (Members and Elections) Act 2024** increased the Senedd to **96 Members**."},{"q":"Within what period can a Senedd Bill be referred to the Supreme Court on competence?","o":["Four weeks after it is passed","Two weeks after it is passed","Three months after Royal Assent","Six weeks after introduction"],"e":"**s.112 GoWA 2006**: the Counsel General or Attorney General may refer within **four weeks**."},{"q":"How does the Senedd consent to Westminster legislating on a devolved matter?","o":["A legislative consent motion","A remedial order","A Letters Patent request","A Sewel certificate"],"e":"Under the **Sewel convention**, consent is given by a **legislative consent motion**."}]},{"id":"legalsys/public-order","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 9: Public order: processions, assemblies and breach of the peace","qs":[{"q":"How much notice must organisers give the police of a public procession?","o":["Six clear days in writing","Seven days orally or in writing","28 days in writing","48 hours in writing"],"e":"**s.11 POA 1986**: **six clear days** written notice, unless not reasonably practicable."},{"q":"How many people make a public assembly under the Public Order Act 1986?","o":["Two or more","Ten or more","Twenty or more","Fifty or more"],"e":"A public assembly is **two or more** people in a public place wholly or partly open to the air."},{"q":"For how long can a s.13 order ban public processions?","o":["Up to three months","Up to one month","Up to six months","Up to twelve months"],"e":"A **s.13** banning order lasts up to **three months** and needs Home Secretary consent."},{"q":"Police stop coaches of protesters miles from a demonstration and send them home, fearing later trouble, though no breach of the peace is about to happen. Is this lawful?","o":["No: a breach of the peace must be imminent","Yes: police may act on any reasonable fear of disorder","Yes: breach of the peace powers are unlimited","Only if the protest was banned under s.13"],"e":"Preventive action for **breach of the peace** requires it to be **imminent** (R (Laporte) v CC Gloucestershire)."}]},{"id":"legalsys/judicial-review-procedure","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 10: Judicial review: nature, amenability, standing, time limits and procedure","qs":[{"q":"What is the general time limit for a judicial review claim?","o":["Promptly and within three months","Within six weeks","Within 30 days","Within one year"],"e":"**CPR 54.5**: **promptly** and in any event within **three months** of the grounds arising."},{"q":"What is the time limit for judicial review of a planning decision?","o":["Six weeks","Three months","30 days","Two months"],"e":"Planning Acts decisions must be challenged within **six weeks**."},{"q":"What test of standing applies in judicial review?","o":["Sufficient interest","Victim status","Direct financial loss","Party to the decision"],"e":"**s.31(3) Senior Courts Act 1981**: **sufficient interest**. 'Victim' is the narrower HRA test."},{"q":"A racehorse owner is disqualified by a sports regulator whose powers come only from contracts with its members. Can the owner seek judicial review?","o":["No: the body's power is contractual, not public","Yes: the body regulates a national activity","Yes: all regulatory bodies are reviewable","Only with the Attorney General's consent"],"e":"Amenability depends on the **source and nature of the power**; a body acting under **contract** is not reviewable (R v Jockey Club ex p Aga Khan; contrast Datafin)."}]},{"id":"legalsys/judicial-review-grounds","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 11: Judicial review: grounds, remedies and ouster clauses","qs":[{"q":"A councillor deciding a planning application is a close friend of the applicant. What test decides apparent bias?","o":["Would a fair-minded, informed observer see a real possibility of bias?","Was the councillor actually biased?","Did the court itself see a real danger of bias?","Did the councillor have a financial interest?"],"e":"**Apparent bias**: would a **fair-minded and informed observer** conclude there was a **real possibility** of bias? (Porter v Magill)."},{"q":"Applying a policy rigidly without considering exceptions is which ground of review?","o":["Fettering discretion","Irrationality","Apparent bias","Improper purpose"],"e":"**Fettering discretion** (illegality): the body must listen to anyone with something new to say (**British Oxygen**)."},{"q":"A health authority promises disabled residents a 'home for life' in a care home, then decides to close it with no overriding public interest. Can the residents hold it to the promise?","o":["Yes: breaking it would be an abuse of power","No: only procedural expectations are protected","No: public bodies can always change policy","Only if they gave consideration for it"],"e":"A clear promise can create a **substantive legitimate expectation**; defeating it without an overriding public interest is an **abuse of power** (Coughlan)."},{"q":"Which Act introduced suspended quashing orders?","o":["Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022","Criminal Justice and Courts Act 2015","Senior Courts Act 1981","Constitutional Reform Act 2005"],"e":"The **JRCA 2022** inserted **s.29A Senior Courts Act 1981**."}]},{"id":"legalsys/human-rights-act","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 12: The Human Rights Act 1998 and Convention rights","qs":[{"q":"Which section of the HRA requires legislation to be read compatibly so far as possible?","o":["Section 3","Section 2","Section 4","Section 6"],"e":"**s.3**; if impossible, a higher court may make a **s.4** declaration."},{"q":"What is the normal time limit for a claim under s.7 HRA?","o":["One year","Three months","Six years","Two years"],"e":"**One year** from the act, or longer if equitable, or a shorter limit for the procedure used."},{"q":"Which is an absolute Convention right?","o":["Article 3","Article 8","Article 10","Article 5"],"e":"**Article 3** (torture, inhuman or degrading treatment) can never be justified."},{"q":"Which courts can make a declaration of incompatibility?","o":["High Court and above","Any court or tribunal","Supreme Court only","County Court and above"],"e":"Only **higher courts** (High Court and above) can make a **s.4** declaration."}]},{"id":"legalsys/eu-law-brexit","name":"Legal System, Constitutional & EU Law 13: EU law in the UK after Brexit","qs":[{"q":"On what date did the implementation period end?","o":["31 December 2020","31 January 2020","29 March 2019","1 January 2024"],"e":"**IP completion day** was **31 December 2020** at 11pm."},{"q":"What is retained EU law called from 1 January 2024?","o":["Assimilated law","Converted law","Withdrawal law","Domesticated law"],"e":"The **REUL Act 2023** renamed it **assimilated law**."},{"q":"Which EU-linked source still prevails over inconsistent domestic law?","o":["The Withdrawal Agreement","Assimilated EU regulations","General principles of EU law","Pre-2021 CJEU case law"],"e":"**s.7A EUWA 2018** gives the **Withdrawal Agreement** priority; supremacy was otherwise abolished."},{"q":"Which Act repealed the European Communities Act 1972?","o":["European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018","Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023","European Union (Future Relationship) Act 2020","European Union (Withdrawal Agreement) Act 2020"],"e":"**s.1 EUWA 2018** repealed the **ECA 1972** on exit day."}]}]},{"id":"ls","name":"Legal Services","topics":[{"id":"ls/sra-regulatory-role","name":"Legal Services 1: The SRA, the Legal Services Act 2007 and reserved activities","qs":[{"q":"How many reserved legal activities are there under the Legal Services Act 2007?","o":["Six","Four","Eight","Ten"],"e":"There are **six**: rights of audience, conduct of litigation, reserved instrument activities, probate activities, notarial activities and administration of oaths."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT a reserved legal activity?","o":["Will writing","Conduct of litigation","Probate activities","Administration of oaths"],"e":"**Will writing** is not reserved, so unregulated will writers can do it. Preparing papers for a grant of probate is reserved."},{"q":"Which body oversees the SRA and the other approved regulators?","o":["Legal Services Board","Law Society","Legal Ombudsman","Ministry of Justice"],"e":"The **Legal Services Board** is the oversight regulator. The Law Society is the approved regulator but has delegated regulation to the SRA."},{"q":"Which body alone can strike a solicitor off the roll?","o":["Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal","Solicitors Regulation Authority","Legal Ombudsman","Legal Services Board"],"e":"Only the **SDT** can strike off or suspend. The SRA can rebuke, fine and impose conditions."}]},{"id":"ls/pii-other-providers","name":"Legal Services 2: Professional indemnity insurance and other regulated providers","qs":[{"q":"What is the minimum PII cover per claim for a solicitors' LLP or company?","o":["£3 million","£2 million","£1 million","£5 million"],"e":"**£3 million** for LLPs and companies; **£2 million** for sole practitioners and partnerships."},{"q":"How long is run-off cover when a firm closes without a successor practice?","o":["Six years","Two years","Three years","Twelve years"],"e":"The SRA Minimum Terms require **six years' run-off** cover."},{"q":"What kind of loss does the SRA Compensation Fund mainly cover?","o":["Dishonesty or failure to account","Ordinary professional negligence","Delay and poor service","Excessive legal fees"],"e":"It is a discretionary **last resort** fund for losses from **dishonesty** or **failure to account**. Negligence is for PII."},{"q":"What shareholding is usually a material interest needing approval in an ABS?","o":["10% or more","25% or more","50% or more","5% or more"],"e":"Anyone with a **material interest** (generally **10%+**) in an ABS must be approved by the licensing authority."},{"q":"Which body regulates licensed conveyancers?","o":["Council for Licensed Conveyancers","CILEx Regulation","Solicitors Regulation Authority","Bar Standards Board"],"e":"The **CLC** regulates licensed conveyancers and can also license ABSs."}]},{"id":"ls/equality-act","name":"Legal Services 3: Equality Act 2010 and law firms","qs":[{"q":"How many protected characteristics are there under the Equality Act 2010?","o":["Nine","Seven","Eight","Ten"],"e":"There are **nine**, from age to sexual orientation."},{"q":"Which protected characteristic is protected only in the work context?","o":["Marriage and civil partnership","Pregnancy and maternity","Religion or belief","Gender reassignment"],"e":"**Marriage and civil partnership** is not protected for services such as legal advice to clients."},{"q":"Which type of discrimination can be justified as a proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim?","o":["Indirect discrimination","Direct sex discrimination","Direct race discrimination","Victimisation"],"e":"**Indirect** discrimination can be justified. Direct discrimination cannot, except for age."},{"q":"Who pays for a reasonable adjustment for a disabled client?","o":["The firm","The disabled client","The client and firm equally","The Legal Aid Agency"],"e":"The disabled person **cannot be charged** for a reasonable adjustment."}]},{"id":"ls/aml-framework-cdd","name":"Legal Services 4: Money laundering: purpose, scope and the MLR 2017 (due diligence)","qs":[{"q":"What shareholding makes someone a beneficial owner of a company for CDD?","o":["More than 25%","More than 10%","More than 50%","More than 5%"],"e":"A beneficial owner owns or controls **more than 25%** of the shares or voting rights."},{"q":"For how long must CDD records be kept after the business relationship ends?","o":["Five years","Three years","Six years","Ten years"],"e":"Records must be kept for **five years** after the relationship or transaction ends."},{"q":"Which step is required for a PEP but not for standard CDD?","o":["Senior management approval","Verifying the client's identity","Identifying beneficial owners","Ongoing monitoring"],"e":"For a PEP, the firm needs **senior management approval**, source of wealth and funds, and enhanced monitoring."},{"q":"If a firm relies on another firm's CDD, who is liable for any failure?","o":["The relying firm","The firm relied on","The client","Both firms equally"],"e":"Under **reg 39** the **relying firm** remains liable."},{"q":"What must a firm do if it cannot complete CDD on a new client?","o":["Not proceed and consider a SAR","Proceed but note the file","Proceed with simplified due diligence","Ask the client for an indemnity"],"e":"Under **reg 31** the firm must not proceed, must end the relationship and must **consider a SAR**."}]},{"id":"ls/poca-offences-reporting","name":"Legal Services 5: Money laundering: POCA offences, defences and reporting","qs":[{"q":"Which POCA section creates the arrangements offence?","o":["Section 328","Section 327","Section 329","Section 330"],"e":"**s.328**: entering into or becoming concerned in an arrangement facilitating criminal property."},{"q":"How long is the NCA notice period after a DAML request?","o":["7 working days","31 days","14 days","186 days"],"e":"**7 working days**. If the NCA does not refuse in that time, consent is deemed."},{"q":"What is the maximum moratorium period if the NCA refuses consent and the court extends it?","o":["186 days","31 days","90 days","365 days"],"e":"The **31-day** moratorium can be extended by the court up to **186 days** in total."},{"q":"A solicitor conducts ordinary litigation and negotiates a consensual settlement involving property that may be criminal property. Does the solicitor commit the s.328 arrangements offence?","o":["No: ordinary litigation and settlements are not arrangements","Yes: any dealing with criminal property is caught","Yes, unless a DAML is obtained first","Only if the court does not approve the settlement"],"e":"The **ordinary conduct of litigation**, including consensual settlement, is **not** an 'arrangement' under s.328; sham litigation is (Bowman v Fels)."},{"q":"What is the mental element of the s.330 failure to disclose offence?","o":["Knows, suspects or has reasonable grounds","Actual knowledge only","Actual suspicion only","Intention to assist laundering"],"e":"s.330 includes an **objective** limb: **reasonable grounds** for knowing or suspecting."}]},{"id":"ls/financial-services","name":"Legal Services 6: Financial services: FSMA 2000 and the exempt regime","qs":[{"q":"Which FSMA 2000 section contains the general prohibition?","o":["Section 19","Section 21","Section 23","Section 327"],"e":"**s.19**: no regulated activity unless authorised or exempt. s.21 covers financial promotion."},{"q":"Which of these is NOT a specified investment?","o":["A freehold house","Shares","A life insurance policy","Units in a unit trust"],"e":"**Land and buildings** themselves are not specified investments."},{"q":"What voting share percentage triggers the sale of a body corporate exclusion?","o":["50% or more","25% or more","75% or more","10% or more"],"e":"Art 70 RAO applies to deals in shares carrying **50% or more** of the voting rights."},{"q":"Under Part 20, what must a firm do with commission received from a product provider?","o":["Account to the client for it","Keep it as a fee","Pay it to the SRA","Share it with the introducer"],"e":"Part 20 requires the firm to **account to the client** for any commission."},{"q":"What may an exempt firm do when a client wants a personal pension recommendation?","o":["Introduce the client to an authorised adviser","Recommend a suitable pension itself","Recommend one if it charges no fee","Recommend one with a status disclosure"],"e":"The SRA Scope Rules prohibit recommending pensions; **introducing** to an authorised person is excluded."}]},{"id":"ls/funding-private-cfa-dba","name":"Legal Services 7: Funding: private retainers, CFAs and DBAs","qs":[{"q":"What is the maximum CFA success fee as a percentage of base costs?","o":["100%","25%","50%","35%"],"e":"The overall cap is **100% of base costs**; in PI it is further limited to 25% of PSLA and past losses."},{"q":"What is the DBA cap in employment tribunal matters?","o":["35%","25%","50%","40%"],"e":"DBA caps: **25%** PI, **35%** employment tribunal, **50%** other civil claims."},{"q":"What is the CFA success fee cap in personal injury claims?","o":["25% of PSLA and past losses","25% of all damages","50% of PSLA and past losses","100% of base costs only"],"e":"In PI the success fee is capped at **25%** of damages for PSLA and **past** pecuniary loss; future losses are excluded."},{"q":"Since April 2013, who pays a CFA success fee?","o":["The client","The losing opponent","The ATE insurer","The Legal Aid Agency"],"e":"LASPO ended recoverability, so the **client** pays it out of damages."}]},{"id":"ls/funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","name":"Legal Services 8: Funding: legal aid, third-party funding and legal expenses insurance","qs":[{"q":"A litigation funder's return is a percentage of any damages recovered. As at September 2026, how is the funding agreement treated?","o":["As a damages-based agreement, so the DBA rules apply","As a conditional fee agreement","As outside all statutory regulation","As void in every type of claim"],"e":"A percentage-of-damages funding agreement is a **DBA** and unenforceable unless it complies with the DBA regime; not yet reversed by legislation (R (PACCAR) v CAT, 2023)."},{"q":"What lets the Legal Aid Agency recover its costs from property a client recovers?","o":["The statutory charge","The merits test","Costs protection","Exceptional case funding"],"e":"The **statutory charge** applies to money or property recovered or preserved."},{"q":"What does ATE insurance mainly cover?","o":["The opponent's costs if the client loses","The client's own solicitor's fees if they win","The CFA success fee","Court fees on appeal only"],"e":"**ATE** covers adverse costs and often the client's own disbursements if they lose."},{"q":"When does a client with BTE insurance have the right to choose their own lawyer?","o":["Once proceedings are issued","Only at the start of the claim","Only after trial","Never"],"e":"Under the 1990 Regulations the insured can **choose their own lawyer** once proceedings are issued (or there is a conflict)."}]},{"id":"ls/transparency-client-care","name":"Legal Services 9: Transparency Rules, client care and costs information","qs":[{"q":"Which service needs published price information under the SRA Transparency Rules?","o":["Residential conveyancing","Commercial leases","Will drafting","Family proceedings"],"e":"**Residential conveyancing** is a listed service. Wills and commercial leases are not."},{"q":"Which information must every SRA-authorised firm publish, whatever services it offers?","o":["Its complaints procedure","Its hourly rates","Its annual turnover","Its partners' salaries"],"e":"**Complaints** information must be published by all firms; price information only for listed services."},{"q":"Which Code paragraph requires the best possible information about likely overall cost?","o":["Para 8.7","Para 8.1","Para 6.3","Para 7.7"],"e":"**Para 8.7**: best possible information on pricing and likely overall cost."},{"q":"How long is the cancellation period for off-premises consumer contracts?","o":["14 days","7 days","28 days","30 days"],"e":"The Consumer Contracts Regulations 2013 give a **14-day** cancellation right."}]}]},{"id":"property","name":"Property Law and Practice","topics":[{"id":"property/transaction-overview","name":"Property Law and Practice 1: The freehold transaction, the Protocol and ethics","qs":[{"q":"Which Law Society form is the seller's main Property Information Form in a residential sale?","o":["TA6","TA10","TA13","TR1"],"e":"**TA6** is the Property Information Form. TA10 covers fittings and contents; TA13 is completion information."},{"q":"For which firms is the Law Society Conveyancing Protocol compulsory?","o":["Members of the Conveyancing Quality Scheme","All firms doing residential conveyancing","Firms acting on commercial sales only","Firms with fewer than ten partners"],"e":"The Protocol is good practice for residential work and is **compulsory** for firms in the **CQS**."},{"q":"Which set of enquiries is used in commercial property transactions instead of the TA forms?","o":["CPSE","CON29","LLC1","SCPC"],"e":"Commercial transactions use the **Commercial Property Standard Enquiries (CPSE)**."},{"q":"What must a solicitor do on suspecting money laundering in a conveyancing matter?","o":["Report to the firm's MLRO without tipping off","Warn the client and ask for an explanation","Complete the matter and then report it","Report directly to the lender only"],"e":"A suspicion must be reported to the **MLRO**, and the client must not be **tipped off**."}]},{"id":"property/title","name":"Property Law and Practice 2: Investigating and reporting on title","qs":[{"q":"Which part of the register shows the class of title and any restrictions?","o":["Proprietorship register","Property register","Charges register","Title plan"],"e":"The **proprietorship register** shows the owner, the class of title and **restrictions**."},{"q":"How old must a good root of title be in unregistered land?","o":["At least 15 years","At least 12 years","At least 20 years","At least 30 years"],"e":"The epitome must start from a **good root** at least **15 years** old."},{"q":"What does a Form A restriction on the proprietorship register indicate?","o":["A tenancy in common","A joint tenancy","A pending bankruptcy","A registered option"],"e":"A **Form A restriction** shows a **tenancy in common**, so capital money must be paid to two trustees to overreach."},{"q":"Where one co-owner under a Form A restriction has died, who must the buyer pay to overreach?","o":["Two trustees","The surviving co-owner alone","The deceased's estate only","The mortgage lender"],"e":"Overreaching needs payment to **two trustees**, so the survivor appoints a second trustee."}]},{"id":"property/searches","name":"Property Law and Practice 3: Pre-contract searches and enquiries","qs":[{"q":"Which search reveals tree preservation orders and listed building status?","o":["LLC1 local land charges search","CON29DW drainage and water search","Index map search","Chancel repair search"],"e":"The **LLC1** register of local land charges shows TPOs, planning conditions, listing and conservation areas."},{"q":"Since what date has chancel repair liability ceased to be an overriding interest?","o":["13 October 2013","1 January 1996","13 October 2003","1 April 2018"],"e":"Since **13 October 2013** chancel repair is not overriding, though it can bind until a registered disposition for value."},{"q":"Which search shows whether the property is connected to mains water and public sewers?","o":["CON29DW","CON29M","LLC1","CON29 Part II"],"e":"The **CON29DW** drainage and water search covers mains water, public sewers and sewers within the boundary."},{"q":"Which principle means the seller need not disclose physical defects to the buyer?","o":["Caveat emptor","Uberrimae fidei","Nemo dat","Res ipsa loquitur"],"e":"**Caveat emptor**: the buyer must make its own searches, enquiries and survey."}]},{"id":"property/finance-lender","name":"Property Law and Practice 4: Finance and acting for a lender","qs":[{"q":"Which lender instructions do most residential lenders use?","o":["UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook","CPSE lender schedule","Law Society Conveyancing Protocol","Standard Conditions of Sale"],"e":"Most lenders use the **UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook** (Part 1 general, Part 2 lender-specific)."},{"q":"Under the Handbook, the lender must be told if the seller has owned the property for less than how long?","o":["6 months","3 months","12 months","2 years"],"e":"Ownership by the seller of **less than 6 months** may affect valuation and must be reported."},{"q":"Within what period must a company's charge be registered at Companies House?","o":["21 days","14 days","30 working days","2 months"],"e":"A company charge must be registered within **21 days** (s.859A CA 2006) or it is void against a liquidator, administrator and creditors."},{"q":"What must you do if the buyer refuses to let you disclose a price reduction to the lender?","o":["Stop acting for the lender","Disclose it to the lender anyway","Continue acting and say nothing","Ask the seller to tell the lender"],"e":"There is a conflict between **confidentiality** and **disclosure**: you must stop acting for the lender (and probably the buyer)."}]},{"id":"property/contract","name":"Property Law and Practice 5: Drafting the contract: standard conditions","qs":[{"q":"Under the SCS, when is completion if no date is agreed?","o":["20 working days after exchange","10 working days after exchange","28 days after exchange","30 working days after exchange"],"e":"The SCS default is **20 working days** after exchange, at **2pm**."},{"q":"Under the SCS, in what capacity does the seller's solicitor hold the deposit by default?","o":["Stakeholder","Agent for the seller","Agent for the buyer","Trustee for the lender"],"e":"The deposit is held as **stakeholder** unless agreed otherwise; in a residential chain it may be used for a related purchase deposit."},{"q":"Under the SCPC 3rd edition, who bears the risk of damage to the property after exchange?","o":["The buyer","The seller","The seller's lender","The stakeholder"],"e":"Under the **SCPC** (condition 8.1) the **buyer** bears risk from exchange. The SCS 5th edn is the same (SC 5.1.1), so the buyer should insure from exchange."},{"q":"Under the SCPC, is the price inclusive or exclusive of VAT?","o":["Exclusive: buyer pays VAT on top","Inclusive of any VAT","Exclusive: seller absorbs the VAT","VAT is never payable"],"e":"The **SCPC** price is **exclusive** of VAT; the SCS price is inclusive."}]},{"id":"property/exchange","name":"Property Law and Practice 6: Exchange of contracts","qs":[{"q":"Which Law Society formula applies where one solicitor holds both signed parts of the contract?","o":["Formula A","Formula B","Formula C","Formula D"],"e":"**Formula A**: one solicitor (usually the seller's) already holds both parts."},{"q":"Which Law Society formula is designed for chains, with a release stage and a later exchange?","o":["Formula C","Formula A","Formula B","Formula E"],"e":"**Formula C** has two parts: an undertaking to exchange if called on, then actual exchange."},{"q":"When is a contract made on an exchange by post?","o":["When the last part is posted","When the last part is received","When both parts are signed","When the deposit clears"],"e":"On postal exchange the contract is made when the **last part is posted**."},{"q":"How can the buyer's equitable interest after exchange be protected in unregistered land?","o":["A C(iv) land charge","A Form A restriction","A D(ii) land charge","A unilateral caution"],"e":"An estate contract is protected by a **C(iv) land charge** (or a notice in registered land)."}]},{"id":"property/pre-completion","name":"Property Law and Practice 7: Pre-completion steps","qs":[{"q":"Which Land Registry form transfers the whole of a registered title?","o":["TR1","TP1","AP1","OS1"],"e":"**TR1** transfers the whole; **TP1** transfers part."},{"q":"How long is the priority period given by an OS1 search?","o":["30 working days","15 working days","30 calendar days","21 days"],"e":"An **OS1** gives **30 working days** priority to apply to register."},{"q":"How long is the priority protection given by a K15 Land Charges search?","o":["15 working days","30 working days","10 working days","2 months"],"e":"A **K15** gives **15 working days**: complete within that period."},{"q":"Which search is made against the buyer for the lender's protection?","o":["K16 bankruptcy search","K15 land charges search","OS2 search of part","Index map search"],"e":"A **K16 bankruptcy search** checks the borrower is not bankrupt."}]},{"id":"property/completion","name":"Property Law and Practice 8: Completion and post-completion","qs":[{"q":"Within what period must an SDLT return be filed and the tax paid?","o":["14 days of the effective date","30 days of the effective date","21 days of completion","60 days of completion"],"e":"**SDLT**: **14 days**. LTT in Wales is 30 days."},{"q":"Within what period must an application for first registration be made?","o":["2 months of completion","30 working days of completion","21 days of completion","6 months of completion"],"e":"**s.6 LRA 2002**: **2 months**, or legal title reverts to the seller on bare trust."},{"q":"Under the Code for Completion by Post, in what capacity does the seller's solicitor complete?","o":["As the buyer's solicitor's agent","As agent for the lender only","As stakeholder for both parties","As the seller's attorney"],"e":"The seller's solicitor acts as the **buyer's solicitor's agent** for completion, without charge."},{"q":"Within what period must an LTT return be made in Wales?","o":["30 days","14 days","21 days","60 days"],"e":"**LTT** return and payment go to the **Welsh Revenue Authority** within **30 days**."}]},{"id":"property/delayed-completion","name":"Property Law and Practice 9: Delayed completion and remedies","qs":[{"q":"Under the SCS, how long does a recipient have to comply with a notice to complete?","o":["10 working days","20 working days","14 days","5 working days"],"e":"Under the SCS the recipient must complete within **10 working days**, and time becomes of the essence."},{"q":"Under SCS 7.2, at what rate is compensation for late completion calculated?","o":["The contract rate","Bank of England base rate","The judgment debt rate","The lender's mortgage rate"],"e":"Compensation is at the **contract rate** on the price (less deposit if the buyer pays), based on relative fault."},{"q":"Under SCPC 10.3, who can be liable to pay contractual compensation for late completion?","o":["Only the buyer","Only the seller","Either party","Neither party"],"e":"Under **SCPC 10.3** only the **buyer** pays; a late seller is liable only for common law damages."},{"q":"What must a party be before it can serve a valid notice to complete?","o":["Ready, able and willing to complete","Already in receipt of the deposit","Holding both parts of the contract","Registered as proprietor"],"e":"The server must itself be **ready, able and willing** to complete."}]},{"id":"property/lease-contents","name":"Property Law and Practice 10: Commercial leases: key clauses and the Code","qs":[{"q":"A lease sets a timetable for the landlord to trigger a rent review but says nothing about time. The landlord starts the review late. Can the review still go ahead?","o":["Yes: time is presumed not of the essence","No: time limits are always of the essence","No: the right to review is lost","Only if the tenant consents"],"e":"Time is **not of the essence** for rent review unless the lease says so or it is linked to a **break clause** (United Scientific Holdings v Burnley BC)."},{"q":"Which provision implies that consent to improvements under a qualified covenant is not to be unreasonably withheld?","o":["s.19(2) LTA 1927","s.19(3) LTA 1927","s.1 LTA 1988","s.146 LPA 1925"],"e":"**s.19(2) LTA 1927** covers improvements; s.19(3) covers user."},{"q":"What does s.19(3) LTA 1927 prevent under a qualified user covenant?","o":["Charging a fine for consent","Refusing consent at all","Unreasonably withholding consent","Requiring a planning check"],"e":"**s.19(3)** bars a **fine** for consent but implies **no** reasonableness requirement."},{"q":"A tenant pays a quarter's rent in advance, then validly breaks the lease mid-quarter. The lease is silent on refunds. Can it recover the rent for the period after the break?","o":["No: no refund term is implied","Yes: a refund is implied by law","Yes: rent is apportioned by statute","Only if the landlord re-lets"],"e":"No term is implied to refund rent paid in advance for the period after a break; the lease must provide for it (Marks & Spencer v BNP Paribas)."}]},{"id":"property/lease-dealings","name":"Property Law and Practice 11: Grant, assignment and underletting of leases","qs":[{"q":"A lease of more than how many years must be registered with its own title?","o":["7 years","3 years","21 years","12 years"],"e":"Leases of **more than 7 years** need their own registered title."},{"q":"Which statute requires a landlord to give consent to assign within a reasonable time, with written reasons?","o":["Landlord and Tenant Act 1988","Landlord and Tenant Act 1927","Landlord and Tenant Act 1954","LTCA 1995"],"e":"**s.1 LTA 1988**: reasonable time, written reasons, burden of proof on the landlord."},{"q":"Within what time must a landlord serve a s.17 notice to claim a fixed charge from a former tenant?","o":["6 months","4 weeks","3 months","12 months"],"e":"A **s.17 LTCA 1995** notice must be served within **6 months** of the sum falling due."},{"q":"From what date are new leases governed by the LTCA 1995 release rules?","o":["1 January 1996","1 January 1990","1 September 2020","31 January 2025"],"e":"Leases granted on or after **1 January 1996** are 'new leases': the tenant is released on lawful assignment."}]},{"id":"property/lease-remedies","name":"Property Law and Practice 12: Remedies for breach and ending a commercial lease","qs":[{"q":"How much rent must be owed before CRAR can be used?","o":["At least 7 days' rent","At least 14 days' rent","At least one quarter's rent","At least 30 days' rent"],"e":"**CRAR** needs at least **7 days' rent** owed and 7 clear days' notice of enforcement."},{"q":"What caps damages for a tenant's breach of repairing covenant?","o":["Diminution in value of the reversion","Full cost of the repairs","Three years' rent","The tenant's deposit"],"e":"**s.18(1) LTA 1927** caps damages at the **diminution in value of the reversion**."},{"q":"What type of clause lets a landlord do repairs itself and recover the cost as a debt?","o":["Jervis v Harris clause","Schedule of condition","Rent suspension clause","Keep open clause"],"e":"A **Jervis v Harris** clause recovers cost as a **debt**, avoiding s.18 and the 1938 Act."},{"q":"Which notice must normally precede forfeiture for breaches other than non-payment of rent?","o":["s.146 LPA 1925 notice","s.17 LTCA 1995 notice","s.25 LTA 1954 notice","s.8 LTCA 1995 notice"],"e":"A **s.146 notice** is needed for non-rent breaches, but not for rent."}]},{"id":"property/lta1954","name":"Property Law and Practice 13: Security of tenure: LTA 1954 Part II","qs":[{"q":"A landlord's s.25 notice must be given how far before the termination date?","o":["6 to 12 months","3 to 6 months","12 to 18 months","2 to 6 months"],"e":"A **s.25** notice: not less than **6** nor more than **12 months** before the stated date."},{"q":"How long does a landlord have to serve a counter-notice to a tenant's s.26 request?","o":["2 months","1 month","3 months","14 days"],"e":"The landlord has **2 months**, otherwise it cannot later oppose renewal."},{"q":"How long must a warning notice be served before the tenant is bound to allow a simple declaration?","o":["At least 14 days","At least 7 days","At least 28 days","At least 3 months"],"e":"**14 days** or more: simple declaration; less: a **statutory declaration**."},{"q":"For ground (g), how long must the landlord have owned its interest?","o":["5 years","3 years","10 years","14 years"],"e":"**s.30(2)**: the landlord must have owned its interest for **5 years** before the end of the tenancy."},{"q":"What is the maximum term a court can order for a new lease under the 1954 Act?","o":["15 years","10 years","14 years","25 years"],"e":"**s.33**: up to **15 years**."}]},{"id":"property/planning","name":"Property Law and Practice 14: Planning and building regulations","qs":[{"q":"Which section of the TCPA 1990 defines development?","o":["s.55","s.57","s.106","s.171"],"e":"**s.55** defines development; **s.57** requires permission for it."},{"q":"Within what period must development under a full planning permission begin?","o":["3 years","2 years","5 years","1 year"],"e":"A **full** permission must be begun within **3 years**."},{"q":"What is the enforcement time limit in England for breaches occurring on or after 25 April 2024?","o":["10 years for all breaches","4 years for all breaches","4 years for operational development","12 months for all breaches"],"e":"The **LURA 2023** applies a **10-year** limit to all new breaches in England."},{"q":"What removes permitted development rights in a particular area?","o":["An Article 4 direction","A breach of condition notice","A stop notice","A lawful development certificate"],"e":"An **Article 4 direction** (or a planning condition) can remove GPDO rights."}]},{"id":"property/tax","name":"Property Law and Practice 15: Property taxes: SDLT, LTT, VAT and CGT","qs":[{"q":"How long is an option to tax generally irrevocable for?","o":["20 years","10 years","3 years","6 months"],"e":"An option to tax is generally irrevocable for **20 years**, after a 6-month cooling-off period."},{"q":"What is the VAT treatment of the sale of a freehold commercial building under three years old?","o":["Standard-rated","Exempt","Zero-rated","Outside the scope of VAT"],"e":"A **new** commercial freehold (under **3 years**) is compulsorily **standard-rated**."},{"q":"Within what period must a CGT return be made on a residential property disposal?","o":["60 days of completion","30 days of completion","14 days of completion","12 months of completion"],"e":"Residential disposals: report and pay CGT within **60 days**."},{"q":"Which final period of ownership always qualifies for Private Residence Relief?","o":["9 months","18 months","36 months","3 months"],"e":"The **final 9 months** always qualify (36 months for disabled people or those going into care)."}]}]},{"id":"tort","name":"Tort","topics":[{"id":"tort/negligence","name":"Tort 1: Negligence: duty of care and breach","qs":[{"q":"A driver negligently injures a pedestrian. Must the court apply the three-stage Caparo test to decide whether a duty of care is owed?","o":["No: it is an established duty category","Yes: Caparo applies to every negligence claim","Yes, but only the proximity stage","No: drivers owe duties only to other drivers"],"e":"In an **established category** a duty exists without more; **Caparo** is used only in **novel** situations (Robinson v CC West Yorkshire)."},{"q":"What standard of care applies to a learner driver?","o":["A reasonably competent qualified driver","A reasonable learner driver","A reasonable driver of similar age","The driver's own personal best"],"e":"**Nettleship v Weston**: no allowance is made for inexperience; the standard is objective."},{"q":"A surgeon does not mention a small risk of serious complication that a reasonable patient would want to know about, though many surgeons would not mention it. How is breach judged?","o":["By whether the risk was material to a reasonable patient","By whether a responsible body of doctors would not disclose it","By whether the risk was above 10%","By what the surgeon honestly thought best"],"e":"Doctors must disclose **material risks**: those a **reasonable patient** would think significant. Bolam does not apply to risk disclosure (Montgomery)."},{"q":"A cricket ball is hit out of a ground, which had happened about six times in 30 years, and injures a passer-by. Was the club in breach of duty?","o":["No: the risk was so small it could reasonably be ignored","Yes: the harm was foreseeable","Yes: sports clubs owe a strict duty","No: passers-by are owed no duty"],"e":"Breach weighs the **likelihood of harm**; a very **small risk** may reasonably be ignored (Bolton v Stone)."},{"q":"Which statute makes a relevant criminal conviction admissible as evidence of breach in a civil claim?","o":["Civil Evidence Act 1968 s.11","Compensation Act 2006 s.1","SARAH Act 2015","Law Reform Act 1945 s.1"],"e":"**Civil Evidence Act 1968 s.11** allows a conviction to be used as evidence in civil proceedings."}]},{"id":"tort/causation-remoteness","name":"Tort 2: Causation and remoteness","qs":[{"q":"A doctor negligently fails to examine a patient, who dies of arsenic poisoning that would have killed him even with proper treatment. Is the doctor liable for the death?","o":["No: but-for causation fails","Yes: failing to examine was a breach","Yes: the hospital owed a duty of care","Only for a proportion of the loss"],"e":"**But-for test**: the death would have happened anyway, so the breach did not cause it (Barnett)."},{"q":"Under the Compensation Act 2006 s.3, how is liability for mesothelioma allocated between defendants?","o":["Jointly and severally","Apportioned by length of exposure","Shared equally per defendant","Borne only by the last employer"],"e":"**s.3** makes each defendant who materially increased the risk liable in full (**joint and several**)."},{"q":"D negligently injures C's back. Before trial, C develops an unrelated natural spinal disease that would have disabled him anyway. How does this affect D's damages?","o":["They are limited to the period before the disease","No effect: D stays liable for the whole loss","D's liability ends at the accident date","They are increased for the extra suffering"],"e":"A **supervening natural illness** is taken into account (Jobling); a later **tort** does not reduce the first tortfeasor's liability (Baker v Willoughby)."},{"q":"Oil negligently spilled in a harbour is ignited by a freak chain of events and burns a wharf. Fire damage was not reasonably foreseeable, though fouling was. Is the defendant liable for the fire damage?","o":["No: that type of damage was not reasonably foreseeable","Yes: the fire was a direct consequence","Yes: some damage to the wharf was foreseeable","Yes: the defendant takes the victim as found"],"e":"**Remoteness**: liability only for damage of a **reasonably foreseeable type**, not all direct consequences (The Wagon Mound (No 1))."},{"q":"D negligently causes a minor burn to C's lip. Because of a pre-malignant condition, the burn triggers cancer and C dies. Is D liable for the death?","o":["Yes: D takes the victim as he finds him","No: cancer was not a foreseeable type of harm","No: the pre-existing condition broke the chain","Only for the burn itself"],"e":"**Eggshell skull rule**: if the type of injury (a burn) was foreseeable, D is liable for its **full extent** (Smith v Leech Brain)."}]},{"id":"tort/economic-loss","name":"Tort 3: Pure economic loss and negligent misstatement","qs":[{"q":"In Spartan Steel v Martin, which loss was NOT recoverable?","o":["Profit on melts not processed","Value of the damaged melt","Profit on the damaged melt","Physical damage to the furnace"],"e":"**Spartan Steel**: loss flowing from physical damage was recoverable, but lost profit on future melts was **pure economic loss**."},{"q":"A council negligently approves defective foundations. Cracks appear and the owner sells at a loss; no one is hurt and no other property is damaged. Can the owner recover the loss in negligence?","o":["No: the loss is pure economic loss","Yes: the building has been physically damaged","Yes: councils owe a duty to all homeowners","Only the cost of repair, not the loss on sale"],"e":"Damage to the **defective thing itself** is **pure economic loss**, not recoverable in negligence (Murphy v Brentwood)."},{"q":"A solicitor negligently delays drafting a will and the client dies before signing it, so the intended beneficiary gets nothing. Can the beneficiary sue the solicitor?","o":["Yes: the solicitor owes a duty to the intended beneficiary","No: only the client could sue, in contract","No: pure economic loss is never recoverable","No: the beneficiary relied on nothing the solicitor said"],"e":"An exception to the economic loss rule: a solicitor owes a **duty to an intended beneficiary** of a will (White v Jones)."},{"q":"A mortgage valuer knows the buyer of a modest house will rely on the valuation, but includes a disclaimer of liability. The valuation is negligent. Will the disclaimer protect the valuer?","o":["No: the disclaimer is unreasonable, so unenforceable","Yes: a disclaimer always negates a Hedley Byrne duty","Yes: the valuer had no contract with the buyer","Yes: the buyer could have got their own survey"],"e":"A valuer's disclaimer to a buyer of a **modest house** who relies on it fails the **reasonableness** test (Smith v Eric S Bush, under UCTA)."}]},{"id":"tort/psychiatric-harm","name":"Tort 4: Psychiatric harm","qs":[{"q":"C is in a car crash caused by D's negligence. C is physically unhurt but the shock triggers a recognised psychiatric illness. Can C recover?","o":["Yes: physical injury was foreseeable, so C is a primary victim","No: psychiatric injury itself was not foreseeable","No: C must satisfy the Alcock control mechanisms","Only if C saw a loved one injured"],"e":"A **primary victim** in the zone of physical danger recovers if **physical injury was foreseeable**; psychiatric harm need not be (Page v Smith)."},{"q":"For which relationship is a close tie of love and affection presumed under Alcock?","o":["Parent and child","Brother and sister","Close friends","Grandparent and grandchild"],"e":"**Alcock** presumes the tie for parents/children, spouses and fiancé(e)s. Siblings and others must prove it."},{"q":"What did Paul v Royal Wolverhampton NHS Trust (2024) require a secondary victim to witness?","o":["An accident","The negligent act itself","The victim's death","A medical diagnosis"],"e":"**Paul** (UKSC 2024): there must be an **accident**, an external event. A medical crisis from negligent treatment is not enough."},{"q":"How are rescuers treated for psychiatric harm claims?","o":["No special status","Automatically primary victims","Automatically secondary victims","Barred from claiming"],"e":"**White v CC South Yorkshire**: rescuers recover only as primary victims or by meeting the secondary-victim rules."}]},{"id":"tort/employers-liability","name":"Tort 5: Employers' primary liability","qs":[{"q":"An employer delegates safety to a competent manager, who sets up an unsafe system of work, and an employee is injured. Can the employer escape liability by showing it delegated properly?","o":["No: the duty is personal and non-delegable","Yes: it chose a competent manager","Yes: only the manager is liable","Yes, if the employee was also careless"],"e":"The employer's duty (competent staff, adequate plant, **safe place, safe system**) is **non-delegable** (Wilsons & Clyde Coal v English)."},{"q":"Which Act deems the employer negligent where defective equipment was due to a third party's fault?","o":["Employers' Liability (Defective Equipment) Act 1969","Employers' Liability (Compulsory Insurance) Act 1969","Consumer Protection Act 1987","Health and Safety at Work Act 1974"],"e":"The **Employers' Liability (Defective Equipment) Act 1969** deems the employer negligent; it can seek contribution from the manufacturer."},{"q":"Which provision removed civil liability for breach of most health and safety regulations?","o":["ERRA 2013 s.69","Compensation Act 2006 s.1","SARAH Act 2015 s.2","Civil Evidence Act 1968 s.11"],"e":"**ERRA 2013 s.69** (breaches from 1 Oct 2013): the employee must now prove **negligence**."},{"q":"An employee known for years for dangerous horseplay, never disciplined, trips a colleague and injures him. Is the employer liable for breach of its own duty?","o":["Yes: it failed to provide competent fellow employees","No: horseplay is not work","No: employers are never liable for deliberate acts","Only if the employer encouraged the horseplay"],"e":"Breach of the duty to provide **competent fellow employees**: the employer knew of the risk and did nothing (Hudson v Ridge Manufacturing)."}]},{"id":"tort/vicarious-liability","name":"Tort 6: Vicarious liability","qs":[{"q":"A prisoner working in a prison kitchen negligently injures a catering manager. Can the prison service be vicariously liable though the prisoner is not an employee?","o":["Yes: the relationship is akin to employment","No: vicarious liability needs a contract of employment","No: prisoners work involuntarily","Only if the prisoner was paid the minimum wage"],"e":"A relationship **akin to employment** (work integral to D's business and for its benefit) can found vicarious liability (Cox v MoJ)."},{"q":"A bank sends job applicants for medicals to a self-employed doctor with his own practice and other clients. He assaults some applicants. Is the bank vicariously liable?","o":["No: the doctor was in business on his own account","Yes: the relationship was akin to employment","Yes: the bank chose and directed him","Yes: the bank paid for each examination"],"e":"No vicarious liability for a genuine **independent contractor** carrying on his own business (Various Claimants v Barclays Bank)."},{"q":"Why was the employer not liable in WM Morrison v Various Claimants (2020)?","o":["Employee pursued a personal vendetta","No employment relationship existed","Data leak caused no damage","Employee acted on instructions"],"e":"**WM Morrison** (2020): leaking payroll data to pursue a **personal vendetta** was not closely connected to the job."},{"q":"A milkman, forbidden to give lifts, lets a boy ride along to help deliver milk, and injures him by negligent driving. Is the employer vicariously liable?","o":["Yes: it was an authorised task done in a prohibited way","No: lifts were expressly prohibited","No: the boy was a trespasser on the float","Only if the employer knew of the lifts"],"e":"A prohibited **way of doing an authorised task** remains in the course of employment, especially where it furthers the employer's business (Rose v Plenty)."}]},{"id":"tort/defences","name":"Tort 7: Defences: consent, contributory negligence, illegality","qs":[{"q":"Which provision stops volenti being used against a passenger in a vehicle requiring compulsory insurance?","o":["Road Traffic Act 1988 s.149","Law Reform Act 1945 s.1","Compensation Act 2006 s.1","OLA 1957 s.2(5)"],"e":"**RTA 1988 s.149** bars volenti against passengers, even one who knew the driver was drunk."},{"q":"Under Froom v Butcher, what reduction applies if a seatbelt would have prevented injury altogether?","o":["25%","15%","20%","50%"],"e":"**Froom v Butcher**: 25% if injury prevented entirely, 15% if less severe, nil if no difference."},{"q":"Which Act governs the partial defence of contributory negligence?","o":["Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945","Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1934","Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978","Compensation Act 2006"],"e":"The **1945 Act s.1** reduces damages to the extent that is **just and equitable**."},{"q":"A claimant injured while jointly committing a crime sues his co-offender in negligence. How does the court decide whether illegality bars the claim?","o":["Weighing the purpose of the prohibition, other policies and proportionality","Asking only whether the claimant must rely on the illegality","Applying a fixed rule that any crime bars the claim","Reducing damages by a fixed 50%"],"e":"Illegality is **policy-based**: purpose of the prohibition, other relevant policies, and **proportionality** (Patel v Mirza)."}]},{"id":"tort/remedies","name":"Tort 8: Remedies for personal injury and death; limitation","qs":[{"q":"What is the personal injury discount rate in England and Wales from 11 January 2025?","o":["+0.5%","-0.25%","-0.75%","+1.0%"],"e":"The rate was set at **+0.5%** from 11 January 2025, used with the Ogden Tables."},{"q":"What is the current statutory bereavement award under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976?","o":["£15,120","£12,980","£11,800","£20,000"],"e":"**FAA 1976 s.1A**: a fixed **£15,120** for spouses/civil partners, 2+ year cohabitants or parents of an unmarried minor."},{"q":"What is the basic limitation period for a personal injury claim?","o":["3 years","6 years","2 years","1 year"],"e":"**LA 1980 ss.11 and 14**: 3 years from the cause of action or date of knowledge; s.33 allows disapplication."},{"q":"What is the longstop for latent damage claims in negligence (not personal injury)?","o":["15 years","10 years","12 years","6 years"],"e":"**LA 1980 s.14B**: a **15-year longstop** from the negligent act."}]},{"id":"tort/occupiers-liability","name":"Tort 9: Occupiers' liability (1957 and 1984 Acts)","qs":[{"q":"A brewery owns a pub run by a manager living upstairs; the brewery keeps the right to do repairs. A visitor falls on the private staircase. Who can be an occupier?","o":["Both the brewery and the manager","Only the brewery, as owner","Only the manager, as resident","Neither, as the staircase is private"],"e":"An occupier is anyone with **sufficient control**; there can be **more than one** (Wheat v Lacon)."},{"q":"Two chimney sweeps die from carbon monoxide while sealing a flue, despite the occupier's warning. Is the occupier liable?","o":["No: skilled visitors must guard against risks of their calling","Yes: occupiers owe visitors a strict duty","Yes: a warning never discharges the duty","Only if the sweeps were unpaid"],"e":"**OLA 1957 s.2(3)(b)**: a person exercising a calling is expected to **guard against special risks** incident to it (Roles v Nathan)."},{"q":"Which type of loss is NOT recoverable under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984?","o":["Property damage","Personal injury","Death","Psychiatric injury"],"e":"**OLA 1984 s.1(8)**: the duty covers **injury only**, not property damage."},{"q":"An adult dives into a shallow lake in a council park, despite 'no swimming' signs, and is paralysed. Is the council liable?","o":["No: no duty to protect against obvious risks freely run","Yes: the council knew people swam there","Yes: signs are never enough","Only if the council failed to fence the lake"],"e":"No duty to protect **adults** from **obvious risks** they **freely choose** to run (Tomlinson v Congleton BC)."}]},{"id":"tort/product-liability","name":"Tort 10: Product liability","qs":[{"q":"What is the minimum value for a property damage claim under the CPA 1987?","o":["Over £275","Over £500","Over £1,000","Over £100"],"e":"**CPA 1987 s.5**: property damage claims must exceed **£275**."},{"q":"Under CPA 1987 s.3, when is a product defective?","o":["Safety below what persons generally expect","Producer failed to take reasonable care","Not of satisfactory quality","Not fit for its purpose"],"e":"**s.3**: safety is not such as **persons generally are entitled to expect**. Fault is not required."},{"q":"What is the longstop for claims under the Consumer Protection Act 1987?","o":["10 years from circulation","15 years from the defect","6 years from damage","3 years from circulation"],"e":"3 years from damage or knowledge, subject to an absolute **10-year longstop** from when the product was put into circulation."},{"q":"When does a retailer become liable as a supplier under the CPA 1987?","o":["Fails to identify producer when asked","Sells the product in its shop","Labels the product with prices","Fails to inspect the product"],"e":"**s.2(3)**: a supplier is liable only if it fails to identify the producer or importer within a reasonable time after request."}]},{"id":"tort/nuisance-rylands","name":"Tort 11: Nuisance and Rylands v Fletcher","qs":[{"q":"A homeowner's adult son, who lives at home but has no interest in the property, suffers noise and dust from a neighbour's works. Can the son sue in private nuisance?","o":["No: he has no proprietary interest in the land","Yes: he lives at the property","Yes: anyone affected can sue","Only with his parents' consent"],"e":"Only those with a **proprietary interest** or exclusive possession can sue in private nuisance (Hunter v Canary Wharf)."},{"q":"How many years of actionable nuisance are needed for prescription?","o":["20 years","12 years","10 years","15 years"],"e":"**Prescription** requires 20 years of actionable nuisance. Coming to the nuisance is not a defence."},{"q":"A council's ordinary water supply pipe bursts, without negligence, and the escaping water damages neighbouring land. Is the council strictly liable under Rylands v Fletcher?","o":["No: an ordinary water pipe is not a non-natural use","Yes: water is likely to do mischief if it escapes","Yes: any escape of something brought onto land suffices","No: Rylands applies only to fire"],"e":"**Non-natural use** means **extraordinary and unusual**; ordinary water pipes do not qualify (Transco v Stockport MBC)."},{"q":"Visitors on a gallery's viewing platform constantly look into and photograph the inside of neighbouring glass-walled flats. Can the flat owners sue in private nuisance?","o":["Yes: intrusive overlooking can be an actionable nuisance","No: overlooking can never be a nuisance","No: glass walls make the owners hypersensitive","Only if the platform lacked planning permission"],"e":"Intrusive **overlooking** that substantially interferes with **use and enjoyment** can be a nuisance (Fearn v Tate Gallery, 2023)."}]}]},{"id":"trusts","name":"Trusts Law","topics":[{"id":"trusts/certainties","name":"Trusts Law 1: Express trusts: the three certainties; fixed and discretionary trusts","qs":[{"q":"Trustees have a discretion to pay income among 'my employees and their relatives and dependants'. Which test of certainty of objects applies?","o":["Can it be said of any person whether they are in the class?","Can every member of the class be listed?","Can at least one person be shown to be in it?","Do the trustees know every member's address?"],"e":"For **discretionary trusts**: the **'is or is not'** test (McPhail v Doulton). Fixed trusts need a complete list."},{"q":"What test of certainty of objects applies to a fixed trust?","o":["The complete list test","The is or is not test","The one person test","The reasonable trustee test"],"e":"A fixed trust needs a **complete list** of beneficiaries (IRC v Broadway Cottages)."},{"q":"S declares himself trustee of 50 of his 950 identical shares in one company, without saying which. Is the trust certain?","o":["Yes: identical shares need no segregation","No: the shares must be segregated","No: it fails for uncertain objects","Only if the certificates are numbered"],"e":"Intangible, identical **shares** of one class need not be segregated, unlike tangible goods (Hunter v Moss; contrast Re London Wine)."},{"q":"What do precatory words such as 'in full confidence' usually create?","o":["An outright gift","A fixed trust","A discretionary trust","A resulting trust"],"e":"Precatory words usually show **no trust**: the recipient takes absolutely (Re Adams and the Kensington Vestry)."}]},{"id":"trusts/formalities","name":"Trusts Law 2: Formalities for creating and dealing with trusts","qs":[{"q":"Which provision requires a declaration of trust of land to be evidenced in signed writing?","o":["LPA 1925 s.53(1)(b)","LPA 1925 s.53(1)(c)","LPA 1925 s.53(2)","LP(MP)A 1989 s.2"],"e":"**s.53(1)(b)**: manifested and proved by signed writing; without it the trust is **unenforceable**."},{"q":"What is the effect of an unwritten disposition of a subsisting equitable interest?","o":["It is void","It is unenforceable","It is voidable","It is valid"],"e":"**s.53(1)(c)** makes an unwritten disposition **void**."},{"q":"A beneficiary under a bare trust of shares tells the trustee to transfer legal title to a third party, intending the beneficial interest to pass too. Is signed writing under s.53(1)(c) needed?","o":["No: legal and equitable title pass together","Yes: every disposition of an equitable interest needs it","Yes: unless the direction is by deed","Only if the third party gives no value"],"e":"Where the beneficiary directs a transfer of **legal title** and the equitable interest passes with it, **s.53(1)(c) does not apply** (Vandervell v IRC)."},{"q":"When must a half-secret trust be communicated to the trustee?","o":["Before or at the time of the will","Any time before death","Within six months of death","After the will is proved"],"e":"Half-secret: **before or at the will** (Blackwell; Re Keen). Fully secret: any time before death."}]},{"id":"trusts/constitution","name":"Trusts Law 3: Constitution of trusts and exceptions","qs":[{"q":"S intends to give shares to his son but uses the wrong form, so the transfer fails. Will equity treat S as holding the shares on trust for his son?","o":["No: equity will not perfect an imperfect gift","Yes: as a declaration of trust","Yes: because S clearly intended to give","Only if the son is a minor"],"e":"A failed outright gift is **not** saved by treating it as a declaration of trust (Milroy v Lord), unless an exception such as Re Rose applies."},{"q":"Under Re Rose, when is a transfer effective in equity?","o":["When the transferor has done everything in their power","When the transferee is registered","When consideration is paid","When the deed is dated"],"e":"**Re Rose**: effective in equity once the settlor has done **everything in their power**."},{"q":"What must happen to the donee for the rule in Strong v Bird to apply?","o":["They become the donor's executor","They give consideration","They take possession of the property","They are a close relative"],"e":"**Strong v Bird**: continuing intention plus the donee becoming **executor** (or administrator: Re James)."},{"q":"Dying, D hands V the box holding the title deeds to his unregistered house, saying 'the house is yours'. Can this be a valid donatio mortis causa?","o":["Yes: land can pass by DMC","No: land can never pass by DMC","No: land can only pass by deed","Only if D recovers"],"e":"A **DMC** can apply to land, by parting with **dominion** (e.g. the title deeds) in contemplation of death (Sen v Headley)."}]},{"id":"trusts/beneficial-entitlement","name":"Trusts Law 4: Beneficial entitlement and Saunders v Vautier","qs":[{"q":"Property is held on trust absolutely for A, aged 30 and of full capacity, but A is not to receive it until 40. Can A call for the property now?","o":["Yes: he is an adult absolutely entitled","No: the settlor's wishes must be respected","No: only the court can end the trust","Only with the trustees' consent"],"e":"Adult, capable beneficiaries together **absolutely entitled** can end the trust and call for the property (Saunders v Vautier)."},{"q":"Which Act lets the court approve a variation of trust on behalf of minors?","o":["Variation of Trusts Act 1958","Trustee Act 1925","TLATA 1996","Trustee Act 2000"],"e":"The **Variation of Trusts Act 1958** allows court approval where it is for the minor's **benefit**."},{"q":"What kind of interest is created by 'to A if she reaches 25'?","o":["Contingent","Vested in possession","Vested in interest","Discretionary"],"e":"The gift depends on a condition, so it is **contingent**; A cannot use Saunders v Vautier alone."},{"q":"What does a discretionary beneficiary have before any appointment?","o":["A hope and a right to be considered","A fixed proprietary share","A vested interest in income","A right to call for the capital"],"e":"Only a **hope** of benefit, plus a right to be **considered** and to proper administration."}]},{"id":"trusts/purpose-charitable","name":"Trusts Law 5: Non-charitable purpose trusts and charitable trusts","qs":[{"q":"Land is held on trust for a perpetuity period as a sports ground 'primarily for the employees of X Ltd'. Is this valid though non-charitable?","o":["Yes: it directly benefits ascertainable people","No: non-charitable purpose trusts always fail","No: nobody can enforce it","Only if X Ltd is a charity"],"e":"A purpose trust that **directly and tangibly benefits ascertainable individuals** is valid as a trust for people (Re Denley)."},{"q":"What is the perpetuity limit for a non-charitable purpose trust?","o":["21 years","125 years","80 years","No limit"],"e":"Anomalous purpose trusts are limited to **21 years** (or a life in being)."},{"q":"A trust is set up to educate the children of employees of a large company, a class of over 100,000. Is it charitable?","o":["No: a personal nexus class lacks public benefit","Yes: education is a charitable purpose","Yes: because the class is very large","Only if fees are charged"],"e":"A class defined by a **personal nexus** (e.g. a common employer) is not a section of the public (Oppenheim v Tobacco Securities)."},{"q":"Which section of the Charities Act 2011 lists the heads of charity?","o":["s.3","s.4","s.1","s.11"],"e":"**s.3** lists charitable purposes; **s.4** deals with public benefit."}]},{"id":"trusts/resulting-trusts","name":"Trusts Law 6: Resulting trusts: automatic and presumed","qs":[{"q":"L lends money to B Ltd solely so it can pay a dividend; B Ltd goes insolvent before paying it. What is L's position?","o":["The money is held on trust for L","L is an unsecured creditor","The money goes to B Ltd's creditors","L can only claim damages"],"e":"Money lent for a **specific purpose** that fails is held on a **Quistclose trust** for the lender, outside the insolvency."},{"q":"Which provision would abolish the presumption of advancement but is not in force?","o":["Equality Act 2010 s.199","LPA 1925 s.60(3)","TLATA 1996 s.19","Trustee Act 2000 s.1"],"e":"**Equality Act 2010 s.199** has never been brought into force, so the presumption survives."},{"q":"What type of resulting trust arises when an express trust fails?","o":["Automatic","Presumed","Common intention","Quasi-charitable"],"e":"Failure of an express trust gives an **automatic** resulting trust."},{"q":"A pays B money to bet on shares using inside information, but the bet is never placed. How does the court decide whether A can recover despite the illegality?","o":["A policy-based, range-of-factors approach","Whether A must rely on the illegality","Illegality always bars recovery","Whether A withdrew in time"],"e":"Courts weigh the **purpose** of the rule broken, other relevant policies and **proportionality**; the old **reliance** test (Tinsley v Milligan) is gone (Patel v Mirza)."}]},{"id":"trusts/family-home","name":"Trusts Law 7: The family home: constructive trusts and proprietary estoppel","qs":[{"q":"What is the starting point for beneficial shares in a joint names home?","o":["Equal shares","Shares in proportion to contributions","Shares set by the court's view of fairness","Sole beneficial ownership by the higher earner"],"e":"**Stack v Dowden**: equity follows the law, so **equal** beneficial joint tenancy unless shown otherwise."},{"q":"An unmarried couple bought a home in joint names. Their intention as to shares changed, but the new shares cannot be inferred from their conduct. What may the court do?","o":["Impute the shares it considers fair","Divide it equally in every case","Divide it by financial contributions only","Order a sale and split by need"],"e":"If shares cannot be inferred, the court may **impute** an intention that is **fair** in view of the whole course of dealing (Jones v Kernott)."},{"q":"The house is in H's sole name. W claims a share under a common intention constructive trust. What must she show?","o":["Common intention to share plus detrimental reliance","A written declaration of trust","That she lived there for two years","That she is married to H"],"e":"Express or inferred **common intention** to share, plus **detrimental reliance** on it (Lloyds Bank v Rosset)."},{"q":"A son works on his parents' farm for years on their promise he will inherit it; they then disinherit him. Where does the court usually start in remedying the estoppel?","o":["Fulfilling his expectation","Compensating his detriment only","Dividing the farm equally among the children","Refusing relief as there is no contract"],"e":"Start with the **expectation**, then adjust where it would be out of all proportion, e.g. for accelerated receipt (Guest v Guest [2022] UKSC 27)."}]},{"id":"trusts/fiduciary-duties","name":"Trusts Law 8: Fiduciary duties: no profit, no conflict, self-dealing","qs":[{"q":"A solicitor to a trust uses information gained in that role to buy shares, acting honestly and benefiting the trust too. Must he account for his own profit?","o":["Yes, but with an allowance for his work","No: he acted honestly","No: the trust also benefited","Only if the trust suffered a loss"],"e":"A fiduciary must not profit from his position; honesty and benefit to the trust are no defence, though an **equitable allowance** may be given (Boardman v Phipps)."},{"q":"An agent takes a secret commission from the other side of a deal and invests it profitably. What claim does the principal have?","o":["Proprietary: it is held on constructive trust","Only a personal claim for the amount","Only damages for its loss","No claim if the deal was fair"],"e":"Bribes and secret commissions are held on **constructive trust** for the principal, who can claim the asset and its profits (FHR v Cedar Capital)."},{"q":"What is the status of a trustee's purchase of trust property?","o":["Voidable by any beneficiary","Valid if the price is fair","Void from the start","Valid if the trustee discloses it"],"e":"**Self-dealing**: voidable at the instance of any beneficiary, however fair the price."},{"q":"What is the hallmark of a fiduciary relationship?","o":["Undertaking to act for another in trust and confidence","Any contract for services","Owing a duty of care in negligence","Being paid to give advice"],"e":"A fiduciary undertakes to act for another in circumstances of **trust and confidence**; the core duty is **loyalty** (Bristol and West BS v Mothew)."}]},{"id":"trusts/trustees-appointment","name":"Trusts Law 9: Trustees: who can act, appointment, retirement and removal","qs":[{"q":"What is the maximum number of trustees of land?","o":["Four","Two","Three","Six"],"e":"**TA 1925 s.34**: a maximum of **four** trustees of land."},{"q":"How long must a trustee remain outside the UK for s.36 replacement to apply?","o":["More than 12 months","More than 6 months","More than 2 years","More than 3 months"],"e":"**s.36 TA 1925**: remaining outside the UK for more than **12 months**."},{"q":"Which provision lets adult beneficiaries absolutely entitled direct a trustee to retire?","o":["TLATA 1996 s.19","TA 1925 s.39","TA 1925 s.41","TA 2000 s.29"],"e":"**TLATA s.19**: written directions to retire or appoint, unless excluded."},{"q":"Under s.39 TA 1925, who must remain for a trustee to retire without replacement?","o":["Two individuals or a trust corporation","One individual trustee","Three individual trustees","Any continuing trustee"],"e":"**s.39** needs at least **two individual trustees** or a **trust corporation** to remain."}]},{"id":"trusts/trustee-duties-powers","name":"Trusts Law 10: Trustees' duty of care, investment, maintenance and advancement","qs":[{"q":"Which section of the Trustee Act 2000 sets the statutory duty of care?","o":["s.1","s.3","s.4","s.5"],"e":"**TA 2000 s.1**: reasonable care and skill, higher for professionals."},{"q":"Which section of the Trustee Act 1925 governs maintenance from income for minors?","o":["s.31","s.32","s.36","s.61"],"e":"**s.31** is maintenance (income); **s.32** is advancement (capital)."},{"q":"For trusts created after 1 October 2014, how much capital can be advanced under s.32?","o":["The whole presumptive share","Half the presumptive share","One third of the share","A quarter of the share"],"e":"Post-2014 trusts: up to the **whole**; earlier trusts: up to **half**."},{"q":"At what age does a contingent beneficiary become entitled to income under s.31?","o":["18","21","25","When the contingency is met"],"e":"**s.31(1)(ii)**: income at **18**, but capital waits for the contingency."}]},{"id":"trusts/breach-of-trust","name":"Trusts Law 11: Liability for breach of trust and defences","qs":[{"q":"A trust deed excludes trustees' liability for loss 'unless caused by actual fraud'. A trustee causes loss by gross negligence. Is the trustee protected?","o":["Yes: gross negligence can be excluded","No: gross negligence cannot be excluded","No: such clauses are always void","Only if the trustee is unpaid"],"e":"An exemption clause can cover even **gross negligence**, but not **actual fraud or dishonesty** (Armitage v Nurse)."},{"q":"What is the general limitation period for breach of trust?","o":["6 years","3 years","12 years","No limit"],"e":"**s.21(3) Limitation Act 1980**: **6 years**, but none for fraud or recovery of trust property held."},{"q":"Which section of the Trustee Act 1925 lets the court excuse an honest and reasonable trustee?","o":["s.61","s.62","s.31","s.27"],"e":"**s.61**: relief where the trustee acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused."},{"q":"Which Act governs contribution between trustees jointly liable for a breach?","o":["Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978","Limitation Act 1980","Trustee Act 2000","Law Reform Act 1945"],"e":"The **Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978**: just and equitable contribution."}]},{"id":"trusts/strangers","name":"Trusts Law 12: Liability of strangers: knowing receipt and dishonest assistance","qs":[{"q":"X receives trust property transferred in breach of trust. What is the test for X's liability in knowing receipt?","o":["Was keeping it unconscionable given X's knowledge?","Was X dishonest by ordinary standards?","Did X give no value for it?","Did X know the precise breach?"],"e":"Knowing receipt: the recipient's knowledge made it **unconscionable** to keep the benefit; dishonesty is not needed (BCCI v Akindele)."},{"q":"X helps a trustee move trust money abroad, believing this is fine, though ordinary decent people would see it as dishonest. Is X dishonest for dishonest assistance?","o":["Yes: the standard is objective","No: X believed it was honest","No: X received nothing","Only if X knew it was illegal"],"e":"Find what X actually knew or believed, then apply the **objective standards of ordinary decent people** (Ivey v Genting)."},{"q":"Must the trustee be dishonest for a third party to be liable for dishonest assistance?","o":["No, only the assistant must be dishonest","Yes, both must be dishonest","Only if the trustee benefited","Only for breaches of fiduciary duty"],"e":"**Royal Brunei v Tan**: the trustee need **not** be dishonest; the assistant must be."}]},{"id":"trusts/remedies-tracing","name":"Trusts Law 13: Equitable remedies and tracing","qs":[{"q":"Under Re Hallett, whose money is a trustee presumed to spend first from a mixed account?","o":["The trustee's own","The trust's money","Both proportionately","Whichever was paid in first"],"e":"**Re Hallett**: the trustee is presumed to spend their **own** money first."},{"q":"A trustee pays £5,000 of trust money into an empty account. It falls to £1,000, then he adds £3,000 of his own. How much can the beneficiaries trace into?","o":["£1,000","£5,000","£4,000","£3,000"],"e":"The claim cannot exceed the **lowest intermediate balance**; later deposits of the trustee's own money are not presumed to replace it (Roscoe v Winder)."},{"q":"Many investors' money is mixed in one fund that is now insufficient, and first-in, first-out would be unjust. How will the shortfall usually be shared?","o":["Pari passu, in proportion to contributions","First in, first out","Last in, first out","Equally, ignoring contributions"],"e":"First-in, first-out (Clayton's Case) is a rule of convenience that can be displaced; courts prefer **pari passu** sharing (Barlow Clowes v Vaughan)."},{"q":"An innocent recipient of stolen money spends it in good faith on a holiday she would not otherwise have taken. What defence may she raise?","o":["Change of position","Bona fide purchase","Laches","Contributory negligence"],"e":"An innocent recipient who has **changed position in good faith** has a defence so far as repayment would be inequitable (Lipkin Gorman v Karpnale)."}]}]},{"id":"wills","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates","topics":[{"id":"wills/validity","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 1: Validity of wills: capacity, knowledge and approval, undue influence","qs":[{"q":"T has mild dementia but understands she is making a will, knows roughly what she owns and who might expect to benefit, and has no delusion affecting the gifts. Does she have testamentary capacity?","o":["Yes: the common law test is met","No: any dementia removes capacity","No: she must recall every asset exactly","Only if a doctor witnesses the will"],"e":"Test: understands the **nature of the act**, the **extent of the property**, the **claims** of others, and no **disorder of mind** affecting the gifts (Banks v Goodfellow)."},{"q":"T gives clear will instructions while capable, but at signing can only grasp that she is signing a will made on those instructions. Is the will valid?","o":["Yes: that is enough at execution","No: full capacity is needed at execution","No: the will must be re-made","Only if a doctor was present"],"e":"Valid if T had capacity when giving instructions and, at execution, understood she was signing a will **made on those instructions** (Parker v Felgate)."},{"q":"Who must prove undue influence in a will challenge?","o":["The person alleging it","The person propounding the will","The executor in every case","The probate registrar"],"e":"There is **no presumption** of undue influence for wills: the **challenger** must prove coercion."},{"q":"What is the minimum age to make a normal will?","o":["18","16","17","21"],"e":"**s.7 Wills Act 1837**: 18, except **privileged wills** of soldiers on active service and sailors at sea."}]},{"id":"wills/formalities","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 2: Formalities: Wills Act 1837 s.9, witnesses and s.15","qs":[{"q":"Which section of the Wills Act 1837 sets the formalities for a valid will?","o":["s.9","s.15","s.18","s.33"],"e":"**s.9**: writing, signature, intention, and two witnesses present at the same time."},{"q":"What is the effect of a beneficiary witnessing the will?","o":["The gift to them is void","The whole will is void","The gift is halved","The witness's signature is ignored"],"e":"**s.15**: the **gift** is void, but the **will** remains valid."},{"q":"Which Act saves a witness-beneficiary's gift where two other independent witnesses also signed?","o":["Wills Act 1968","Administration of Justice Act 1982","Trustee Act 2000","Law Reform (Succession) Act 1995"],"e":"**Wills Act 1968**: the beneficiary's attestation is disregarded if two other witnesses signed."},{"q":"Video-witnessing of wills was permitted for wills made up to which date?","o":["31 January 2024","31 January 2022","31 December 2025","It is still permitted"],"e":"Video-witnessing applied to wills made **31 January 2020 to 31 January 2024**; physical presence is now required."},{"q":"What does an attestation clause raise?","o":["A presumption of due execution","A presumption of capacity only","A presumption of undue influence","A requirement for a third witness"],"e":"An attestation clause is not required but raises a **presumption of due execution**; without one an affidavit (NCPR r.12) may be needed."}]},{"id":"wills/executors","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 3: Executors: appointment, eligibility, renunciation and power reserved","qs":[{"q":"What is the maximum number of executors who can take a grant for the same property?","o":["Four","Two","Three","Six"],"e":"**s.114 Senior Courts Act 1981**: no more than **four**; others may have power reserved."},{"q":"When can an executor no longer renounce?","o":["After intermeddling in the estate","After the funeral","After the death certificate issues","After six months from death"],"e":"Once an executor has **intermeddled** (e.g. collecting assets), they have accepted office."},{"q":"Which section of the AEA 1925 creates the chain of executorship?","o":["s.7","s.25","s.33","s.36"],"e":"**AEA 1925 s.7**: the executor of a sole proving executor becomes executor of the original testator."},{"q":"From when does an executor's authority derive?","o":["The will, from death","The grant of probate","The statement of truth","The IHT400 filing"],"e":"An executor's authority comes from the **will** at death; the grant only **confirms** it. An administrator's comes from the grant."}]},{"id":"wills/alterations-revocation","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 4: Alterations, codicils and revocation","qs":[{"q":"Under s.21 Wills Act, when are unattested alterations presumed to have been made?","o":["After execution","Before execution","At execution","At the date of death"],"e":"**s.21** presumes alterations were made **after** execution, so they are invalid unless executed like a will."},{"q":"T draws a line through her will, writes 'revoked' across it and throws it away; it is recovered intact. Is it revoked?","o":["No: there was no burning, tearing or destroying","Yes: her intention to revoke is clear","Yes: writing 'revoked' is enough","Only if she also signed the word"],"e":"Revocation by destruction (**s.20 Wills Act 1837**) needs an actual act of **burning, tearing or destroying** plus intention (Cheese v Lovejoy)."},{"q":"What is the effect of marriage on an earlier will?","o":["It revokes the will","It has no effect","It revokes gifts to the spouse","It revokes executor appointments only"],"e":"**s.18**: marriage revokes the will, unless made in contemplation of marriage to a particular person."},{"q":"On divorce, how is a gift to the former spouse treated?","o":["As if they died on the divorce date","As revoking the whole will","As valid and unaffected","As passing to the spouse's estate"],"e":"**s.18A**: the ex-spouse is treated as having **died** on the date of the final order."}]},{"id":"wills/interpretation","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 5: Interpretation: types of gift and failure of gifts","qs":[{"q":"What is a gift of £5,000 from my Barclays account?","o":["A demonstrative gift","A specific gift","A residuary gift","A general gift of chattels"],"e":"A **demonstrative** gift is paid from a specified fund; any shortfall is paid as a general legacy."},{"q":"Which section of the Wills Act saves a gift to a predeceased child who leaves issue?","o":["s.33","s.24","s.15","s.18A"],"e":"**s.33**: the gift passes to the child's **issue per stirpes**, unless a contrary intention appears."},{"q":"Which type of gift is affected by ademption?","o":["Specific gifts","Pecuniary gifts","General gifts","Residuary gifts"],"e":"**Ademption** only affects **specific** gifts not in the estate at death."},{"q":"Which gifts abate first where the estate is insufficient?","o":["Residue","Specific gifts","Pecuniary legacies","Demonstrative gifts"],"e":"**Residue** abates first, then general gifts rateably, then specific gifts rateably."},{"q":"Where the order of deaths is uncertain, who is presumed to have survived?","o":["The younger","The elder","Neither","Whoever is named first in the will"],"e":"**s.184 LPA 1925** (commorientes): the **younger** is presumed to survive the elder."}]},{"id":"wills/intestacy","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 6: Intestacy: entitlement and partial intestacy","qs":[{"q":"How long must a spouse survive the intestate to inherit?","o":["28 days","7 days","30 days","3 months"],"e":"A spouse must survive the intestate by **28 days**."},{"q":"What is the statutory legacy for deaths on or after 26 July 2023?","o":["£322,000","£270,000","£250,000","£325,000"],"e":"The statutory legacy is **£322,000** (it was £270,000 before)."},{"q":"Where an intestate leaves a spouse but no issue, who takes the estate?","o":["The spouse takes everything","Spouse and parents equally","Spouse half, siblings half","Spouse statutory legacy, parents the rest"],"e":"A spouse with **no issue** takes **everything**; parents and siblings take nothing."},{"q":"Within what period can a spouse require appropriation of the matrimonial home?","o":["12 months of the grant","6 months of the grant","12 months of death","2 years of death"],"e":"**Intestates' Estates Act 1952 Sch 2**: within **12 months of the grant**."},{"q":"With no spouse or issue, who are next entitled on intestacy?","o":["Parents","Whole-blood siblings","Grandparents","The Crown"],"e":"After issue, **parents** take equally and absolutely."}]},{"id":"wills/outside-estate","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 7: Property passing outside the estate","qs":[{"q":"How does a deceased joint tenant's interest in land pass?","o":["By survivorship to the other joint tenant","Under the will","Under the intestacy rules","To the PRs on trust"],"e":"**Survivorship** applies; a will cannot override it unless the joint tenancy was severed in life."},{"q":"Which Act's s.11 policies create an automatic trust for a spouse or children?","o":["Married Women's Property Act 1882","Trustee Act 1925","Administration of Estates Act 1925","Inheritance Act 1975"],"e":"**MWPA 1882 s.11** policies create a trust, so proceeds pass outside the estate."},{"q":"From what date will unused pension funds be included in the estate for IHT?","o":["6 April 2027","6 April 2026","1 January 2027","6 April 2028"],"e":"Finance Act 2026 brings most pension death benefits into IHT from **6 April 2027**, not yet in force for the exam."},{"q":"Up to what amount can some bodies pay out without a grant under the 1965 Act?","o":["£5,000","£10,000","£15,000","£2,500"],"e":"The **Administration of Estates (Small Payments) Act 1965** allows payment up to **£5,000**."}]},{"id":"wills/grants","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 8: Grants of representation: need, types, priority and application","qs":[{"q":"Which grant is made where there is a valid will but no executor able and willing to prove?","o":["Letters of administration with will annexed","Grant of probate","Simple letters of administration","Grant de bonis non"],"e":"**Probate** goes only to executors; otherwise it is administration **with will annexed**."},{"q":"Which Non-Contentious Probate Rule sets priority on intestacy?","o":["NCPR r.22","NCPR r.20","NCPR r.12","NCPR r.27"],"e":"**r.22** covers intestacy; **r.20** covers a will with no proving executor."},{"q":"How many administrators are needed where a minority or life interest arises?","o":["At least two","At least three","One is always enough","Exactly four"],"e":"**At least two** administrators (or a trust corporation); a sole executor is fine."},{"q":"After submitting IHT400, how long must the applicant wait before applying for the grant?","o":["20 working days","10 working days","28 days","6 months"],"e":"For non-excepted estates, wait **20 working days** after IHT400 before applying."}]},{"id":"wills/iht-funding-incidence","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 9: Funding the IHT, liability and incidence","qs":[{"q":"When is IHT on death due?","o":["6 months after the end of the month of death","12 months after death","6 months after death","On application for the grant"],"e":"Due **6 months after the end of the month of death**; interest runs after that."},{"q":"In how many yearly instalments can IHT on instalment property be paid?","o":["10","5","7","12"],"e":"Tax on instalment property can be paid in **10 equal yearly instalments**."},{"q":"By default, who bears IHT on UK free estate?","o":["The residuary beneficiaries","The specific legatees","The PRs personally","Each beneficiary rateably"],"e":"**IHTA s.211**: IHT on UK free estate is a testamentary expense paid from **residue**."},{"q":"Who is primarily liable for IHT on a failed PET?","o":["The donee","The PRs","The donor's spouse","The residuary beneficiaries"],"e":"The **donee** is primarily liable; PRs only if unpaid 12 months after the end of the month of death."}]},{"id":"wills/administration","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 10: Administration: PR duties, debts, protection and distribution","qs":[{"q":"How long is the executor's year before PRs can be compelled to distribute?","o":["One year from death","One year from the grant","Six months from death","Six months from the grant"],"e":"**AEA s.44**: PRs cannot be compelled to distribute before **one year from death**."},{"q":"How long must claimants be given to respond to s.27 Trustee Act 1925 notices?","o":["At least 2 months","At least 1 month","At least 6 months","At least 28 days"],"e":"**s.27** notices give claimants **at least 2 months**, protecting PRs against unknown claims."},{"q":"Which court order protects PRs distributing on the basis that a missing beneficiary is dead?","o":["A Benjamin order","A Beddoe order","A s.27 order","A Hastings-Bass order"],"e":"A **Benjamin order** authorises distribution on a stated basis, e.g. that a beneficiary is dead."},{"q":"In an insolvent estate, what ranks immediately after secured creditors?","o":["Funeral, testamentary and administration expenses","Preferential debts","Secondary preferential HMRC debts","Ordinary unsecured debts"],"e":"Under the 1986 Order, **funeral and administration expenses** rank ahead of preferential debts."}]},{"id":"wills/family-provision","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 11: Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975","qs":[{"q":"Within what period must an Inheritance Act 1975 claim be made?","o":["6 months from the grant","6 months from death","12 months from the grant","2 years from death"],"e":"**s.4**: **6 months from the grant**, though the court can extend."},{"q":"How long must a cohabitant have lived with the deceased before death to claim?","o":["2 years immediately before death","5 years at any time","1 year immediately before death","3 years immediately before death"],"e":"**s.1(1)(ba)**: the whole of the **2 years immediately before death**."},{"q":"Which applicant gets the higher standard of provision, not limited to maintenance?","o":["A surviving spouse or civil partner","A cohabitant","An adult child","A dependant being maintained"],"e":"The **surviving spouse** standard applies; all others get the **maintenance** standard."},{"q":"An adult daughter, estranged for years and living on benefits, claims against her mother's estate left to charities. On what standard is any award made?","o":["Maintenance only","Whatever is reasonable in all circumstances","An equal share with the charities","Her full intestacy entitlement"],"e":"Adult children get provision for **maintenance** only; estrangement and the testator's wishes carry weight (Ilott v The Blue Cross)."}]},{"id":"wills/iht-lifetime","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 12: Inheritance tax: lifetime gifts, exemptions and anti-avoidance","qs":[{"q":"How long must a donor survive for a PET to become exempt?","o":["7 years","3 years","5 years","14 years"],"e":"A PET becomes **exempt** if the donor survives **7 years**."},{"q":"What is the annual exemption for IHT?","o":["£3,000","£250","£5,000","£6,000"],"e":"**s.19**: **£3,000** per tax year, carried forward one year only."},{"q":"What is the marriage gift exemption for a parent?","o":["£5,000","£2,500","£1,000","£3,000"],"e":"**s.22**: £5,000 by a parent, £2,500 by a grandparent or party, £1,000 by others."},{"q":"What does taper relief reduce?","o":["The tax on the gift","The value of the gift","The nil rate band","The death estate"],"e":"**Taper relief** reduces the **tax**, for gifts made 3 to 7 years before death."},{"q":"At what rate is lifetime IHT charged on a chargeable transfer?","o":["20%","40%","36%","25%"],"e":"Lifetime chargeable transfers are taxed at **20%** above the available NRB (40% on death)."}]},{"id":"wills/iht-death-reliefs","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 13: Inheritance tax on death: estate, reliefs, nil rate bands and calculation","qs":[{"q":"What is the residence nil rate band for 2026/27?","o":["£175,000","£325,000","£150,000","£250,000"],"e":"The **RNRB** is **£175,000**, for a home closely inherited by direct descendants."},{"q":"What reduced death rate applies if 10% or more of the baseline amount goes to charity?","o":["36%","20%","30%","38%"],"e":"Leaving **10%** of the baseline amount to charity gives a **36%** rate."},{"q":"From 6 April 2026, to what value of combined APR/BPR property does 100% relief apply?","o":["First £2.5 million","First £1 million","First £3 million","First £5 million"],"e":"Finance Act 2026: **£2.5 million** allowance; property above it gets 50% relief."},{"q":"What minimum ownership period applies for business property relief?","o":["2 years","7 years","3 years","5 years"],"e":"Relevant business property must be owned for at least **2 years**."},{"q":"Above what estate value is the RNRB tapered?","o":["£2 million","£1 million","£2.5 million","£3 million"],"e":"The RNRB reduces by **£1 for every £2** over **£2 million**."}]},{"id":"wills/income-cgt-administration","name":"Wills and the Administration of Estates 14: Income tax and CGT during administration","qs":[{"q":"What is the PRs' base cost for CGT on assets of the estate?","o":["Probate value at death","The deceased's original cost","Value at the date of the grant","Value at the date of sale"],"e":"PRs acquire assets at **probate value**: the **death uplift** wipes out the deceased's gains."},{"q":"For how many tax years do PRs get the annual exempt amount?","o":["Year of death and next 2","Year of death only","Year of death and next 1","Every year until administration ends"],"e":"PRs get the annual exempt amount for the **tax year of death and the next 2**."},{"q":"Which form do PRs give beneficiaries showing income and tax paid?","o":["R185","IHT400","IHT423","PA1P"],"e":"Form **R185** shows the gross income and tax already paid by the PRs."},{"q":"Within what period must a deed of variation be made to be read back for IHT?","o":["2 years of death","1 year of death","6 months of the grant","2 years of the grant"],"e":"**IHTA s.142**: within **2 years of death**, with a statement in the deed."}]}]}];